window.DD_TESTS = [{"exam":"sat","set":1,"title":{"en":"SAT Reading and Writing — Full practice test 1","th":"SAT Reading and Writing — ข้อสอบเสมือนจริงชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"Full test: 2 modules × 27 questions × 32 minutes (Module 2 is the harder module). DeepDistrict practice — not an official College Board test.","th":"ข้อสอบเต็ม 2 โมดูล โมดูลละ 27 ข้อ 32 นาที (โมดูล 2 เป็นชุดยาก) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการของ College Board"},"minutes":64,"kinds":{"sat":54},"subjects":{},"free":true,"file":"sat-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Ecology","th":"นิเวศวิทยา"},"passage":"Ecologist Ruth Mbeki expected the drained wetland she was monitoring to recover slowly, if at all. Within three growing seasons, however, native sedges had returned in such numbers that the site's plant community was nearly ______ that of nearby undisturbed marshes: even experienced field surveyors could not reliably tell the two apart.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["derived from","indistinguishable from","remote from","absent from"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The colon explains the blank: surveyors 'could not reliably tell the two apart,' so the restored community was nearly 'indistinguishable from' the undisturbed marshes. 'Remote from' and 'absent from' suggest difference or distance, the opposite of the point, and 'derived from' (originating in) says nothing about the two looking alike.","th":"ข้อความหลังเครื่องหมาย : อธิบายช่องว่างว่า นักสำรวจ 'แยกสองแห่งออกจากกันไม่ได้' จึงต้องเป็น indistinguishable from (แทบแยกไม่ออกจาก) ส่วน remote from (ห่างไกลจาก) และ absent from (ไม่มีอยู่ใน) สื่อถึงความแตกต่างซึ่งตรงข้ามกับใจความ และ derived from (มีที่มาจาก) ไม่ได้บอกว่าทั้งสองแห่งเหมือนกัน"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Genetics and behavior","th":"พันธุศาสตร์และพฤติกรรม"},"passage":"Early studies linking a single gene variant to a tendency toward risk-taking attracted wide public attention, but the association ultimately proved ______: follow-up studies drawing on tens of thousands of participants found that the variant's effect, if it existed at all, was far too small to detect reliably.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["tenuous","conspicuous","intractable","venerable"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The larger studies found an effect too small to detect, so the link turned out to be weak and poorly supported: 'tenuous.' 'Conspicuous' (obvious) is the opposite, 'intractable' means hard to solve or manage, and 'venerable' means respected because of age; none describes a link that fell apart under testing.","th":"งานวิจัยขนาดใหญ่พบว่าผลน้อยเกินกว่าจะตรวจพบ ความเชื่อมโยงจึง tenuous (อ่อน ไม่มั่นคง) ส่วน conspicuous (เด่นชัด) มีความหมายตรงข้าม intractable (แก้ยาก ควบคุมยาก) และ venerable (น่าเคารพเพราะเก่าแก่) ไม่ได้อธิบายความเชื่อมโยงที่พังลงเมื่อถูกทดสอบ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-quantitative","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Agricultural science","th":"วิทยาศาสตร์การเกษตร"},"passage":"Alternate wetting and drying (AWD) is a rice-growing method in which fields are allowed to dry out periodically instead of being kept continuously flooded (CF). Agronomists compared the two methods at three sites. They report that AWD reduced water use at every site but that its effect on yield was not uniform: ______","stem":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["AWD used less water at Site A (860 mm) than at Site C (900 mm).","the highest yield under continuous flooding was recorded at Site C (6.0 tonnes per hectare).","yield under AWD was higher than yield under continuous flooding at Site A.","yield fell by 0.6 tonnes per hectare under AWD at Site C but changed by only 0.1 tonnes per hectare at Sites A and B."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The statement needs data showing that AWD's effect on yield differed among sites. At Site C yield dropped from 6.0 to 5.4 (0.6 t/ha), while at Site A it changed from 5.2 to 5.1 and at Site B from 4.8 to 4.9 (0.1 t/ha each). The water comparison between sites and the highest CF yield are accurate but say nothing about how AWD changed yield, and at Site A AWD yield (5.1) was lower, not higher, than CF yield (5.2).","th":"ข้อความต้องการข้อมูลที่แสดงว่าผลของ AWD ต่อผลผลิตไม่เท่ากันในแต่ละแหล่ง ที่ Site C ผลผลิตลดจาก 6.0 เหลือ 5.4 (ลด 0.6 ตัน/เฮกตาร์) ขณะที่ Site A เปลี่ยนจาก 5.2 เป็น 5.1 และ Site B จาก 4.8 เป็น 4.9 (เปลี่ยนเพียง 0.1) การเทียบปริมาณน้ำระหว่างแหล่งและผลผลิต CF สูงสุดเป็นข้อมูลจริงแต่ไม่ได้บอกผลของ AWD ต่อผลผลิต และที่ Site A ผลผลิต AWD (5.1) ต่ำกว่า CF (5.2) ไม่ใช่สูงกว่า"},"table":{"title":"Rice yield and water use under two irrigation methods","rows":[["Site","Method","Yield (tonnes per hectare)","Water used (mm)"],["Site A","CF","5.2","1,200"],["Site A","AWD","5.1","860"],["Site B","CF","4.8","1,150"],["Site B","AWD","4.9","830"],["Site C","CF","6.0","1,300"],["Site C","AWD","5.4","900"]]}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Contemporary art","th":"ศิลปะร่วมสมัย"},"passage":"Thai artist Montien Boonma's installation House of Hope—a room hung with thousands of strands of beads made from fragrant medicinal ______ designed to surround visitors with a soothing, healing scent.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["herbs, was","herbs—was","herbs; was","herbs was"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The description 'a room hung with… medicinal herbs' is a supplementary element that begins with a dash, so it must close with a matching dash before the verb 'was.' A comma would mismatch the opening dash, a semicolon separates the subject from its verb, and no punctuation leaves the element unclosed.","th":"ส่วนขยาย a room hung with… medicinal herbs เปิดด้วยขีดยาว (—) จึงต้องปิดด้วยขีดยาวเช่นกันก่อนกริยา was การใช้จุลภาคไม่เข้าคู่กับขีดเปิด อัฒภาคแยกประธานออกจากกริยา และไม่ใส่เครื่องหมายทำให้ส่วนขยายไม่ถูกปิด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Geology","th":"ธรณีวิทยา"},"passage":"Iceland sits directly on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, a boundary where two tectonic plates are slowly pulling apart and molten rock rises to fill the gap. ______ the island experiences frequent volcanic eruptions and is dotted with hot springs.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["In contrast,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","As a result,"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Iceland's position on a spreading plate boundary where molten rock rises causes its frequent eruptions and hot springs, so a cause-and-effect transition, 'As a result,' fits. 'In contrast' and 'Nevertheless' signal opposition, and 'For instance' introduces an example of a general claim, which the second sentence is not.","th":"ตำแหน่งของไอซ์แลนด์บนแนวแยกของแผ่นเปลือกโลกที่มีหินหลอมเหลวดันขึ้นมาเป็นสาเหตุของภูเขาไฟและน้ำพุร้อน จึงใช้คำเชื่อมเหตุและผล As a result ส่วน In contrast และ Nevertheless แสดงความขัดแย้ง และ For instance ใช้ยกตัวอย่าง ซึ่งไม่ตรงกับความสัมพันธ์นี้"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"International relations","th":"ความสัมพันธ์ระหว่างประเทศ"},"passage":"The language of the 1998 river-sharing treaty was deliberately ______. By never defining what counted as a \"fair share\" of the water, the negotiators allowed each government to present the agreement to its own citizens as a victory, while postponing the hardest questions to future talks.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["equivocal","redundant","candid","punitive"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The negotiators avoided defining key terms so that each side could read the treaty its own way; the language was open to more than one interpretation: 'equivocal.' 'Candid' (frank) is nearly the opposite, 'redundant' means needlessly repetitive, and 'punitive' means intended to punish; none explains why each side could claim victory.","th":"ผู้เจรจาจงใจไม่นิยามคำสำคัญเพื่อให้แต่ละฝ่ายตีความสนธิสัญญาตามแบบของตน ภาษาจึง equivocal (กำกวม ตีความได้หลายทาง) ส่วน candid (ตรงไปตรงมา) เกือบตรงข้าม redundant (ซ้ำซ้อน) และ punitive (มุ่งลงโทษ) ไม่ได้อธิบายว่าทำไมทุกฝ่ายจึงอ้างชัยชนะได้"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Literature: fiction","th":"วรรณกรรม: บันเทิงคดี"},"passage":"The following text is from a 1911 novel. Agnes has returned to the village where she grew up after an absence of twenty years.\nThe lane was narrower than she remembered, and the great elm that had once seemed to hold up the sky was only a tree, handsome but ordinary. She waited for the old feeling of homecoming as one waits for a train that is already late. It did not come. Instead she found herself noting, with a surveyor's detachment, which cottages had been re-roofed and which had not.","stem":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To catalog the physical changes the village has undergone during the character's absence.","To explain why the character left the village twenty years earlier.","To suggest that the character deeply regrets her decision to return to the village.","To depict a character's realization that a familiar place no longer stirs the emotions she expected it to."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The details (the narrow lane, the ordinary elm, the waited-for feeling that 'did not come,' her 'surveyor's detachment') all show Agnes expecting emotion and finding none. The physical details serve that purpose rather than being the purpose, as the lane and elm may not have changed at all; no reason for leaving is given; and detachment is not the same as deep regret.","th":"รายละเอียดทั้งหมด (ทางที่แคบลง ต้นเอล์มที่ธรรมดา ความรู้สึกที่รอแต่ 'ไม่มา' และความเฉยชาแบบนักสำรวจ) แสดงว่าแอกเนสคาดหวังความรู้สึกแต่ไม่พบ รายละเอียดทางกายภาพเป็นเครื่องมือ ไม่ใช่จุดประสงค์ (ทางและต้นไม้อาจไม่ได้เปลี่ยนเลย แต่เธอมองต่างไป) บทอ่านไม่ได้บอกเหตุผลที่เธอจากไป และความเฉยชาไม่ใช่ความเสียใจอย่างลึกซึ้ง"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-textual","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Literature: fiction","th":"วรรณกรรม: บันเทิงคดี"},"passage":"The Lantern Field is an 1897 novel by Margaret Ashdown. In the novel, the narrator portrays Mrs. Penrose as a woman who conceals strong feeling behind a show of indifference.","stem":"Which quotation from The Lantern Field most effectively illustrates the claim?","choices":["\"Mrs. Penrose never wept at funerals, and the village admired her greatly for her composure.\"","\"'It is nothing to me whether the boy writes or not,' said Mrs. Penrose, and she turned the letter face down on the table—but not before she had read it a third time.\"","\"Mrs. Penrose wept openly on the platform when her son's train pulled away, and she did not care who saw her.\"","\"She kept her house as she kept her accounts: orderly, exact, and without a single ornament.\""],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The claim needs both a show of indifference and hidden strong feeling. Mrs. Penrose says the letter is 'nothing' to her (indifference) yet has read it three times (concealed feeling). The funeral quotation shows composure but no hidden emotion, the platform quotation shows feeling displayed openly rather than concealed, and the housekeeping quotation shows neither.","th":"ข้ออ้างต้องมีทั้งการแสดงท่าทีไม่ใส่ใจ และความรู้สึกลึกที่ซ่อนไว้ นางเพนโรสพูดว่าจดหมาย 'ไม่สำคัญ' (ท่าทีไม่ใส่ใจ) แต่ได้อ่านไปแล้วสามรอบ (ความรู้สึกที่ซ่อนอยู่) คำพูดเรื่องงานศพแสดงความสงบแต่ไม่มีความรู้สึกที่ซ่อน คำพูดเรื่องร้องไห้ที่ชานชาลาแสดงความรู้สึกอย่างเปิดเผย และคำพูดเรื่องการดูแลบ้านไม่ตรงกับทั้งสองส่วน"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"form-structure-sense","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Astronomy","th":"ดาราศาสตร์"},"passage":"The rings of icy debris that astronomers detected around Quaoar, a dwarf planet orbiting far beyond Neptune, ______ much farther from the body than standard models predict rings can survive without clumping together into a moon.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["lie","lies","is lying","has lain"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The subject is the plural noun 'rings'; 'of icy debris,' the 'that' clause, and the appositive about Quaoar are all modifiers. A plural subject takes the plural verb 'lie.' 'Lies,' 'is lying,' and 'has lain' are singular forms that wrongly agree with a nearer noun such as 'debris' or 'Quaoar.'","th":"ประธานคือ rings (พหูพจน์) ส่วน of icy debris อนุประโยค that และส่วนขยายเกี่ยวกับ Quaoar เป็นตัวขยายทั้งหมด จึงใช้กริยาพหูพจน์ lie ส่วน lies, is lying และ has lain เป็นรูปเอกพจน์ที่ผันตามคำนามใกล้ ๆ อย่าง debris หรือ Quaoar อย่างผิด ๆ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Chemistry","th":"เคมี"},"passage":"Nearly all metals are solid at room temperature, which is why they are so useful for building tools and structures. Mercury, ______ is a liquid at room temperature and freezes only at about −39°C.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["therefore,","for example,","however,","in addition,"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Mercury being a liquid contrasts with the fact that nearly all metals are solid, so 'however' fits. 'Therefore' signals a result, 'for example' would present mercury as a typical solid metal, and 'in addition' adds a similar point.","th":"ปรอทเป็นของเหลว ซึ่งขัดกับข้อเท็จจริงที่ว่าโลหะเกือบทั้งหมดเป็นของแข็ง จึงใช้ however ส่วน therefore แสดงผลลัพธ์ for example จะทำให้ปรอทเป็นตัวอย่างของโลหะที่เป็นของแข็ง และ in addition ใช้เพิ่มข้อมูลที่คล้ายกัน"}}]}},{"exam":"sat","set":2,"title":{"en":"SAT Reading and Writing — Full practice test 2","th":"SAT Reading and Writing — ข้อสอบเสมือนจริงชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"Full test: 2 modules × 27 questions × 32 minutes (Module 2 is the harder module). DeepDistrict practice — not an official College Board test.","th":"ข้อสอบเต็ม 2 โมดูล โมดูลละ 27 ข้อ 32 นาที (โมดูล 2 เป็นชุดยาก) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการของ College Board"},"minutes":64,"kinds":{"sat":54},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"sat-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Civil engineering","th":"วิศวกรรมโยธา"},"passage":"Concrete cracks as it ages, and water seeping through the cracks corrodes the steel bars inside. Engineers are now testing concrete laced with dormant bacterial spores and calcium-rich nutrients. When water enters a crack, the spores revive and produce limestone that seals the gap. Because the bacteria act only when damage occurs, the repair is essentially ______: no inspector needs to find the crack, and no worker needs to fill it.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["ornamental","autonomous","temporary","reluctant"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The colon explains the blank: no inspector and no worker is needed, so the repair happens by itself, without outside control — 'autonomous'. 'Temporary' is not supported (nothing says the seal fails), 'ornamental' means decorative, and 'reluctant' (unwilling) contradicts bacteria that act whenever damage occurs.","th":"ข้อความหลังเครื่องหมาย : อธิบายคำที่เว้นไว้ คือไม่ต้องมีผู้ตรวจหาหรือคนงานมาซ่อม การซ่อมจึงเกิดขึ้นเองโดยไม่ต้องมีใครควบคุม = autonomous (ทำงานได้เอง) ส่วน temporary (ชั่วคราว) ไม่มีข้อมูลสนับสนุน ornamental แปลว่าเพื่อการตกแต่ง และ reluctant (ไม่เต็มใจ) ขัดกับการที่แบคทีเรียทำงานทันทีเมื่อเกิดรอยร้าว"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"History of science","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์วิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"Historians increasingly portray Charles Darwin as the hub of a vast network of correspondents: pigeon breeders, gardeners, colonial officials, and naturalists on several continents sent him observations that he drew on in On the Origin of Species. Historian Léa Morand cautions, however, against concluding that Darwin merely compiled what others told him. He did not simply ______ his correspondents' reports, she notes; he checked them against one another and against his own experiments, and he discarded many that failed these tests.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["corroborate","repudiate","embellish","defer to"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Morand's point is that Darwin was not passive: instead of just accepting what he was told, he tested the reports and threw many out. 'Defer to' (accept someone's judgment without question) is exactly what he did not 'simply' do. 'Corroborate' (confirm with evidence) already involves checking, so it cannot be contrasted with checking; 'repudiate' (reject) would make the contrast illogical; 'embellish' (add decorative detail) is unrelated to testing.","th":"ประเด็นของ Morand คือ Darwin ไม่ได้รับข้อมูลมาเฉย ๆ แต่ตรวจสอบและทิ้งรายงานจำนวนมาก สิ่งที่เขา 'ไม่ได้ทำเพียงอย่างเดียว' คือการเชื่อตามโดยไม่ตั้งคำถาม = defer to ส่วน corroborate (ยืนยันด้วยหลักฐาน) ก็คือการตรวจสอบอยู่แล้ว จึงนำมาเปรียบเทียบแบบขัดแย้งกับการตรวจสอบไม่ได้ repudiate (ปฏิเสธ) ทำให้ความหมายขัดกัน และ embellish (เสริมแต่ง) ไม่เกี่ยวกับการตรวจสอบ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-quantitative","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Ecology","th":"นิเวศวิทยา"},"passage":"Ecologist Tomasz Wierzbicki studied how far the \"edge effect\"—increased predation on bird nests near the boundary between a forest and open land—extends into a forest. He placed artificial nests at four distances from the edge of a Polish forest and recorded the percentage raided by predators within two weeks. He concluded that the edge effect is concentrated within about 100 meters of the edge, noting that ______","table":{"title":"Artificial nests raided by predators, by distance from the forest edge","rows":[["Distance from edge (m)","Nests placed","Nests raided (%)"],["0–50","120","62"],["50–100","120","47"],["100–200","120","30"],["more than 200","120","28"]]},"stem":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["nests within 50 meters of the edge were raided more often (62%) than nests at any other distance.","nests 100–200 meters from the edge were raided at nearly the same rate as nests more than 200 meters from the edge (30% versus 28%), while nests closer than 100 meters were raided far more often.","the same number of nests, 120, was placed at each of the four distances from the edge.","the percentage of nests raided fell by a similar amount with each increase in distance, from 62% to 28%."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"To show the effect is concentrated within 100 m, the data must show high predation inside 100 m and little change beyond it: 62% and 47% inside 100 m, but 30% and 28% — almost equal — beyond. The 0–50 m figure alone does not locate where the effect stops; the equal number of nests is about method; and the drops were not similar (15, 17, then only 2 points), so that statement is false.","th":"หากจะแสดงว่าผลกระทบจากขอบป่ากระจุกอยู่ภายใน 100 เมตร ต้องชี้ว่าภายใน 100 เมตรถูกล่ามาก (62% และ 47%) แต่เลย 100 เมตรไปแล้วแทบไม่เปลี่ยน (30% กับ 28%) ตัวเลข 62% อย่างเดียวไม่ได้บอกว่าผลกระทบสิ้นสุดที่ใด จำนวนรัง 120 รังเป็นเรื่องวิธีการ และการลดลงไม่ได้เท่า ๆ กัน (ลด 15, 17 แล้วเพียง 2 จุด) ข้อความนั้นจึงผิด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Architecture","th":"สถาปัตยกรรม"},"passage":"The architect Lina Bo Bardi, who was born in Rome in 1914 but did her most celebrated work after moving to Brazil ______ designed the São Paulo Museum of Art, whose main gallery is raised high above the ground on four massive concrete pillars, leaving an open public plaza underneath.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Brazil designed","Brazil—designed","Brazil; designed","Brazil, designed"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The clause 'who was born in Rome … moving to Brazil' is extra information that opens with a comma after 'Bo Bardi', so it must close with a matching comma before the main verb 'designed'. Leaving it out runs the clause into the verb, a dash does not match the opening comma, and a semicolon wrongly separates the subject from its verb.","th":"อนุประโยค who was born in Rome … moving to Brazil เป็นข้อมูลเสริมที่เปิดด้วยจุลภาคหลัง Bo Bardi จึงต้องปิดด้วยจุลภาคก่อนกริยาหลัก designed ให้เป็นคู่กัน การไม่ใส่เครื่องหมายทำให้ส่วนขยายต่อกับกริยาโดยตรง ขีดยาว (—) ไม่เข้าคู่กับจุลภาคที่เปิดไว้ และ ; แยกประธานออกจากกริยาอย่างผิดหลัก"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Evolutionary biology","th":"ชีววิทยาวิวัฒนาการ"},"passage":"Mammals that eat chili peppers grind the seeds with their molars, destroying them. Birds, by contrast, swallow the seeds whole and later excrete them intact, often far from the parent plant. Capsaicin, the compound that makes chilies taste hot, activates pain receptors in mammals but not in birds. ______ capsaicin may have evolved to deter seed-destroying mammals while leaving seed-dispersing birds free to eat the fruit.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Nevertheless,","Thus,","Likewise,","For example,"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The last sentence draws a conclusion from the facts before it (mammals destroy seeds, birds spread them, capsaicin hurts only mammals), so a cause-and-effect transition, 'Thus', fits. 'Nevertheless' signals contrast, 'Likewise' a similar point, and 'For example' an illustration of a general claim — none of which the sentence provides.","th":"ประโยคสุดท้ายเป็นข้อสรุปจากข้อเท็จจริงก่อนหน้า (สัตว์เลี้ยงลูกด้วยนมทำลายเมล็ด นกช่วยกระจายเมล็ด และสารแคปไซซินทำให้เจ็บเฉพาะสัตว์เลี้ยงลูกด้วยนม) จึงใช้คำเชื่อมแสดงผล Thus ส่วน Nevertheless แสดงความขัดแย้ง Likewise แสดงความคล้ายกัน และ For example ใช้ยกตัวอย่าง ซึ่งไม่ตรงกับหน้าที่ของประโยค"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Oceanography","th":"สมุทรศาสตร์"},"passage":"Until the late 1970s, many oceanographers assumed that the deep seafloor, far beyond the reach of sunlight, was nearly ______ of life. The discovery of hydrothermal vents near the Galápagos Islands in 1977 overturned this view: around the vents, where bacteria draw energy from chemicals in the hot water, scientists found dense colonies of giant tubeworms, clams, and crabs.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["capable","representative","fond","devoid"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The discovery 'overturned' the earlier view by revealing dense colonies of animals, so the old assumption must have been that the deep seafloor had almost no life — it was 'devoid' (empty) of life. 'Capable of life' would not be overturned by finding life, 'representative of life' makes no sense here, and 'fond of' describes affection.","th":"การค้นพบนี้ 'ล้มล้าง' ความเชื่อเดิมด้วยการพบสัตว์จำนวนมาก ความเชื่อเดิมจึงต้องเป็นว่าพื้นทะเลลึกแทบไม่มีสิ่งมีชีวิต = devoid of (ปราศจาก) ถ้าเชื่อเดิมว่า capable of life (มีชีวิตอยู่ได้) การพบสิ่งมีชีวิตก็ไม่ได้ล้มล้างอะไร representative of ไม่มีความหมายในบริบทนี้ และ fond of แปลว่าชอบ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Neuroscience","th":"ประสาทวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"Place cells in the hippocampus of rats fire when an animal occupies a particular spot in its environment, so that, collectively, they form something like an internal map. In a 2023 study, neuroscientist Ruth Adeyemi recorded these cells while rats rested between runs through a maze. The cells fired in rapid sequences resembling those produced when a rat actually runs along a path—sometimes along paths the animal had never run. Adeyemi cautions that the finding does not show that rats imagine or plan journeys; it shows only that the brain can generate route-like patterns of activity while the animal is still.","stem":"Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To argue that rats plan their routes through a maze before they begin moving","To challenge the view that place cells form an internal map of an animal's surroundings","To report a finding about activity in the rat hippocampus while qualifying what that finding can be taken to demonstrate","To explain why rats need to rest between runs through a maze in order to learn its layout"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The text presents Adeyemi's finding (route-like firing while rats rest) and then limits its meaning: it does not show planning or imagination. The claim that rats plan routes is exactly what Adeyemi says the finding does not show; the map view is accepted, not challenged; and the need for rest is never discussed.","th":"บทอ่านเสนอผลการศึกษา (เซลล์ยิงสัญญาณเหมือนกำลังวิ่งตามเส้นทาง ขณะที่หนูพักอยู่) แล้วจำกัดขอบเขตการตีความว่าไม่ได้พิสูจน์ว่าหนูวางแผนหรือจินตนาการ จุดประสงค์จึงเป็น 'รายงานผลการค้นพบพร้อมระบุข้อจำกัดของสิ่งที่ผลนั้นพิสูจน์ได้' การอ้างว่าหนูวางแผนเส้นทางคือสิ่งที่ Adeyemi บอกว่าพิสูจน์ไม่ได้ บทอ่านยอมรับแนวคิดแผนที่ภายใน ไม่ได้โต้แย้ง และไม่ได้พูดถึงความจำเป็นของการพัก"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-quantitative","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Agricultural science","th":"เกษตรศาสตร์"},"passage":"Agronomist Wanjiru Kamau grew maize and beans both separately (in monoculture) and together in alternating rows (intercropping). To compare such systems, agronomists use the land equivalent ratio (LER): for each crop, the intercropped yield is divided by the monoculture yield, and the resulting fractions are added together. An LER greater than 1 indicates that intercropping produced more from a given area than growing the same crops separately would have. Kamau concluded that intercropping used land more efficiently, even though each crop yielded less per hectare when intercropped.","table":{"title":"Yields in Kamau's trial (metric tons per hectare)","rows":[["Crop","Monoculture yield","Intercropped yield"],["Maize","6.0","4.5"],["Beans","2.0","1.0"]]},"stem":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Kamau's conclusion?","choices":["Intercropped maize produced 75% and intercropped beans 50% of their monoculture yields, giving an LER of 1.25.","Intercropped maize yielded 4.5 metric tons per hectare, 1.5 metric tons less than maize grown in monoculture.","Together, the intercropped crops yielded 5.5 metric tons per hectare, less than the 6.0 metric tons per hectare of maize grown in monoculture.","Intercropped beans produced 50% of their monoculture yield, so the LER for the trial was 0.5."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The conclusion is that intercropping used land more efficiently, which the text defines as an LER above 1. Maize: 4.5 ÷ 6.0 = 0.75; beans: 1.0 ÷ 2.0 = 0.5; LER = 0.75 + 0.5 = 1.25, which is greater than 1. The 1.5-ton drop in maize shows only that each crop yielded less; comparing 5.5 tons of mixed crops with 6.0 tons of maize ignores the LER method and seems to point the other way; an LER of 0.5 is miscalculated, since it leaves out the maize fraction.","th":"ข้อสรุปคือการปลูกร่วมใช้ที่ดินได้มีประสิทธิภาพกว่า ซึ่งบทอ่านนิยามว่า LER มากกว่า 1 ข้าวโพด 4.5 ÷ 6.0 = 0.75 ถั่ว 1.0 ÷ 2.0 = 0.5 รวม LER = 1.25 > 1 จึงสนับสนุนข้อสรุป ส่วนการที่ข้าวโพดลดลง 1.5 ตันแสดงแค่ว่าผลผลิตแต่ละพืชลดลง การเทียบ 5.5 ตันกับ 6.0 ตันไม่ได้ใช้วิธี LER และดูเหมือนแย้งข้อสรุป และ LER 0.5 คำนวณผิดเพราะไม่ได้รวมสัดส่วนของข้าวโพด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"form-structure-sense","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Botany","th":"พฤกษศาสตร์"},"passage":"Iceland's lichens grow on lava fields, sea cliffs, and even the walls of old farmhouses, yet for many years no one attempted to document them comprehensively. Having spent more than a decade collecting specimens across the island, ______","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["a field guide describing nearly 750 lichen species was published by botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir.","botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir's field guide describes nearly 750 lichen species.","botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir published a field guide describing nearly 750 lichen species.","nearly 750 lichen species are described in botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir's field guide."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The opening phrase 'Having spent more than a decade collecting specimens' must be followed immediately by the person who did the collecting. Only the version beginning with 'botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir' does this. The other versions make a field guide, the botanist's guide (a possessive, so the guide is the subject), or lichen species the collectors — dangling modifiers.","th":"วลีนำ Having spent more than a decade collecting specimens ต้องตามด้วยผู้ที่เก็บตัวอย่างทันที คือ botanist Sigrún Halldórsdóttir ส่วนตัวเลือกอื่นทำให้คู่มือภาคสนาม คู่มือของนักพฤกษศาสตร์ (รูปแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ ประธานจึงเป็นคู่มือ) หรือพันธุ์ไลเคนกลายเป็นผู้เก็บตัวอย่าง ซึ่งเป็น dangling modifier"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Urban history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์เมือง"},"passage":"During the hot summer of 1858, the stench of sewage in the River Thames grew so overpowering that Parliament considered abandoning Westminster. Within weeks, lawmakers funded engineer Joseph Bazalgette's plan for a network of sewers that would carry waste far downstream. The system was meant to remove foul odors, which many physicians of the day believed caused disease. ______ it also largely ended London's cholera outbreaks, since cholera in fact spreads through contaminated drinking water rather than through the air.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["As it happened,","For this reason,","Similarly,","In other words,"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The sewers were built for the wrong reason (to remove smells), yet they ended cholera for a different reason (cleaner water). 'As it happened' introduces this unplanned, fortunate outcome. 'For this reason' would make the mistaken belief about odors the cause of the success; 'Similarly' and 'In other words' signal a parallel point or a restatement, which the sentence is not.","th":"ท่อระบายน้ำถูกสร้างด้วยเหตุผลที่ผิด (เพื่อกำจัดกลิ่น) แต่บังเอิญยุติอหิวาตกโรคได้ด้วยเหตุผลอื่น (น้ำดื่มสะอาดขึ้น) จึงใช้ As it happened (ปรากฏว่า/บังเอิญว่า) ส่วน For this reason ทำให้ความเชื่อผิด ๆ เรื่องกลิ่นกลายเป็นสาเหตุของความสำเร็จ Similarly แสดงความคล้าย และ In other words ใช้กล่าวซ้ำด้วยคำอื่น ซึ่งไม่ตรงกับเนื้อความ"}}]}},{"exam":"sat","set":3,"title":{"en":"SAT Reading and Writing — Full practice test 3","th":"SAT Reading and Writing — ข้อสอบเสมือนจริงชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"Full test: 2 modules × 27 questions × 32 minutes (Module 2 is the harder module). DeepDistrict practice — not an official College Board test.","th":"ข้อสอบเต็ม 2 โมดูล โมดูลละ 27 ข้อ 32 นาที (โมดูล 2 เป็นชุดยาก) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการของ College Board"},"minutes":64,"kinds":{"sat":54},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"sat-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Planetary science","th":"วิทยาศาสตร์ดาวเคราะห์"},"passage":"Lava tubes form when the outer surface of a lava flow cools and hardens while molten rock keeps moving beneath it; once the eruption ends and the lava drains away, a hollow tunnel remains. Because thick rock walls would shield anyone inside from cosmic radiation, meteorite impacts, and extreme swings in surface temperature, many planetary scientists regard the large lava tubes detected beneath the Moon's surface as ______ locations for future crewed bases.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["hazardous","ornamental","promising","redundant"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The text lists only advantages of lava tubes (protection from radiation, impacts and temperature swings), so scientists must see them as good candidates for bases: 'promising'. 'Hazardous' contradicts the protective qualities described, 'ornamental' (decorative) is irrelevant to a base, and 'redundant' (unnecessary) does not follow from a list of benefits.","th":"บทความกล่าวถึงแต่ข้อดีของอุโมงค์ลาวา (ป้องกันรังสีคอสมิก อุกกาบาต และอุณหภูมิที่แกว่งรุนแรง) นักวิทยาศาสตร์จึงมองว่าเป็นทำเลที่ 'มีแนวโน้มดี' คือ promising ส่วน hazardous (อันตราย) ขัดกับคุณสมบัติด้านการป้องกัน ornamental (ใช้ประดับ) ไม่เกี่ยวกับฐานที่พัก และ redundant (ซ้ำซ้อนไม่จำเป็น) ไม่ได้ตามมาจากรายการข้อดี"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Literature (fiction)","th":"วรรณกรรม (บันเทิงคดี)"},"passage":"The following text is from a short story.\nMarguerite had rehearsed the conversation for weeks, polishing each sentence until it gleamed. She would be calm; she would be reasonable; she would not mention the piano. But when her brother opened the door wearing their father's gray cardigan, its elbows worn nearly through, every prepared word deserted her. She stood on the step and heard herself remark, absurdly, that the hedge needed trimming. Edmund looked at the hedge, then at her, and something in his face loosened. \"It always did,\" he said, and stepped aside to let her in.","stem":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It marks a shift from Marguerite's careful planning to an unscripted moment, prompted by a detail that recalls the siblings' shared past.","It reveals the specific disagreement that caused Marguerite and Edmund to become estranged.","It explains why Marguerite had decided beforehand that she would not mention the piano.","It suggests that Edmund had anticipated Marguerite's visit and prepared for it as carefully as she had."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The first two sentences show Marguerite's rehearsed plan; the third ('But when her brother opened the door…') shows the plan collapsing when she sees their father's cardigan, a reminder of their shared family past, and the rest of the text follows her unplanned remark. The sentence never names the cause of any quarrel or the reason for avoiding the piano, and it says nothing about Edmund preparing for the visit.","th":"สองประโยคแรกแสดงแผนที่มาร์เกอริตซ้อมมา ประโยคที่สามแสดงว่าแผนพังทลายเมื่อเธอเห็นเสื้อคาร์ดิแกนของพ่อซึ่งเตือนถึงอดีตที่พี่น้องมีร่วมกัน และส่วนที่เหลือเล่าคำพูดที่ไม่ได้เตรียมไว้ ประโยคนี้ไม่ได้บอกสาเหตุของความบาดหมาง ไม่ได้อธิบายเรื่องเปียโน และไม่ได้บอกว่าเอ็ดมันด์เตรียมตัวรอเธอ"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"inferences","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Geology","th":"ธรณีวิทยา"},"passage":"Tiny zircon crystals from the Jack Hills of Western Australia are among the oldest known fragments of Earth, some having formed about 4.4 billion years ago. The ratio of oxygen isotopes in several of these crystals matches the ratio found in rocks that formed from material altered by liquid water at Earth's surface, leading some geologists to conclude that oceans existed far earlier than once thought. Other geologists caution that fluids passing through the rocks long after the crystals formed could have changed their oxygen isotope ratios. If such later alteration occurred, then ______","stem":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["the crystals must have formed more recently than 4.4 billion years ago.","Earth's earliest oceans must have contained more oxygen than present-day oceans do.","the crystals' isotope ratios would not necessarily reveal whether liquid water was present at Earth's surface 4.4 billion years ago.","the Jack Hills would have been covered by ocean water for most of Earth's history."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The conclusion about early oceans depends on the isotope ratios recording conditions when the crystals formed. If fluids changed the ratios later, the ratios might reflect that later event instead, so they could no longer be trusted as evidence about the surface 4.4 billion years ago. Later alteration says nothing about when the crystals formed, about the oxygen content of ancient oceans, or about how long the Jack Hills were under water.","th":"ข้อสรุปเรื่องมหาสมุทรยุคแรกอาศัยว่าอัตราส่วนไอโซโทปบันทึกสภาพตอนที่ผลึกก่อตัว ถ้าของไหลเปลี่ยนอัตราส่วนในภายหลัง อัตราส่วนนั้นอาจสะท้อนเหตุการณ์ภายหลังแทน จึงใช้เป็นหลักฐานเรื่องน้ำบนผิวโลกเมื่อ 4.4 พันล้านปีก่อนไม่ได้อย่างแน่ชัด การเปลี่ยนแปลงภายหลังไม่ได้บอกว่าผลึกเกิดเมื่อใด ไม่ได้บอกปริมาณออกซิเจนในมหาสมุทรโบราณ และไม่ได้บอกว่าแจ็กฮิลส์จมน้ำนานเท่าใด"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Music history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์ดนตรี"},"passage":"Clara Schumann was among the most admired concert pianists of the nineteenth century, and her programs helped establish the solo recital as a concert format. Schumann—who first performed in public at the age of nine and toured Europe for more than six decades, often playing works by composers who were still largely ______ also composed songs, chamber music, and a piano concerto that she completed while still a teenager.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["unknown,","unknown—","unknown;","unknown"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The interruption about Schumann's performing career opens with a dash after 'Schumann', so it must close with a matching dash before the main verb 'also composed'. A comma does not pair with an opening dash, a semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, and leaving out punctuation would run the interruption into the main clause.","th":"ข้อความแทรกเกี่ยวกับอาชีพนักเปียโนเปิดด้วยขีดยาว (—) หลัง Schumann จึงต้องปิดด้วยขีดยาวเช่นกันก่อนกริยาหลัก also composed จุลภาคไม่เข้าคู่กับขีดยาว อัฒภาคตัดประธานออกจากกริยา และหากไม่ใส่เครื่องหมาย ข้อความแทรกจะต่อกับประโยคหลักผิดโครงสร้าง"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"e","topic":{"en":"Materials science","th":"วัสดุศาสตร์"},"passage":"Engineers have long been interested in the dragline silk that orb-weaving spiders use for the frames of their webs and for their safety lines. Weight for weight, this silk is stronger than steel, and spiders produce it at room temperature from a water-based solution of proteins. ______ it can stretch to roughly 130 percent of its original length before breaking, allowing a web to absorb the impact of a fast-flying insect without tearing.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["However,","Therefore,","Moreover,","For example,"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The sentence adds a second impressive property (stretchiness) to the first (strength), so an additive transition, 'Moreover', fits. 'However' would wrongly signal a contrast; 'Therefore' would claim that strength causes stretchiness; 'For example' would present the stretchiness as an instance of strength, which it is not.","th":"ประโยคนี้เพิ่มคุณสมบัติเด่นข้อที่สอง (ยืดได้) ต่อจากข้อแรก (แข็งแรง) จึงใช้คำเชื่อมแสดงการเพิ่มเติม Moreover ส่วน However แสดงความขัดแย้งซึ่งไม่มี Therefore อ้างว่าความแข็งแรงเป็นสาเหตุของการยืด และ For example ทำให้การยืดกลายเป็นตัวอย่างของความแข็งแรง ซึ่งไม่ถูกต้อง"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"words-in-context","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Behavioral economics","th":"เศรษฐศาสตร์พฤติกรรม"},"passage":"When employers automatically enroll new workers in a retirement savings plan, allowing them to opt out if they wish, participation rates are often well above 80 percent; when workers must actively sign up, rates are frequently far lower. Economists attribute much of this gap to inertia. Rather than ______ a decision that has already been made on their behalf, most people simply let it stand, even when changing it would take only a few minutes.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["ratify","contest","anticipate","publicize"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The sentence contrasts letting a decision 'stand' with doing something to it; the logical opposite of accepting a default is challenging or overriding it: 'contest'. 'Ratify' (formally approve) is a trap: approving a decision is close to letting it stand, so 'rather than ratify' makes no sense. You cannot 'anticipate' a decision that has already been made, and 'publicize' has nothing to do with inertia.","th":"ประโยคเปรียบการปล่อยให้การตัดสินใจคงอยู่ กับการทำบางอย่างต่อการตัดสินใจนั้น สิ่งที่ตรงข้ามกับการยอมรับค่าเริ่มต้นคือการคัดค้านหรือเปลี่ยนแปลง จึงใช้ contest ส่วน ratify (ให้สัตยาบัน/รับรอง) เป็นตัวลวง เพราะการรับรองก็ใกล้เคียงกับการปล่อยไว้ จึงขัดกับ rather than คาดการณ์ (anticipate) การตัดสินใจที่เกิดขึ้นแล้วไม่ได้ และการเผยแพร่ (publicize) ไม่เกี่ยวกับความเฉื่อย"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"cs","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"European history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์ยุโรป"},"passage":"Popular accounts often credit Johannes Gutenberg's printing press with igniting the Protestant Reformation almost single-handedly. Historian Margit Ellery contends that such accounts confuse a necessary condition with a sufficient one. To be sure, Martin Luther's pamphlets circulated in hundreds of thousands of copies within a few years of 1517, a scale unimaginable a century earlier. But presses had operated in German-speaking lands for more than half a century without provoking comparable upheaval; what changed, Ellery argues, was the convergence of print with the political ambitions of territorial princes and a literate urban public hungry for religious writing in its own language.","stem":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It acknowledges evidence that appears to support the view Ellery questions before the text explains why that evidence is not decisive.","It presents the main evidence Ellery uses to show that the printing press was irrelevant to the Reformation.","It introduces a counterargument to Ellery's claim that the text ultimately accepts as correct.","It explains why Luther chose to publish his ideas in pamphlets rather than in longer books."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'To be sure' signals a concession: the huge circulation of Luther's pamphlets seems to back the popular view that print caused the Reformation. The next sentence ('But …') limits that evidence: presses had existed for decades without such upheaval, so print was necessary but not sufficient. Ellery never calls the press irrelevant, the text does not end up accepting the popular view, and Luther's choice of format is never discussed.","th":"To be sure เป็นสัญญาณของการยอมรับบางส่วน คือยอดพิมพ์จุลสารของลูเทอร์ดูเหมือนสนับสนุนความเชื่อที่ว่าแท่นพิมพ์ทำให้เกิดการปฏิรูปศาสนา ประโยคถัดไป (But …) จำกัดน้ำหนักของหลักฐานนั้น เพราะแท่นพิมพ์มีมาหลายสิบปีโดยไม่เกิดความปั่นป่วนแบบนี้ แท่นพิมพ์จึงจำเป็นแต่ไม่เพียงพอ เอลเลอรีไม่ได้บอกว่าแท่นพิมพ์ไม่เกี่ยวข้อง บทความไม่ได้ยอมรับมุมมองยอดนิยมในตอนท้าย และไม่ได้พูดถึงเหตุผลที่ลูเทอร์เลือกพิมพ์เป็นจุลสาร"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"ii","skill":"evidence-textual","difficulty":"h","topic":{"en":"Cognitive psychology","th":"จิตวิทยาการรู้คิด"},"passage":"In several experiments, museum visitors who photographed artworks later recalled fewer details of those artworks than visitors who simply looked at them, a pattern known as the photo-taking-impairment effect. Psychologist Noor Haddad hypothesizes that the effect arises from cognitive offloading: people who take photos implicitly expect the camera to remember for them and so devote less effort to forming their own memories. To test her hypothesis, Haddad told some visitors that their photos would be saved and told others that each photo would be deleted automatically as soon as it was taken.","stem":"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Haddad's hypothesis?","choices":["Visitors who expected to keep their photos recalled fewer details than visitors who were told that their photos would be deleted.","Visitors who took photos reported enjoying their museum visit about as much as visitors who did not take photos.","Visitors who photographed artworks still recalled fewer details than non-photographers when tested a week later.","Visitors who were told that their photos would be deleted immediately recalled as few details as visitors who expected to keep their photos."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"If offloading is the cause, visitors who know their photos will vanish cannot rely on the camera, so they should remember more, like non-photographers. Finding that they remembered just as little as those keeping their photos means the impairment occurs even without any expectation that the camera will remember, which weakens the hypothesis. Keepers recalling less than deleters is what the hypothesis predicts (support). Enjoyment is irrelevant, and a lasting impairment does not distinguish offloading from other explanations.","th":"หากสาเหตุคือการฝากความจำไว้กับกล้อง ผู้ที่รู้ว่าภาพจะถูกลบทันทีพึ่งกล้องไม่ได้ จึงควรจำได้มากขึ้นเหมือนผู้ที่ไม่ถ่ายภาพ ถ้าพบว่าพวกเขาจำได้น้อยเท่ากับผู้ที่เก็บภาพไว้ แปลว่าการจำได้น้อยเกิดขึ้นแม้ไม่ได้คาดหวังว่ากล้องจะจำแทน จึงทำให้สมมติฐานอ่อนลง การที่ผู้เก็บภาพจำได้น้อยกว่าผู้ที่ภาพถูกลบตรงกับสมมติฐาน (สนับสนุน) ความเพลิดเพลินไม่เกี่ยวข้อง และผลที่คงอยู่หนึ่งสัปดาห์ไม่ได้แยกการฝากความจำออกจากคำอธิบายอื่น"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"sec","skill":"form-structure-sense","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"Developmental psychology","th":"จิตวิทยาพัฒนาการ"},"passage":"In the original \"marshmallow test\" studies of the 1960s and 1970s, children who waited longer for a second treat tended to have higher test scores as adolescents. In 2018, a team of researchers repeated the study with a much larger and more diverse sample of children. The ______ conclusion was that the link between a young child's ability to delay gratification and later academic achievement, while real, was considerably weaker than the original studies had suggested.","stem":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["researcher's","researchers","researchers's","researchers'"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The conclusion belongs to several researchers (the team), so the plural possessive 'researchers'' is needed. 'Researcher's' is singular possessive (one researcher), 'researchers' is a plain plural with no possession, and 'researchers's' is not a standard form.","th":"ข้อสรุปเป็นของนักวิจัยหลายคน (ทั้งทีม) จึงต้องใช้รูปแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของพหูพจน์ researchers' ส่วน researcher's เป็นเจ้าของเอกพจน์ (นักวิจัยคนเดียว) researchers เป็นพหูพจน์ที่ไม่แสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ และ researchers's ไม่ใช่รูปมาตรฐาน"}},{"kind":"sat","domain":"eoi","skill":"transitions","difficulty":"m","topic":{"en":"History of medicine","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์การแพทย์"},"passage":"Long before Edward Jenner developed his smallpox vaccine in 1796, practitioners in China, India, the Ottoman Empire, and parts of Africa practiced variolation, deliberately exposing healthy people to material taken from smallpox sores. The procedure carried real risks: a small proportion of variolated patients developed severe smallpox and died, and they could spread the disease to others. ______ death rates among people who were variolated were far lower than among people who caught smallpox naturally, which explains why the practice persisted for centuries.","stem":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Likewise,","Even so,","In other words,","As a result,"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The previous sentence lists the dangers of variolation; the next sentence gives a contrasting point (it was still far safer than natural infection). 'Even so' signals that contrast. 'Likewise' would introduce a similar point, 'In other words' a restatement of the risks, and 'As a result' would absurdly claim that the risks caused the lower death rates.","th":"ประโยคก่อนหน้าบอกอันตรายของการปลูกฝีแบบโบราณ ประโยคถัดไปเสนอประเด็นตรงข้าม คือยังปลอดภัยกว่าการติดเชื้อเองมาก จึงใช้ Even so ส่วน Likewise ใช้เสนอประเด็นที่คล้ายกัน In other words ใช้กล่าวซ้ำด้วยคำอื่น และ As a result จะสื่อผิดว่าความเสี่ยงทำให้อัตราการตายต่ำลง"}}]}},{"exam":"ielts","set":1,"title":{"en":"IELTS Academic — Practice test 1 (Reading + Writing)","th":"IELTS Academic — ชุดฝึก 1 (Reading + Writing)"},"note":{"en":"Reading: 3 passages, 40 questions, 60 minutes. Writing: Task 1 + Task 2, 60 minutes. DeepDistrict practice — not an official IELTS test.","th":"Reading 3 บทความ 40 ข้อ 60 นาที · Writing Task 1 + Task 2 60 นาที — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการ"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"ielts-r":3,"ielts-w":2},"subjects":{},"free":true,"file":"ielts-1","sample":{"reading":[{"kind":"ielts-r","title":"Archives in the ice","topic":{"en":"Climate science and glaciology","th":"วิทยาศาสตร์ภูมิอากาศและธารน้ำแข็ง"},"difficulty":1,"labelled":true,"paragraphs":["The ice sheets of Antarctica and Greenland are the most detailed archives of the Earth's past climate that scientists possess. Each year's snowfall is buried by the next, and over millennia the accumulated layers are compressed into ice that may be more than three kilometres thick. Locked inside are traces of the atmosphere and dust of the time when the snow fell. The longest continuous record recovered so far reaches back roughly 800,000 years, and several international teams are now drilling in the hope of extending it to well over a million.","Snow does not become ice overnight. Freshly fallen snow is mostly air. As it is buried, the crystals are rounded and packed more tightly into a granular material known as firn, through which air can still move between the grains. Only at a depth of somewhere between 50 and 100 metres do the pores finally close, sealing small samples of air into bubbles. One consequence is easily overlooked: because air continues to circulate through the firn for decades or centuries after the snow itself has fallen, the gas in any bubble is younger than the ice that surrounds it. At sites where little snow accumulates, the difference can amount to several thousand years, and it must be calculated with care before the records of gas and of ice can be compared.","Drilling teams generally select a dome or a ridge on the ice sheet, where there is minimal horizontal flow, so that the layers lie undisturbed in the order in which they were laid down. A hollow drill extracts a cylinder of ice, which is raised in sections of around three metres and stored at minus 20 degrees Celsius or colder. A particular difficulty arises at intermediate depths, typically between about 600 and 1,200 metres. Here the bubbles are under great pressure, and when the ice is brought up and the confining weight above it is removed, it tends to crack and even shatter. Ice from this so-called brittle zone is therefore commonly left to rest in a storage trench beneath the surface, sometimes for a whole year, while the pressure slowly equalises before it is cut.","Establishing the age of each layer is the next challenge. In the upper part of a core, annual layers can be counted much like tree rings, using visible bands as well as chemical signals that peak in a particular season. Deeper down, however, the weight of the overlying ice stretches the layers so thin that they can no longer be distinguished, and researchers turn instead to computer models of how the ice has flowed. These models are anchored by fixed points such as layers of volcanic ash or sulphate from eruptions whose dates are known independently. Very large eruptions deposit material in both hemispheres, which allows cores from Greenland and Antarctica to be placed on a common timescale.","Past temperatures can be read from the water itself. Molecules containing heavier isotopes of oxygen and hydrogen condense more readily than lighter ones, so the colder the air, the smaller the share of heavy molecules that reaches the polar snow. By measuring this ratio, scientists can estimate the temperature at which the snow formed. The method has pitfalls. The relationship between the ratio and temperature is usually calibrated using differences between places today, and there is no guarantee that it held in the past. If, for instance, a greater proportion of the year's snow fell in summer during some earlier period, the record would suggest a warmer climate than actually existed. An independent check comes from the borehole itself: the ice retains a faint memory of past surface temperatures, which can be measured with sensitive thermometers lowered down the hole. In central Greenland, such measurements indicated that the coldest part of the last glacial period had been substantially colder than the isotopes alone had implied.","Greenhouse gases are measured as well. When samples are crushed in a vacuum, the air released from the bubbles can be analysed directly. Over the past 800,000 years, the concentration of carbon dioxide rose and fell between roughly 180 parts per million in glacial periods and about 280 in warm intervals; today it exceeds 420. Antarctic ice is preferred for these measurements. Greenland ice contains more dust and other impurities, and chemical reactions between them can generate additional carbon dioxide inside the ice, distorting the record, although Greenland cores remain invaluable for methane and for precise dating.","The search is now on for ice that is 1.5 million years old or more. Such a record would cover a puzzling period about a million years ago when the rhythm of ice ages changed from cycles of roughly 41,000 years to cycles of around 100,000 years. At the base of the thickest ice sheets, heat escaping from the rock below can melt the oldest layers away. Candidate sites are identified through airborne radar surveys that reveal the internal structure of the ice and the temperature conditions at its base. Even where ancient ice survives, the deepest layers are so compressed that a single metre may contain more than 10,000 years."],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"text":"The air sealed into a layer of ice is of the same age as the frozen snow that encloses it.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Sections of core from intermediate depths are usually cut up as soon as they reach the surface.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Volcanic material is found more frequently in Greenland cores than in Antarctic ones.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]},{"text":"A change in the season when most snow falls could make a past climate appear warmer than it was.","answer":["TRUE"]},{"text":"Temperature measurements made in a borehole suggested that the last ice age had been less severe than isotope evidence indicated.","answer":["FALSE"]}]},{"type":"matching-info","instructions":"Reading Passage 1 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–G. NB You may use any letter more than once.","questions":[{"text":"a way of testing an estimate by using a separate source of evidence","answer":["E"]},{"text":"a means of matching records taken from opposite ends of the globe","answer":["D"]},{"text":"the reason why some ice is not processed immediately after it is drilled","answer":["C"]},{"text":"an assumption about the present that may not apply to the distant past","answer":["E"]}]},{"type":"flowchart-completion","instructions":"Complete the flow-chart below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","wordLimit":{"words":2,"number":false},"context":"From snowfall to dated sample\n\nFresh snow is buried by later snowfall\n↓\nGrains become rounder and are packed into (10) ______, through which air can still circulate\n↓\nAt 50–100 metres the pores close and air is trapped in (11) ______\n↓\nDrilling takes place on a dome or ridge where there is little (12) ______, so the layers stay in order\n↓\nIn the upper core, (13) ______ are counted; deeper down, flow models are fixed to dated eruptions","questions":[{"text":"(10) ______","answer":["firn"]},{"text":"(11) ______","answer":["bubbles"]},{"text":"(12) ______","answer":["horizontal flow"]},{"text":"(13) ______","answer":["annual layers"]}]}]}],"writing":[{"kind":"ielts-w","task":1,"visual":"table","prompt":"The table below shows the average number of hours per week that boys and girls aged 13–17 in one country spent on five activities outside school hours in 2010 and 2025.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.","table":{"title":"Average hours per week spent on activities outside school, ages 13–17","rows":[["Activity","Boys 2010","Girls 2010","Boys 2025","Girls 2025"],["Social media and messaging","4.5","6.0","13.0","17.5"],["Homework and private study","7.0","8.5","6.5","8.0"],["Sport and exercise","6.5","4.0","4.5","3.0"],["Reading for pleasure","3.0","4.5","1.5","2.5"],["Paid part-time work","2.5","2.0","1.0","1.5"]]}},{"kind":"ielts-w","task":2,"essayType":"opinion","prompt":"Now that artificial intelligence tools can produce fluent essays in seconds, some educators argue that schools should stop setting written homework altogether and assess students' writing only under supervision in the classroom.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words."}]}},{"exam":"ielts","set":2,"title":{"en":"IELTS Academic — Practice test 2 (Reading + Writing)","th":"IELTS Academic — ชุดฝึก 2 (Reading + Writing)"},"note":{"en":"Reading: 3 passages, 40 questions, 60 minutes. Writing: Task 1 + Task 2, 60 minutes. DeepDistrict practice — not an official IELTS test.","th":"Reading 3 บทความ 40 ข้อ 60 นาที · Writing Task 1 + Task 2 60 นาที — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการ"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"ielts-r":3,"ielts-w":2},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"ielts-2","sample":{"reading":[{"kind":"ielts-r","title":"Frozen assets: the rise and fall of the ice trade","difficulty":2,"topic":{"en":"Economic history","th":"ประวัติศาสตร์เศรษฐกิจ"},"paragraphs":["Before the age of electricity, keeping anything cold in a hot climate was a luxury reserved for the very wealthy. Rulers in Persia, China and the Mediterranean had stored winter snow in pits and thick-walled buildings for centuries, but the quantities were small and the cold rarely travelled far. That changed in the nineteenth century, when an unlikely industry grew up around a resource that seemed, to most people, entirely worthless: the ice that formed each winter on the lakes and ponds of New England. Within a few decades, frozen water cut from those ponds was being sold in the Caribbean, in South America and as far away as India.","The industry is usually traced to Frederic Tudor, a young Boston merchant who in 1806 sent a ship loaded with ice to the Caribbean island of Martinique. His contemporaries thought the plan absurd, and the first voyages appeared to prove them right. Much of the cargo melted before it could be sold, and the islanders who received the rest had little idea what to do with it. Tudor's difficulty, however, was not the crossing itself; ice packed tightly in a ship's hold melts far more slowly than most people expected. The real problem lay at the destination, where there was nowhere to keep the ice once it had been unloaded. Only after years of heavy losses, including a spell in prison for debt, did he begin to build insulated ice houses in the ports he supplied.","Two developments turned this fragile business into a profitable one. The first was the choice of insulation. Early shippers had experimented with straw, hay and rice husks, but sawdust proved the most effective, and it had the additional merit of being almost free, since the timber mills of Maine produced it in enormous quantities as waste. The second was the mechanisation of harvesting. Cutting ice by hand with axes and saws produced blocks of uneven size that wasted space in storage and in transit. A horse-drawn cutter, introduced by one of Tudor's suppliers, Nathaniel Wyeth, scored the frozen surface of a pond in a regular grid, so that workers could split it into identical blocks that could be stacked with almost no gaps between them. Tightly stacked blocks melted more slowly, because less of their surface was exposed to warm air.","With these improvements, the trade expanded rapidly. In 1833 a cargo of ice left Boston for Calcutta, a voyage of around four months that took the ship twice across the equator; more than half of the ice was still frozen when it arrived. By the middle of the century, ships were carrying ice to ports throughout the tropics, and the ponds around Boston had become so valuable that the rights to harvest them were bought and sold like farmland. The writer Henry David Thoreau, who lived for two years beside Walden Pond, recorded watching a large team of labourers remove ice from its surface one winter, and reflected that the water he drank might one day cool a glass in India.","Yet the largest market for natural ice was never overseas. As cities in the United States grew, so did the demand for ice at home. Households bought it to fill wooden iceboxes, in which food could be kept fresh for several days, while brewers needed it to produce lager, a style of beer that must be fermented at low temperatures. Meat-packers in the Midwest packed ice into railway wagons to send fresh beef to the cities of the east coast, an innovation that changed the national diet. In Europe a similar pattern emerged, although most of the supply came not from America but from Norway, whose lakes provided ice that was shipped across the North Sea to British ports in huge quantities.","The success of the industry concealed two weaknesses. The first was its dependence on the weather. A mild winter could leave the ponds too thin to cut, and in such years the price of ice rose sharply; the shortages that followed were known as ice famines. The second weakness was purity. As towns grew along rivers and lakes, the water that froze into ice was increasingly polluted by sewage and industrial waste, and doctors began to warn that the same ice that preserved food could also spread disease. Public confidence, once lost, proved hard to restore.","These problems created an opening for a competitor. Machines that produced ice by compressing and expanding gases had existed since the middle of the century, but they had been expensive and unreliable. As the technology improved, factory-made ice, known in the trade as plant ice, became cheaper; made from filtered water, it could be produced in any season and in any town, close to the customers who needed it. The arrival of affordable electric refrigerators in private homes during the 1920s and 1930s removed the need for regular ice deliveries, and an industry that had once employed tens of thousands of people faded almost entirely from public memory."],"groups":[{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.\n\nQuestions 1–5","questions":[{"text":"Tudor's early ventures failed chiefly because the ice melted quickly during the sea voyage.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"One reason for using sawdust as insulation was its low cost.","answer":["TRUE"]},{"text":"Blocks of ice cut by hand were generally heavier than those cut with Wyeth's equipment.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]},{"text":"European demand for ice was met largely by imports from the United States.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Warnings from doctors led to a ban on selling ice taken from polluted rivers.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]}]},{"type":"matching-info","instructions":"Reading Passage 1 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–G.\n\nQuestions 6–8","questions":[{"text":"a contrast between the assumed cause of a commercial failure and its actual cause","answer":["B"]},{"text":"a reference to methods of preserving cold that existed long before the ice trade","answer":["A"]},{"text":"an explanation of why a rival product was more convenient for buyers","answer":["G"]}]},{"type":"note-completion","instructions":"Complete the notes below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.\n\nQuestions 9–11","wordLimit":{"words":1,"number":false},"context":"Uses of natural ice within the United States\n• Households: kept food fresh in (9) ______\n• Brewers: needed low temperatures to make (10) ______\n• Meat-packers: sent beef east in railway (11) ______","questions":[{"text":"(9) ______","answer":["iceboxes"]},{"text":"(10) ______","answer":["lager"]},{"text":"(11) ______","answer":["wagons"]}]},{"type":"short-answer","instructions":"Answer the questions below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.\n\nQuestions 12–13","wordLimit":{"words":2,"number":false},"questions":[{"text":"What name was given to the shortages that followed unusually warm winters?","answer":["ice famines"]},{"text":"What term did the industry use for ice manufactured by machines?","answer":["plant ice"]}]}]}],"writing":[{"kind":"ielts-w","task":1,"visual":"table","prompt":"The table below gives information about public transport in five cities in 2024.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.","table":{"title":"Public transport in five cities, 2024","rows":[["City","Population (millions)","Rail/metro network (km)","Public transport trips per day (millions)","Commuters using public transport (%)","Single fare (US$)"],["Halvern","8.4","410","9.8","63","1.80"],["Marlow Bay","6.9","40","1.7","21","0.60"],["Corrin","5.2","95","2.9","38","0.90"],["Tessaly","3.6","210","3.1","55","2.60"],["Velden","1.8","0","0.9","34","1.20"]]}},{"kind":"ielts-w","task":2,"essayType":"two-part","prompt":"In many countries, people are living longer than ever before, yet a growing share of those extra years is spent in poor health.\n\nWhy is this happening? What can individuals and governments do to make sure that longer lives are also healthier ones?\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words."}]}},{"exam":"ielts","set":3,"title":{"en":"IELTS Academic — Practice test 3 (Reading + Writing)","th":"IELTS Academic — ชุดฝึก 3 (Reading + Writing)"},"note":{"en":"Reading: 3 passages, 40 questions, 60 minutes. Writing: Task 1 + Task 2, 60 minutes. DeepDistrict practice — not an official IELTS test.","th":"Reading 3 บทความ 40 ข้อ 60 นาที · Writing Task 1 + Task 2 60 นาที — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบทางการ"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"ielts-r":3,"ielts-w":2},"subjects":{},"free":false,"file":"ielts-3","sample":{"reading":[{"kind":"ielts-r","title":"Lessons from Roman concrete","difficulty":2,"labelled":true,"topic":{"en":"Materials science and engineering","th":"วัสดุศาสตร์และวิศวกรรม"},"paragraphs":["Concrete is the most widely used manufactured material on Earth; after water, it is often said to be the substance that people consume in the greatest quantity. Its essential ingredient, cement, is made by heating limestone and clay to around 1,450°C in a kiln, a process that releases carbon dioxide both from the fuel that is burnt and from the limestone itself as it breaks down. Cement manufacture is estimated to account for roughly 8 per cent of global carbon dioxide emissions. Modern reinforced concrete is also surprisingly short-lived: many bridges and buildings from the twentieth century required major repairs within fifty to a hundred years. It is a striking irony, then, that some structures built by the Romans two thousand years ago are still standing, and that a few appear to be stronger now than when they were completed.","Roman builders did not use Portland cement, which was patented in England only in 1824. Instead, they combined lime, produced by heating limestone at lower temperatures than modern kilns reach, with volcanic ash, the finest of which came from deposits around the Bay of Naples. The ash, rich in silica and alumina, reacted with the lime and water to form a binding paste. Writers of the period describe how this mixture was packed down together with chunks of rock or broken brick, rather than poured as a liquid in the way that modern concrete is. The result was denser than might be expected, and its chemistry differed fundamentally from that of the modern material.","The most remarkable examples are the harbour walls and piers that Roman engineers built directly in the sea. For modern concrete, seawater is an enemy: salt penetrates the material and corrodes the steel bars inside, and as the steel rusts and swells, the surrounding concrete cracks. Roman marine concrete contains no steel, and analysis of cores drilled from ancient harbours has revealed something more surprising still. As seawater seeped through the structures over centuries, it dissolved part of the volcanic material and allowed rare minerals to crystallise in the gaps, among them a plate-shaped mineral called aluminous tobermorite. These interlocking crystals appear to have reinforced the material from within, so that contact with the sea, rather than weakening it, gradually made it more cohesive.","A second puzzle concerned the small white lumps of lime scattered through many samples of Roman concrete. Researchers had long dismissed these 'lime clasts' as signs of careless mixing or poor raw materials. More recent studies have proposed another explanation: that the Romans mixed their concrete 'hot', adding quicklime directly instead of first combining it with water. The intense heat of this reaction would have left behind lumps of brittle, calcium-rich material. When a crack later formed and water entered it, the lumps could dissolve and recrystallise as calcium carbonate, sealing the crack. To test the idea, one team made new samples using the hot-mixing method, cracked them deliberately and ran water through them. Within about two weeks the cracks had closed, whereas in otherwise identical samples made without quicklime the water continued to flow.","It would be wrong, however, to conclude that Roman concrete is simply superior. It is considerably weaker in compression than its modern equivalent and takes far longer to reach its full strength, which would be unacceptable on a modern building site, where the timber or metal moulds must be removed and work on the next floor begun within days. Nor could it be used for the slender beams and tall towers of contemporary architecture, which depend on the steel reinforcement that Roman builders did without. Moreover, suitable volcanic ash occurs only in certain regions, and supplying it in the quantities that today's construction industry consumes would be impractical.","What engineers hope to borrow, therefore, is not the Roman recipe but some of its principles. One line of research adds volcanic ash or industrial by-products such as fly ash from power stations to modern mixes, reducing the amount of cement required and, with it, carbon emissions. Another seeks to reproduce the self-healing effect by other means. Some experimental concretes contain dormant bacteria, packed with a food source in tiny capsules; when a crack lets in water, the bacteria become active and produce limestone that fills the gap. Others contain capsules of a liquid healing agent that burst open when the concrete around them cracks.","Whether such materials are adopted widely will depend less on chemistry than on economics and regulation. Self-healing concretes currently cost considerably more than conventional mixes, and building codes, which are based on decades of experience with Portland cement, are slow to accept materials whose long-term behaviour is unknown. Supporters argue that the higher initial price should be weighed against the cost of repairs over a structure's whole life, which can exceed the cost of building it in the first place. If they are right, the most valuable lesson of Roman concrete may be one of perspective: a material should be judged not only by how quickly it can be built with, but by how long it lasts."],"groups":[{"type":"matching-info","instructions":"Reading Passage 1 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–G.","questions":[{"text":"the date when the modern type of cement was first given legal protection","answer":["B"]},{"text":"a feature of the material that was once taken as evidence of poor workmanship","answer":["D"]},{"text":"a reason why the ancient material would delay modern construction projects","answer":["E"]}]},{"type":"tfng","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? Write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","questions":[{"text":"The carbon dioxide released in making cement comes only from the fuel burnt in the kilns.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Roman writers recorded the exact proportions of lime and ash used in their concrete.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]},{"text":"In Roman harbour structures, new minerals formed as a result of seawater passing through the concrete.","answer":["TRUE"]},{"text":"The hot-mixing experiment was carried out on samples taken from ancient buildings.","answer":["FALSE"]},{"text":"Concrete containing capsules of healing agent was developed after concrete containing bacteria.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"]}]},{"type":"table-completion","instructions":"Complete the table below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","wordLimit":{"words":1,"number":false},"context":"Roman and modern concrete compared\nFeature | Roman concrete | Modern concrete\nBinder | lime combined with volcanic (9) ______ | Portland cement\nHow it is placed | packed down with chunks of rock or brick | poured as a liquid\nEffect of seawater | crystals strengthen it from within | salt attacks the steel (10) ______ inside\nMain drawbacks | slow to reach full strength; weaker in (11) ______ | short-lived; high carbon emissions","questions":[{"text":"(9) ______","answer":["ash"]},{"text":"(10) ______","answer":["bars"]},{"text":"(11) ______","answer":["compression"]}]},{"type":"mcq-multi","instructions":"Choose TWO letters, A–E.","questions":[{"text":"Which TWO ways of improving modern concrete are being investigated, according to the passage?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"replacing part of the cement with volcanic ash or industrial waste"},{"key":"B","text":"removing steel reinforcement from tall buildings"},{"key":"C","text":"heating limestone to higher temperatures in the kiln"},{"key":"D","text":"importing volcanic ash from the Bay of Naples"},{"key":"E","text":"adding inactive bacteria that produce limestone when water gets in"}],"answer":["A","E"]}]}]}],"writing":[{"kind":"ielts-w","task":1,"visual":"table","prompt":"The table below gives information about the use of public libraries in one country in 2005, 2015 and 2025.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.","table":{"title":"Public libraries in one country, 2005–2025","rows":[["","2005","2015","2025"],["Number of libraries","4,620","4,130","3,580"],["Visits (millions)","312","268","241"],["Printed books borrowed (millions)","405","290","176"],["E-books and audiobooks borrowed (millions)","0.4","38","121"],["Average opening hours per week","46","41","43"],["Events held (thousands)","58","84","131"]]}},{"kind":"ielts-w","task":2,"essayType":"discussion","prompt":"Some people argue that governments should fund scientific research only when it is likely to produce practical benefits, such as new medicines or technologies. Others believe that research driven purely by curiosity deserves equal public support, even if it has no obvious use.\n\nDiscuss both these views and give your own opinion.\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words."}]}},{"exam":"sk1","set":1,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.1 English (GATE / EPLUS+) — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE / EPLUS+) — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"40 questions in 50 minutes: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, cloze and reading, in the style of the Suankularb M.1 pre-test. GATE and EPLUS+ applicants take the same paper; EPLUS+ needs a higher score. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"40 ข้อ 50 นาที: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ตามแนวข้อสอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EPLUS+ ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน EPLUS+ ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":50,"kinds":{"m1":40},"subjects":{"eng":40},"free":true,"file":"sk1-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to thanks","th":"การตอบรับคำขอบคุณ"},"topic":{"en":"School conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในโรงเรียน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Thanks so much for helping me carry these books to the library.\nB: ______","choices":["Never mind.","Help yourself.","I'm afraid not.","Don't mention it."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Don't mention it\" is a polite reply to thanks. \"Never mind\" answers an apology or a problem, \"Help yourself\" invites someone to take something, and \"I'm afraid not\" is a negative answer to a question.","th":"Don't mention it ใช้ตอบเมื่อมีคนขอบคุณ (ไม่เป็นไรเลย) ส่วน Never mind ใช้ตอบคำขอโทษหรือบอกว่าไม่ต้องกังวล Help yourself ใช้เชิญให้หยิบเอง และ I'm afraid not เป็นการตอบปฏิเสธคำถาม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Accepting an apology","th":"การตอบรับคำขอโทษ"},"topic":{"en":"School conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในโรงเรียน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: I'm sorry I'm late. The traffic on Rama IV Road was awful.\nB: ______ We've only just started.","choices":["Me neither.","Don't mention it.","Never mind.","You're welcome."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Never mind\" accepts an apology and tells the person not to worry, which fits \"We've only just started\". \"Don't mention it\" and \"You're welcome\" answer thanks, not an apology, and \"Me neither\" agrees with a negative sentence.","th":"Never mind ใช้ตอบคำขอโทษ แปลว่า ไม่เป็นไร เข้ากับ We've only just started (เพิ่งเริ่มเอง) ส่วน Don't mention it และ You're welcome ใช้ตอบคำขอบคุณ และ Me neither ใช้เห็นด้วยกับประโยคปฏิเสธ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing the right word form (adjective)","th":"การเลือกชนิดของคำ (คำคุณศัพท์)"},"topic":{"en":"School life","th":"ชีวิตในโรงเรียน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\nPlease be ______ in the library; other students are reading.","choices":["loudly","quiet","silence","noise"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"After \"be\" we need an adjective: \"be quiet\". \"Silence\" and \"noise\" are nouns, and \"loudly\" is an adverb with the opposite meaning.","th":"หลัง be ต้องใช้คำคุณศัพท์ จึงใช้ quiet (เงียบ) ส่วน silence และ noise เป็นคำนาม และ loudly เป็นคำกริยาวิเศษณ์ที่ความหมายตรงข้าม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Adjectives of feeling (-ing / -ed)","th":"คำคุณศัพท์บอกความรู้สึก (-ing / -ed)"},"topic":{"en":"Media and entertainment","th":"สื่อและความบันเทิง"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\nThe documentary about the ocean was so ______ that I watched it twice.","choices":["fascinated","exhausted","fascinating","disappointing"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The documentary causes the feeling, so we use the -ing form \"fascinating\". \"Fascinated\" describes how a person feels, \"exhausted\" means very tired, and a \"disappointing\" film would not make you watch it twice.","th":"สารคดีเป็นสิ่งที่ทำให้เกิดความรู้สึก จึงใช้รูป -ing คือ fascinating (น่าทึ่ง) ส่วน fascinated ใช้บอกความรู้สึกของคน exhausted แปลว่า เหนื่อยมาก และสารคดีที่ disappointing (น่าผิดหวัง) คงไม่มีใครดูซ้ำ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"There is / there are with uncountable nouns","th":"There is / There are กับนามนับไม่ได้"},"topic":{"en":"Countable and uncountable nouns","th":"นามนับได้และนับไม่ได้"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the correct answer to complete the sentence.\nThere ______ some milk in the fridge if you're thirsty.","choices":["is","are","has","have"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Milk\" is uncountable, so it takes \"there is\". \"There are\" is for plural nouns, and we never say \"there have\" or \"there has\" to show that something exists.","th":"milk เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ จึงใช้ There is ส่วน There are ใช้กับนามพหูพจน์ และไม่ใช้ there have หรือ there has เพื่อบอกว่ามีสิ่งใดอยู่"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"make + object + bare infinitive","th":"make + กรรม + กริยาช่องที่ 1 ไม่มี to"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns","th":"รูปแบบคำกริยา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the correct answer to complete the sentence.\nMy mother made me ______ my room before I could play games.","choices":["to tidy","tidy","tidying","tidied"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Make someone do something\" uses the base form without \"to\": \"made me tidy\". \"To tidy\", \"tidying\" and \"tidied\" are wrong after \"make + object\".","th":"make + กรรม + กริยาช่องที่ 1 ไม่มี to จึงเป็น made me tidy ส่วน to tidy, tidying และ tidied ใช้หลัง make + กรรม ไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Possessive adjective in context","th":"คำคุณศัพท์แสดงความเป็นเจ้าของในบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"School news cloze","th":"เติมคำในข่าวโรงเรียน"},"passage":"Our school held its first Plastic-Free Week last month. All students were asked to bring (1) ______ own water bottle instead of buying drinks in plastic cups. At first, many students found it difficult, (2) ______ by Wednesday almost everyone remembered. The canteen also (3) ______ plastic bags with banana leaves for wrapping snacks. The student council counted the rubbish every afternoon, and by the end of the week the amount of plastic waste had (4) ______ by nearly half. \"We were surprised at how easy it was (5) ______ small changes,\" said council president Natcha. The school now hopes to make Plastic-Free Week a monthly event, and teachers are asking students for ideas to (6) ______ waste at home, too.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage and choose the best answer for gap (1).","choices":["their","your","his","its"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The owner is \"All students\", which is plural, so we need \"their own water bottle\". \"Its\" refers to a thing, \"his\" to one male, and \"your\" to the person spoken to.","th":"เจ้าของคือ All students ซึ่งเป็นพหูพจน์ จึงใช้ their own water bottle ส่วน its ใช้กับสิ่งของ his ใช้กับผู้ชายคนเดียว และ your ใช้กับคนที่เราพูดด้วย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past participle after 'had'","th":"กริยาช่องที่ 3 หลัง had"},"topic":{"en":"School news cloze","th":"เติมคำในข่าวโรงเรียน"},"passage":"Our school held its first Plastic-Free Week last month. All students were asked to bring (1) ______ own water bottle instead of buying drinks in plastic cups. At first, many students found it difficult, (2) ______ by Wednesday almost everyone remembered. The canteen also (3) ______ plastic bags with banana leaves for wrapping snacks. The student council counted the rubbish every afternoon, and by the end of the week the amount of plastic waste had (4) ______ by nearly half. \"We were surprised at how easy it was (5) ______ small changes,\" said council president Natcha. The school now hopes to make Plastic-Free Week a monthly event, and teachers are asking students for ideas to (6) ______ waste at home, too.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage and choose the best answer for gap (4).","choices":["fallen","fell","falling","fall"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Had\" + past participle forms the past perfect: \"had fallen\". \"Fell\" is the past simple, and \"falling\" and \"fall\" cannot follow \"had\" here.","th":"had + กริยาช่องที่ 3 เป็น past perfect จึงใช้ had fallen ส่วน fell เป็นกริยาช่องที่ 2 และ falling กับ fall ใช้หลัง had ในประโยคนี้ไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main purpose","th":"การระบุจุดประสงค์หลัก"},"topic":{"en":"Notice / poster","th":"ประกาศ / โปสเตอร์"},"passage":"THONBURI PUBLIC LIBRARY — VOLUNTEER READERS WANTED\n\nDo you love stories? We are looking for students aged 11–15 to read aloud to young children at our Children's Book Fair, 14–16 March.\n\nWhat you will do:\n• Read picture books to groups of 4- to 6-year-olds for 20 minutes at a time\n• Help children choose books and fill in their \"My Reading Passport\"\n• Join one short training session on Saturday 7 March, 9:00–11:00\n\nWhat we need from you:\n• A clear reading voice in Thai or English (English readers are especially needed this year)\n• At least two three-hour shifts during the fair\n• A permission form signed by a parent or guardian\n\nWhat you will get:\n• A volunteer T-shirt and a free lunch on each shift\n• A certificate signed by the library director\n• A free book of your choice on your final day\n\nHow to apply: Fill in the online form on the library website by 28 February. Places are limited to 40 volunteers, and students who have not volunteered with us before will be chosen first.\nQuestions? Call Khun Areerat on 02-123-4567 (Tuesday–Saturday, 10:00–17:00).","table":null,"stem":"What is the main purpose of the notice?","choices":["To invite parents to a training session","To advertise books for sale at the fair","To ask young children to fill in a reading passport","To find students who will read stories to young children"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The heading \"Volunteer Readers Wanted\" and the first lines show the library wants students to read aloud to children. Selling books, inviting parents and asking children to fill in passports are not the purpose.","th":"หัวเรื่อง Volunteer Readers Wanted และประโยคแรกบอกว่าห้องสมุดต้องการนักเรียนมาอ่านนิทานให้เด็กเล็กฟัง ไม่ได้ขายหนังสือ เชิญผู้ปกครอง หรือให้เด็กกรอกสมุดสะสมการอ่าน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Understanding a character's reason","th":"การเข้าใจเหตุผลของตัวละคร"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative text","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"Krit had always believed that his grandfather's old radio was useless. It sat on a shelf in the living room, covered in dust, and nobody had turned it on for years. So when his class was given a project called \"Objects That Tell Stories\", Krit decided to bring something more modern — his new smartwatch.\n\nThat evening, however, his grandfather noticed him looking at the radio. \"That radio,\" he said quietly, \"is the reason I met your grandmother.\" Krit put down his watch. His grandfather explained that in 1975, while he was studying in Bangkok, he had written to a radio programme that played songs requested by listeners. A young woman in Chiang Mai heard her favourite song played \"for everyone who feels far from home\", and she wrote to the programme to find out who had asked for it. The presenter passed her letter on, and the two wrote to each other for two years before they finally met.\n\nThe next week, Krit carried the radio carefully to school. He did not win first prize, but when he finished his talk, his teacher asked whether she could invite his grandfather to tell the story to the whole school. For the first time, Krit realised that old things are sometimes valuable not for what they can do, but for what they remember.","table":null,"stem":"Why did Krit first plan to bring his smartwatch to school?","choices":["His teacher asked for modern objects.","The radio was too heavy to carry.","His grandfather would not lend him the radio.","He thought the old radio was useless."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The story begins by saying Krit believed the radio was useless, so he chose something modern. The teacher did not ask for modern objects, and weight or his grandfather refusing are never mentioned.","th":"เรื่องเริ่มว่ากฤษคิดว่าวิทยุเก่าไม่มีประโยชน์ จึงเลือกนำของทันสมัยไป ครูไม่ได้สั่งให้นำของทันสมัย และไม่มีการพูดถึงน้ำหนักวิทยุหรือคุณตาไม่ยอมให้ยืม"}}]}},{"exam":"sk1","set":2,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.1 English (GATE / EPLUS+) — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE / EPLUS+) — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"40 questions in 50 minutes: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, cloze and reading, in the style of the Suankularb M.1 pre-test. GATE and EPLUS+ applicants take the same paper; EPLUS+ needs a higher score. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"40 ข้อ 50 นาที: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ตามแนวข้อสอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EPLUS+ ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน EPLUS+ ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":50,"kinds":{"m1":40},"subjects":{"eng":40},"free":false,"file":"sk1-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to a gift","th":"การตอบเมื่อได้รับของขวัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Happy birthday, Mew! This is for you.\nB: ______","choices":["Here you are.","Same to you.","Oh, thank you! You shouldn't have.","Many happy returns to you."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"When you receive a present you thank the giver; \"You shouldn't have\" politely means the gift is very kind. \"Many happy returns\" is a birthday wish for someone else, \"Same to you\" returns a wish, and \"Here you are\" is said when giving something.","th":"เมื่อได้รับของขวัญควรขอบคุณ และ You shouldn't have เป็นสำนวนสุภาพ แปลว่า ไม่น่าลำบากเลย ส่วน Many happy returns เป็นคำอวยพรวันเกิดให้ผู้อื่น Same to you ใช้อวยพรกลับ และ Here you are ใช้ตอนยื่นของให้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Refusing an offer of food","th":"การปฏิเสธเมื่อมีคนเสนออาหาร"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Would you like some more som tam?\nB: ______ I'm really full.","choices":["Help yourself.","I'd like to.","No, thanks.","Yes, please."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"I'm really full\" shows B is refusing, so \"No, thanks\" is right. \"Yes, please\" accepts, \"Help yourself\" is what the host says, and \"I'd like to\" accepts an invitation.","th":"I'm really full (อิ่มมากแล้ว) แสดงว่าปฏิเสธ จึงตอบ No, thanks ส่วน Yes, please เป็นการตอบรับ Help yourself เป็นคำพูดของเจ้าของบ้าน และ I'd like to ใช้ตอบรับคำชวน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Everyday reading materials","th":"สิ่งพิมพ์ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"topic":{"en":"Family and home","th":"ครอบครัวและบ้าน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\nMy father reads the ______ every morning to find out what is happening in the world.","choices":["timetable","newspaper","menu","dictionary"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"We read a newspaper to find out the news. A menu lists food, a dictionary explains words, and a timetable gives times.","th":"อ่าน newspaper (หนังสือพิมพ์) เพื่อรู้ข่าวสาร ส่วน menu คือรายการอาหาร dictionary คือพจนานุกรม และ timetable คือตารางเวลา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Event vocabulary","th":"คำศัพท์เกี่ยวกับงานกิจกรรม"},"topic":{"en":"Festivals and events","th":"เทศกาลและงานกิจกรรม"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\nThe Loy Krathong festival at the park was ______ because of the heavy storm, so nobody could go.","choices":["cancelled","attended","decorated","celebrated"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Nobody could go\" shows the event did not happen, so it was \"cancelled\". \"Celebrated\", \"attended\" and \"decorated\" all mean the event took place.","th":"nobody could go แสดงว่างานไม่ได้จัด จึงใช้ cancelled (ยกเลิก) ส่วน celebrated, attended และ decorated ล้วนหมายความว่างานได้จัดขึ้น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Present simple, third person","th":"Present simple กับประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3"},"topic":{"en":"Present simple","th":"Present simple"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the correct answer to complete the sentence.\nMy cousin ______ to school by bus every day.","choices":["is go","go","going","goes"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Every day\" shows a habit, so we use the present simple, and \"my cousin\" (he/she) needs \"-es\": \"goes\".","th":"every day แสดงกิจวัตร จึงใช้ present simple และประธาน my cousin เป็นเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3 จึงเติม -es เป็น goes"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Wish + past simple","th":"Wish + past simple"},"topic":{"en":"Wishes","th":"การแสดงความปรารถนา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the correct answer to complete the sentence.\nI wish I ______ how to play the khim like my cousin.","choices":["will know","have known","knew","know"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"For a wish about the present, we use \"wish\" + past simple: \"I wish I knew\". The present, future and present perfect forms are not used after \"wish\" in this meaning.","th":"การปรารถนาสิ่งที่ไม่จริงในปัจจุบันใช้ wish + past simple จึงเป็น I wish I knew ส่วน know, will know และ have known ไม่ใช้หลัง wish ในความหมายนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Vocabulary in context: restaurants","th":"คำศัพท์ในบริบท: ร้านอาหาร"},"topic":{"en":"Story cloze","th":"เติมคำในเรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"Every Saturday morning, Fah helps at her aunt's noodle shop near Bang Lamphu. Last week, something unusual happened. An elderly tourist came in and (1) ______ a bowl of boat noodles. When he tried to pay, he could not find his wallet, (2) ______ he searched every pocket. He looked very worried. Fah remembered that she (3) ______ a man drop something near the bus stop earlier that morning. She ran outside and found a brown wallet lying on the pavement (4) ______ two parked motorcycles. When she gave it back, the man was so (5) ______ that he wanted to give her a reward. Fah politely refused. \"My aunt always says that honesty is its own reward,\" she explained. The next Saturday, the man came back with his whole family, and the shop was busier than it had been for months. Her aunt joked that Fah's honesty had (6) ______ off after all.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage and choose the best answer for gap (1).","choices":["ordered","served","booked","cooked"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A customer who comes into a shop \"orders\" food. The shop cooks and serves it, and you book a table, not a bowl of noodles.","th":"ลูกค้าที่เข้ามาในร้านจะ ordered (สั่ง) อาหาร ส่วน cooked และ served เป็นสิ่งที่ร้านทำ และ booked ใช้กับการจองโต๊ะ ไม่ใช่ก๋วยเตี๋ยว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Prepositions of place: between / among","th":"บุพบทบอกตำแหน่ง: between / among"},"topic":{"en":"Story cloze","th":"เติมคำในเรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"Every Saturday morning, Fah helps at her aunt's noodle shop near Bang Lamphu. Last week, something unusual happened. An elderly tourist came in and (1) ______ a bowl of boat noodles. When he tried to pay, he could not find his wallet, (2) ______ he searched every pocket. He looked very worried. Fah remembered that she (3) ______ a man drop something near the bus stop earlier that morning. She ran outside and found a brown wallet lying on the pavement (4) ______ two parked motorcycles. When she gave it back, the man was so (5) ______ that he wanted to give her a reward. Fah politely refused. \"My aunt always says that honesty is its own reward,\" she explained. The next Saturday, the man came back with his whole family, and the shop was busier than it had been for months. Her aunt joked that Fah's honesty had (6) ______ off after all.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage and choose the best answer for gap (4).","choices":["among","across","between","along"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"With two separate things (two motorcycles), we use \"between\". \"Among\" is for a group of three or more, and \"along\" and \"across\" describe lines or movement, not a position between two objects.","th":"เมื่อมีของสองชิ้น (มอเตอร์ไซค์สองคัน) ใช้ between ส่วน among ใช้กับกลุ่มตั้งแต่สามขึ้นไป และ along กับ across ใช้กับแนวยาวหรือการเคลื่อนที่ข้าม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Scanning for details","th":"การอ่านหารายละเอียด"},"topic":{"en":"Notice / timetable","th":"ประกาศ / ตารางเวลา"},"passage":"SUANLUANG SCHOOL SPORTS DAY — Information for M.1 Students\n\nSports Day will be held on Friday 20 November at the school stadium. Please read the following information carefully.\n\nHouses: Every student belongs to one of four colour houses: Red, Yellow, Green or Blue. Check your house on the noticeboard outside Room 104. Wear a T-shirt in your house colour, sports shorts and running shoes. Jeans are not allowed.\n\nTimetable\n7:30 Arrive at the stadium and sit with your house\n8:00 Opening parade (each house marches with its flag)\n9:00–12:00 Track events (100 m, 400 m, relay)\n12:00–13:00 Lunch break — food stalls run by the Parents' Association\n13:00–15:00 Team games: tug-of-war, football, sack race\n15:30 Prize-giving and closing ceremony\n\nImportant notes\n• Students taking part in track events must register with Kru Somsak by Friday 13 November. Late registrations will not be accepted.\n• Bring a refillable water bottle. Free drinking water is available at four stations around the stadium.\n• If it rains, the morning events will move to the indoor gym, and the afternoon team games will be postponed to the following Monday.\n• Students may leave only after the closing ceremony, unless a parent collects them in person and signs them out at the main gate.","table":null,"stem":"Where can students find out which colour house they are in?","choices":["On the noticeboard outside Room 104","In the indoor gym","At the main gate","From Kru Somsak"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The notice says \"Check your house on the noticeboard outside Room 104\". Kru Somsak handles track registration, the main gate is for signing out, and the gym is for rain.","th":"ประกาศบอกให้ดูสีของตนเองที่บอร์ดหน้าห้อง 104 ส่วนครูสมศักดิ์รับลงชื่อแข่งกรีฑา ประตูหน้าใช้สำหรับเซ็นรับกลับ และโรงยิมใช้เมื่อฝนตก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Finding stated details","th":"การหารายละเอียดที่ระบุในบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Informational text","th":"บทความให้ความรู้"},"passage":"Along the coast of the Gulf of Thailand, a strange forest grows with its feet in the sea. These are mangroves, trees that can live in salty water where most plants would die. Their roots grow above the mud like long, bent fingers, helping the trees breathe and stay upright as the tide comes in and goes out twice a day.\n\nMangroves are much more than unusual trees. Their thick roots slow down waves, so they protect coastal villages from storms. When the 2004 tsunami hit southern Thailand, some communities behind healthy mangroves suffered less damage than those on open beaches. The roots are also a \"nursery\" for young fish, crabs and shrimps, which hide there from larger animals until they are big enough to survive in open water. Many fishing families depend on this.\n\nSadly, Thailand lost a large part of its mangroves in the second half of the twentieth century, mostly to make room for shrimp farms and buildings. Today, however, the situation is improving. Schools, villagers and companies plant thousands of young mangrove trees every year, and in some provinces the forest is slowly spreading again. Planting alone is not enough, though: young trees must be planted in the right places and protected for several years, or most of them will not survive.","table":null,"stem":"According to the passage, how do the roots help the mangrove trees themselves?","choices":["They provide food for fishing families.","They remove salt from seawater for people.","They stop the tide from coming in.","They help the trees breathe and stay upright."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 1 says the roots help \"the trees breathe and stay upright\". The roots slow waves but do not stop the tide, and removing salt for people or feeding families is not what the roots do for the trees.","th":"ย่อหน้าแรกบอกว่ารากช่วยให้ต้นไม้หายใจและตั้งตรงอยู่ได้ รากชะลอคลื่นแต่ไม่ได้หยุดน้ำขึ้นน้ำลง และไม่ได้กรองเกลือให้คนหรือเป็นอาหารให้ชาวประมง"}}]}},{"exam":"sk1","set":3,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.1 English (GATE / EPLUS+) — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE / EPLUS+) — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"40 questions in 50 minutes: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, cloze and reading, in the style of the Suankularb M.1 pre-test. GATE and EPLUS+ applicants take the same paper; EPLUS+ needs a higher score. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"40 ข้อ 50 นาที: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ตามแนวข้อสอบเข้า ม.1 สวนกุหลาบ ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EPLUS+ ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน EPLUS+ ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":50,"kinds":{"m1":40},"subjects":{"eng":40},"free":false,"file":"sk1-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Answering a question about a seat","th":"การตอบคำถามเรื่องที่นั่ง"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Excuse me, is anyone sitting here?\nB: ______ Please, sit down.","choices":["No, it's free.","Yes, it's free.","I'm sitting.","Yes, please."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Is anyone sitting here?\" is answered \"No\" when the seat is empty, which matches \"Please, sit down\". \"Yes, it's free\" contradicts itself, \"Yes, please\" accepts an offer, and \"I'm sitting\" does not answer the question.","th":"Is anyone sitting here? ถ้าไม่มีคนนั่งต้องตอบ No, it's free ซึ่งเข้ากับ Please, sit down ส่วน Yes, it's free ขัดแย้งในตัวเอง Yes, please ใช้ตอบรับข้อเสนอ และ I'm sitting ไม่ได้ตอบคำถาม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Expressing doubt","th":"การแสดงความไม่แน่ใจ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Do you think it'll rain during our school trip to Kanchanaburi?\nB: ______ The forecast says it'll be sunny all week.","choices":["I doubt it.","Of course it will.","I hope so.","I'm afraid so."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The sunny forecast shows B thinks rain is unlikely, so \"I doubt it\" fits. \"I'm afraid so\" and \"Of course it will\" say it will rain, and \"I hope so\" says B wants rain — neither matches the forecast.","th":"พยากรณ์บอกว่าแดดออกทั้งสัปดาห์ แสดงว่า B คิดว่าฝนไม่น่าจะตก จึงตอบ I doubt it ส่วน I'm afraid so และ Of course it will บอกว่าฝนจะตก และ I hope so แปลว่า อยากให้ฝนตก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"School activity vocabulary","th":"คำศัพท์กิจกรรมโรงเรียน"},"topic":{"en":"School life","th":"ชีวิตในโรงเรียน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\nOur class went on a ______ to the National Science Museum last Friday.","choices":["field trip","flight","holiday","voyage"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A class visit to a museum is a \"field trip\". A holiday is time off, a flight is a journey by plane, and a voyage is a long journey by sea.","th":"การพานักเรียนไปพิพิธภัณฑ์คือ field trip (ทัศนศึกษา) ส่วน holiday คือวันหยุด flight คือเที่ยวบิน และ voyage คือการเดินทางทางทะเลระยะไกล"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs with 'put'","th":"กริยาวลีที่ใช้ put"},"topic":{"en":"Sport","th":"กีฬา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\nThe heavy rain forced the organisers to ______ the football match, so we played it the next day instead.","choices":["put on","put out","put up","put off"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Put off\" means postpone, which matches playing the match the next day. \"Put on\" means wear or organise a show, \"put up\" means build or raise, and \"put out\" means extinguish.","th":"put off แปลว่า เลื่อนออกไป เข้ากับการไปแข่งวันรุ่งขึ้น ส่วน put on แปลว่า สวมใส่หรือจัดการแสดง put up แปลว่า ติดตั้งหรือยกขึ้น และ put out แปลว่า ดับ (ไฟ)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Past simple with 'last night'","th":"Past simple กับ last night"},"topic":{"en":"Past simple","th":"Past simple"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the correct answer to complete the sentence.\nPim and her friends ______ to the concert at Impact Arena last night.","choices":["goes","went","go","have gone"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Last night\" is a finished time in the past, so we use the past simple \"went\". The present perfect \"have gone\" cannot be used with a finished time.","th":"last night เป็นเวลาในอดีตที่จบแล้ว จึงใช้ past simple คือ went ส่วน have gone เป็น present perfect ใช้กับเวลาที่จบแล้วไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement: 'the number of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องของกริยากับ the number of"},"topic":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานและกริยา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the correct answer to complete the sentence.\nThe number of students who take the bus to our school ______ increased a lot this year.","choices":["were","are","have","has"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"The number of …\" is singular (one number), so we use \"has increased\". \"A number of students\" would be plural, which is the trap here.","th":"the number of … เป็นเอกพจน์ (จำนวนหนึ่งค่า) จึงใช้ has increased ส่วน a number of students จึงจะเป็นพหูพจน์ ซึ่งเป็นกับดักในประโยคนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Prepositions of time: until / by","th":"บุพบทบอกเวลา: until / by"},"topic":{"en":"School news cloze","th":"เติมคำในข่าวโรงเรียน"},"passage":"When the M.1 students of a Bangkok school started a \"Phone-Free Lunch\" experiment last term, few people expected it to last. The rule was simple: phones stayed in a basket at the canteen door (1) ______ the end of the lunch break. During the first week, many students complained that they felt bored and did not know what to (2) ______ about. By the third week, however, something had changed. Groups that (3) ______ never spoken to each other were playing chess, sharing snacks and even planning a small concert. Teachers noticed that fewer students arrived at afternoon classes looking tired, (4) ______ they had not spent the whole break staring at screens. In a survey at the end of term, 78% of the students said they wanted the experiment to continue. \"I didn't think I could (5) ______ without my phone for an hour,\" admitted one student, \"but now I actually look forward to lunch.\" The school has decided to keep the rule, and the student council is now (6) ______ other schools to try it, too.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage and choose the best answer for gap (1).","choices":["until","since","during","by"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The phones stayed in the basket all the time up to the end of the break, so \"until\" fits. \"By\" means \"not later than\" and does not go with \"stayed\", \"since\" looks back from now, and \"during\" means within a period.","th":"โทรศัพท์อยู่ในตะกร้าต่อเนื่องจนถึงหมดเวลาพัก จึงใช้ until ส่วน by แปลว่า ภายใน (ไม่เกิน) ไม่ใช้กับ stayed since ใช้นับจากอดีตถึงปัจจุบัน และ during แปลว่า ในระหว่าง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Linking a result to a possible reason","th":"การเชื่อมผลกับเหตุผลที่เป็นไปได้"},"topic":{"en":"School news cloze","th":"เติมคำในข่าวโรงเรียน"},"passage":"When the M.1 students of a Bangkok school started a \"Phone-Free Lunch\" experiment last term, few people expected it to last. The rule was simple: phones stayed in a basket at the canteen door (1) ______ the end of the lunch break. During the first week, many students complained that they felt bored and did not know what to (2) ______ about. By the third week, however, something had changed. Groups that (3) ______ never spoken to each other were playing chess, sharing snacks and even planning a small concert. Teachers noticed that fewer students arrived at afternoon classes looking tired, (4) ______ they had not spent the whole break staring at screens. In a survey at the end of term, 78% of the students said they wanted the experiment to continue. \"I didn't think I could (5) ______ without my phone for an hour,\" admitted one student, \"but now I actually look forward to lunch.\" The school has decided to keep the rule, and the student council is now (6) ______ other schools to try it, too.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage and choose the best answer for gap (4).","choices":["although","unless","so that","perhaps because"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Not staring at screens is a likely reason why fewer students looked tired, so \"perhaps because\" fits. \"Although\" shows contrast, \"unless\" means \"if not\", and \"so that\" shows purpose.","th":"การไม่จ้องจอเป็นเหตุผลที่เป็นไปได้ที่ทำให้นักเรียนดูเหนื่อยน้อยลง จึงใช้ perhaps because ส่วน although แสดงความขัดแย้ง unless แปลว่า ถ้าไม่ และ so that แสดงจุดประสงค์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Scanning for details","th":"การอ่านหารายละเอียด"},"topic":{"en":"Notice / poster","th":"ประกาศ / โปสเตอร์"},"passage":"YOUNG WRITERS' AWARD 2027\nTheme: \"A Place That Changed Me\"\nOpen to students from Prathom 5 to Mathayom 2 living in Thailand\n\nHave you ever visited a place — a market, a temple, a beach, even your grandmother's kitchen — that made you see the world differently? Write about it, and you could see your story in print!\n\nRules\n• Stories must be written in English and be between 400 and 600 words. Entries outside this limit will not be read.\n• The story must be your own work. Teachers and parents may give advice, but they must not write or correct any part of it.\n• Do not write your name or school anywhere in the story itself; put these details only on the entry form. This helps the judges stay fair.\n• Each student may send only one entry.\n\nPrizes\nThe two age groups (P.5–P.6 and M.1–M.2) will be judged separately. In each group:\n1st prize: 5,000 baht and a set of books for the winner's school library\n2nd prize: 3,000 baht\nTen Highly Commended writers will receive certificates.\nAll prize-winning and Highly Commended stories will appear in a printed collection, launched at a book fair in Bangkok in April.\n\nDeadline\nEntries must arrive by 31 January. Send your story and completed entry form by email. Results will be posted on our website on 15 March, and winners will also be contacted directly.","table":null,"stem":"Who can enter the competition?","choices":["Any writer who lives in Bangkok","Only students in M.1 and M.2","Students from P.5 to M.2 living in Thailand","Teachers and parents of young writers"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The poster says it is \"open to students from Prathom 5 to Mathayom 2 living in Thailand\". It is not limited to M.1–M.2, adults cannot enter, and it is not only for Bangkok.","th":"โปสเตอร์ระบุว่าเปิดรับนักเรียน ป.5 ถึง ม.2 ที่อาศัยในประเทศไทย ไม่ได้จำกัดเฉพาะ ม.1–ม.2 ผู้ใหญ่สมัครไม่ได้ และไม่ได้จำกัดเฉพาะกรุงเทพฯ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Understanding other characters' views","th":"การเข้าใจมุมมองของตัวละครอื่น"},"topic":{"en":"Narrative text","th":"เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"In 2019, eleven-year-old Pailin started doing something her classmates found rather strange. Every morning before school, she sat on the balcony of her family's flat in Bangkok for fifteen minutes and wrote down every bird she saw or heard. Most days the list was short: mynas, sparrows, a few pigeons. Her friends could not understand why anyone would bother.\n\nWhat they did not know was that Pailin was sending her lists to an online project run by scientists, who collect millions of records like hers from ordinary people around the world. One person's notes may seem unimportant, but when thousands of people record what they see in the same way, year after year, the results can reveal changes that no small team of scientists could ever spot on its own.\n\nFour years later, a university researcher contacted her. Pailin's records, it turned out, were among the very few long-term lists from her district, and they showed that a small green bird called the coppersmith barbet had become noticeably more common there after a nearby park was planted with fig trees. The researcher asked permission to include her data in a scientific paper. \"I never imagined that sitting on my balcony counted as science,\" Pailin said. \"Now I tell everyone: you don't need a laboratory to make a discovery — you just need patience.\"","table":null,"stem":"Why did Pailin's classmates find her habit strange?","choices":["They thought she was too young to use the internet.","They could not see the point of recording common birds every day.","They thought she should be studying instead.","They did not believe there were birds in Bangkok."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The lists showed only common birds, and \"her friends could not understand why anyone would bother\". The passage says nothing about her age online, doubting that birds exist, or studying.","th":"รายชื่อมีแต่นกธรรมดา และเพื่อนไม่เข้าใจว่าจะทำไปทำไม บทความไม่ได้พูดถึงอายุกับการใช้อินเทอร์เน็ต การไม่เชื่อว่ามีนก หรือการที่ควรไปอ่านหนังสือ"}}]}},{"exam":"sp1","set":1,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.1 English — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes, reading-centred with reasoning (tables, notices, emails, stories, informational and opinion texts), plus conversation, vocabulary and grammar. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที เน้นการอ่านและการคิดวิเคราะห์ (ตาราง ประกาศ อีเมล เรื่องสั้น บทความความรู้ และบทแสดงความคิดเห็น) พร้อมบทสนทนา คำศัพท์ และไวยากรณ์ — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":true,"file":"sp1-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a suitable response","th":"การเลือกคำตอบที่เหมาะสม"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the best response.\nA: Could you keep an eye on my bag while I buy a drink?\nB: ______","choices":["Yes, I can see it clearly.","No, it isn't my bag.","I'd like a drink too, thanks.","Sure, take your time."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Keep an eye on' means 'watch and look after', so agreeing with 'Sure, take your time' fits. 'I can see it clearly' takes the phrase literally; the other replies do not answer the request.","th":"'keep an eye on' แปลว่า ช่วยดูแลให้ จึงตอบรับด้วย 'Sure, take your time.' ส่วน 'I can see it clearly' แปลสำนวนตามตัวอักษร และคำตอบอื่นไม่ได้ตอบคำขอ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing appropriate register (politeness)","th":"การเลือกระดับภาษาที่เหมาะสมกับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Polite and formal language","th":"ภาษาสุภาพและเป็นทางการ"},"passage":"","stem":"You want to ask the school director for permission to put up a poster about your club. What is the most appropriate thing to say?","choices":["Excuse me, sir. Would it be all right if I put up a poster for our club?","Give me permission for a poster, please.","You must let me put up a poster for our club.","Hey, I'm putting up a poster for our club, OK?"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"When speaking to a senior adult, a polite indirect question such as 'Would it be all right if I…?' is best. The other sentences sound too casual, too direct, or like an order.","th":"เมื่อพูดกับผู้ใหญ่ที่มีตำแหน่งสูง ควรใช้คำถามอ้อมที่สุภาพ เช่น 'Would it be all right if I…?' ประโยคอื่นฟังเป็นกันเองเกินไป ห้วนเกินไป หรือเหมือนออกคำสั่ง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a suitable response","th":"การเลือกคำตอบที่เหมาะสม"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Excuse me, is this seat taken?\nB: ______","choices":["Yes, please sit down.","I'm afraid it is. My friend has just gone to get some food.","It's taken me ten minutes to get here.","No, I'm not taking it anywhere."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'Is this seat taken?' asks whether someone is using the seat. 'I'm afraid it is' politely says yes and gives a reason. 'Yes, please sit down' contradicts itself, and the other two misunderstand 'taken'.","th":"'Is this seat taken?' ถามว่ามีคนจองที่นั่งนี้หรือยัง 'I'm afraid it is' คือตอบอย่างสุภาพว่ามีคนนั่งแล้วพร้อมเหตุผล 'Yes, please sit down' ขัดแย้งในตัวเอง ส่วนอีกสองคำตอบเข้าใจคำว่า taken ผิดความหมาย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Adjectives","th":"คำคุณศัพท์"},"passage":"","stem":"The film was so ______ that half the audience fell asleep.","choices":["frightening","dull","thrilling","popular"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"People fall asleep when something is boring, so 'dull' is correct. A thrilling or frightening film would keep them awake.","th":"คนดูหลับเพราะหนังน่าเบื่อ จึงใช้ 'dull' ส่วนหนังที่ตื่นเต้นหรือน่ากลัวจะทำให้คนดูตื่นตัว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Adjectives","th":"คำคุณศัพท์"},"passage":"","stem":"Pim never buys things she doesn't need, and she always compares prices first. She is very ______ with money.","choices":["jealous","careless","sensible","generous"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Comparing prices and avoiding unnecessary buying shows good judgement, so she is 'sensible' with money. 'Generous' means happy to give money away; 'careless' is the opposite of the description.","th":"การเปรียบเทียบราคาและไม่ซื้อของไม่จำเป็นแสดงว่าใช้เงินอย่างมีเหตุผล คือ 'sensible' ส่วน 'generous' คือใจกว้างชอบให้ และ 'careless' มีความหมายตรงข้าม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Verbs","th":"คำกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Because of the storm, we had to ______ the picnic until next Saturday.","choices":["postpone","cancel","prevent","arrange"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Until next Saturday' shows the picnic will still happen later, so 'postpone' (move to a later time) is correct. 'Cancel' means it will not happen at all.","th":"'until next Saturday' แสดงว่ายังจัดปิกนิกอยู่แต่เลื่อนออกไป จึงใช้ 'postpone' ส่วน 'cancel' คือยกเลิกไปเลย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals (first conditional)","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 1"},"passage":"","stem":"If it ______ tomorrow, we will stay at home and play board games.","choices":["rained","would rain","rains","will rain"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The first conditional uses the present simple in the if-clause and 'will' in the main clause: 'If it rains…, we will…'.","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 1 ใช้ present simple ในส่วน if และใช้ will ในประโยคหลัก จึงเป็น 'If it rains'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Past perfect","th":"Past perfect tense"},"passage":"","stem":"By the time we got to the cinema, the film ______, so we missed the beginning.","choices":["is already starting","has already started","already starts","had already started"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Two past actions: the film started before we arrived. The earlier action takes the past perfect, 'had already started'.","th":"มีเหตุการณ์ในอดีตสองเหตุการณ์ หนังเริ่มก่อนเราไปถึง เหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนใช้ past perfect คือ 'had already started'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Inversion after negative adverbs","th":"การสลับที่หลังคำวิเศษณ์ปฏิเสธ"},"passage":"","stem":"Hardly ______ the classroom when the bell rang.","choices":["did she entered","she had entered","she entered","had she entered"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"When a sentence begins with 'Hardly', the auxiliary comes before the subject, and the earlier action uses the past perfect: 'Hardly had she entered…when…'.","th":"เมื่อขึ้นต้นประโยคด้วย Hardly ต้องสลับกริยาช่วยไว้หน้าประธาน และเหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนใช้ past perfect จึงเป็น 'had she entered'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a new situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์ใหม่"},"topic":{"en":"Table / timetable","th":"ตาราง"},"passage":"Read the table and the notes, then answer the questions.\n\nNotes:\n• All courses run for four weeks, starting on 1 April.\n• Members of the centre get 20% off every course.\n• If you join two courses, you pay 300 baht less for the cheaper one.\n• Rock climbing: you must bring a letter of permission signed by a parent.","table":{"title":"Riverside Sports Centre — April holiday courses","rows":[["Course","Ages","Days","Time","Fee (baht)"],["Swimming (beginners)","8–12","Mon, Wed","9:00–10:30","1,200"],["Badminton","10–15","Tue, Thu","9:00–11:00","1,500"],["Rock climbing","12–16","Sat","13:00–16:00","2,000"],["Table tennis","9–14","Mon, Wed, Fri","14:00–15:30","1,000"],["Junior yoga","7–11","Tue, Thu","15:00–16:00","900"]]},"stem":"Which student can join the rock climbing course?","choices":["Kan, 13, who is free on Saturday afternoons and has a letter signed by his mother","Ton, 14, who is free only on Saturday mornings and has a letter from his parents","Mai, 15, who is free on Saturday afternoons but whose parents have not agreed yet","Ploy, 11, who is free every Saturday and has a letter from her father"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Rock climbing needs three things: age 12–16, free on Saturday 13:00–16:00, and a signed parent letter. Only Kan meets all three. Ploy is too young, Ton is busy in the afternoon, and Mai has no permission.","th":"ปีนผาต้องครบสามเงื่อนไข: อายุ 12–16 ปี ว่างวันเสาร์ 13:00–16:00 และมีจดหมายอนุญาตจากผู้ปกครอง มีเพียง Kan ที่ครบ Ploy อายุน้อยไป Ton ว่างแค่ตอนเช้า และ Mai ยังไม่ได้รับอนุญาต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมาน (อ่านระหว่างบรรทัด)"},"topic":{"en":"Emails and messages","th":"อีเมลและข้อความ"},"passage":"From: Ben\nTo: Aunt Sue\nSubject: Next weekend\n\nHi Aunt Sue,\nMum says I can stay with you from Friday to Sunday next weekend. My train gets to Hua Hin at 4:15 p.m. on Friday. Could you meet me at the station? If you're busy, I can take a taxi — Mum has given me money for one.\nAlso, is it OK if I bring my friend Leo? His family is staying at a hotel near your house, and he'd love to see your turtles.\nBen\n\nFrom: Aunt Sue\nTo: Ben\nSubject: Re: Next weekend\n\nDear Ben,\nHow wonderful! Friday is fine, but I have a dentist appointment at four o'clock, so I may be about half an hour late. Please wait at the café opposite the ticket office instead of taking a taxi — some drivers at the station charge visitors far too much.\nOf course Leo is welcome, but the turtles won't be here for long. On Saturday morning I'm taking them back to the conservation centre, where they will be released into the sea. Perhaps you'd both like to come and watch? We'll need to leave at 6 a.m.!\nLove,\nAunt Sue","stem":"At about what time will Aunt Sue probably arrive at the station?","choices":["5:15 p.m.","4:15 p.m.","4:00 p.m.","4:45 p.m."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Ben's train arrives at 4:15 and Aunt Sue says she may be 'about half an hour late', so she will probably arrive at about 4:45. Four o'clock is the time of her dentist appointment.","th":"รถไฟของเบนถึง 4:15 น. และป้าซูบอกว่าอาจมาช้าประมาณครึ่งชั่วโมง จึงน่าจะมาถึงราว 4:45 น. ส่วน 4 โมงเย็นเป็นเวลานัดหมอฟัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing the best title","th":"การเลือกชื่อเรื่องที่เหมาะสมที่สุด"},"topic":{"en":"Short story","th":"เรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"The Missing Medal\n\nFor weeks Arthit had trained for the school's five-kilometre race. On the day, he ran as fast as he could and finished second, just behind his best friend, Krit. At the prize-giving, Krit received a shiny gold medal. Arthit clapped with everyone else, but inside he felt a little jealous.\n\nThat evening, Arthit's phone buzzed. It was a message from Krit: 'Check your bedroom door.' Arthit rushed upstairs. His little sister, Ploy, was giggling in the hallway. On his door hung the gold medal with a note:\n\n'You stopped to help me when I fell at the third kilometre. Without you, I wouldn't have finished at all. This medal belongs to both of us. I'll come and collect it next month — your turn first!'\n\nArthit smiled. He had almost forgotten that he had stopped. Krit had not forgotten at all.","stem":"Which would be the best new title for the story?","choices":["A Jealous Friend","The Fastest Boy in School","How to Train for a Race","A Medal for Two"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The twist is that the winner shares his medal with the friend who helped him, so 'A Medal for Two' covers the whole story. The other titles fit only a small part or none of it.","th":"จุดหักมุมคือผู้ชนะแบ่งเหรียญให้เพื่อนที่ช่วยเขา ชื่อ 'A Medal for Two' จึงครอบคลุมทั้งเรื่อง ชื่ออื่นตรงกับเพียงบางส่วนหรือไม่ตรงเลย"}}]}},{"exam":"sp1","set":2,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.1 English — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes, reading-centred with reasoning (tables, notices, emails, stories, informational and opinion texts), plus conversation, vocabulary and grammar. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที เน้นการอ่านและการคิดวิเคราะห์ (ตาราง ประกาศ อีเมล เรื่องสั้น บทความความรู้ และบทแสดงความคิดเห็น) พร้อมบทสนทนา คำศัพท์ และไวยากรณ์ — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"sp1-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a suitable response","th":"การเลือกคำตอบที่เหมาะสม"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Would you mind closing the window?\nB: ______ It's getting a bit cold, isn't it?","choices":["Not at all.","No, you can't.","Never mind.","Yes, I would."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Would you mind…?' literally asks whether something bothers you, so the polite way to agree is 'Not at all.' 'Yes, I would' means B refuses, which does not match B's friendly comment about the cold.","th":"'Would you mind…?' แปลตรงตัวว่า รังเกียจไหม การตอบตกลงจึงใช้ 'Not at all.' ถ้าตอบ 'Yes, I would' แปลว่าไม่ยอมทำ ซึ่งขัดกับที่ B พูดต่อว่าอากาศเริ่มหนาว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing appropriate register (politeness)","th":"การเลือกระดับภาษาที่เหมาะสมกับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Polite and formal language","th":"ภาษาสุภาพและเป็นทางการ"},"passage":"","stem":"You are writing an email to a teacher you have never met to ask for more time to hand in a report. Which sentence is most appropriate?","choices":["I am writing to ask whether I could hand in my report on Monday instead of Friday, as I have been unwell.","Can I send my report late? Thanks!!","I need more time, so I'll send my report on Monday.","You should give me until Monday because I was sick."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A formal request to an unknown teacher should be indirect and give a reason: 'I am writing to ask whether I could…'. The others are too casual, too direct, or sound like a demand.","th":"การขอร้องครูที่ไม่รู้จักผ่านอีเมลควรใช้ภาษาทางการ ถามอ้อม ๆ และให้เหตุผล คือ 'I am writing to ask whether I could…' ส่วนประโยคอื่นเป็นกันเองเกินไป ห้วน หรือเหมือนเรียกร้อง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a suitable response","th":"การเลือกคำตอบที่เหมาะสม"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I'm thinking of joining the drama club this term.\nB: ______ You were brilliant in the class play last year.","choices":["Rather you than me.","It's up to me.","Go for it!","Never mind."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B praises A's acting, so B is encouraging A: 'Go for it!' 'Rather you than me' means B would not want to do it, which does not match the praise.","th":"B ชมว่า A เล่นละครเก่ง จึงเป็นการให้กำลังใจ 'Go for it!' (ลุยเลย) ส่วน 'Rather you than me' แปลว่า ฉันไม่อยากทำหรอก ไม่เข้ากับคำชม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Adjectives","th":"คำคุณศัพท์"},"passage":"","stem":"My grandmother is ______ to cats, so she sneezes whenever she visits us.","choices":["afraid","similar","kind","allergic"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Sneezing near cats is a sign of an allergy: 'allergic to cats'. 'Afraid' takes 'of', not 'to'.","th":"การจามเมื่ออยู่ใกล้แมวเป็นอาการแพ้ จึงใช้ 'allergic to' ส่วน afraid ต้องใช้กับ of"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Adjectives","th":"คำคุณศัพท์"},"passage":"","stem":"Mana is a ______ reader; he finishes at least three novels every week.","choices":["lazy","slow","keen","careless"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Finishing three novels a week shows he loves reading: a 'keen' reader.","th":"อ่านนิยายจบสัปดาห์ละสามเล่มแสดงว่าชอบอ่านมาก จึงใช้ 'keen' (กระตือรือร้น ชื่นชอบ)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Verbs","th":"คำกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Instead of throwing her old jeans away, Fah ______ them into bags and cushions.","choices":["throws","wears","buys","transforms"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Instead of throwing them away' plus 'into bags and cushions' shows a change of form: she 'transforms' them.","th":"'แทนที่จะทิ้ง' และ 'into bags and cushions' แสดงการแปลงรูป จึงใช้ 'transforms'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Quantifiers","th":"คำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"There isn't ______ milk left. Can you buy some on your way home?","choices":["many","a few","few","much"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Milk' is uncountable, so in a negative sentence we use 'much'. 'Many', 'a few' and 'few' go with countable nouns.","th":"milk เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ ประโยคปฏิเสธจึงใช้ 'much' ส่วน many, a few และ few ใช้กับนามนับได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals (second conditional)","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 2"},"passage":"","stem":"If I ______ you, I would say sorry to her before the weekend.","choices":["had","will be","am","were"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Giving advice with an imaginary situation uses the second conditional: 'If I were you, I would…'.","th":"การให้คำแนะนำโดยสมมติว่าเป็นอีกฝ่ายใช้เงื่อนไขแบบที่ 2 คือ 'If I were you, I would…'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Verb + -ing / to-infinitive","th":"กริยาที่ตามด้วย V-ing หรือ to + V"},"passage":"","stem":"Pond suggested ______ to the museum by boat instead of by taxi.","choices":["to go","going","go","to going"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'Suggest' is followed by a gerund (or a that-clause), not by 'to + verb': 'suggested going'.","th":"suggest ตามด้วย V-ing (หรือ that-clause) ไม่ตามด้วย to + V จึงเป็น 'suggested going'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a new situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์ใหม่"},"topic":{"en":"Table / timetable","th":"ตาราง"},"passage":"Read the timetable and the notes, then answer the questions.\n\nNotes:\n• '—' means the bus does not stop there.\n• Fares: Central Station → Old Market 20 baht; Central Station → Seaview Beach 45 baht.\n• Express buses cost 15 baht extra.\n• Children under 12 pay half the full fare (including any express charge).\n• The last bus back from Seaview Beach leaves at 17:30.","table":{"title":"Bus 39: Central Station → Seaview Beach (daily unless stated)","rows":[["Bus","Leaves Central Station","Old Market","Arrives Seaview Beach","Notes"],["A","07:10","07:35","08:20",""],["B","08:00","—","08:40","Express"],["C","09:15","09:40","10:25",""],["D","10:30","—","11:10","Express"],["E","12:00","12:25","13:10","Weekends only"],["F","14:45","15:10","15:55",""]]},"stem":"Nan must be at Seaview Beach before 9:00, but she wants to leave Central Station as late as possible. Which bus should she take?","choices":["The 07:10 bus","The 08:00 bus","The 10:30 bus","The 09:15 bus"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Both the 07:10 (arrives 08:20) and the 08:00 express (arrives 08:40) arrive before 9:00. The 08:00 leaves later, so it is the best choice.","th":"รถเที่ยว 07:10 (ถึง 08:20) และรถด่วน 08:00 (ถึง 08:40) ถึงก่อน 9:00 ทั้งคู่ แต่เที่ยว 08:00 ออกช้ากว่า จึงเหมาะที่สุด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Understanding cause and effect","th":"การเข้าใจเหตุและผล"},"topic":{"en":"Emails and messages","th":"อีเมลและข้อความ"},"passage":"Class 1/4 group chat\n\nNina (18:02): Guys, Ms Lee's last day is Friday 😢 Shall we get her something?\nTom (18:05): Yes! Flowers?\nNina (18:06): She's allergic to pollen, remember? She sneezed all through Teachers' Day.\nArm (18:10): What about a photo book of our class? My uncle's shop can print one for 420 baht.\nTom (18:11): Love it! But can it be ready by Friday?\nArm (18:13): Printing takes three working days. If I send the photos tonight, the shop can start tomorrow morning and it'll be ready on Thursday afternoon.\nNina (18:16): Perfect. There are 30 of us, so that's 14 baht each. I'll collect the money tomorrow.\nTom (18:20): Can everyone send Arm their best class photo by 9 tonight?\nArm (18:21): Make it 8. My uncle's shop closes at 9.","stem":"Why did the students decide NOT to buy flowers?","choices":["The flower shop is closed.","Flowers are too expensive.","Ms Lee is allergic to pollen.","Ms Lee already has many flowers."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Nina reminds them that Ms Lee is allergic to pollen, so flowers would make her sneeze.","th":"นีน่าเตือนว่าครูลีแพ้เกสรดอกไม้ ดอกไม้จึงไม่เหมาะ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมาน (อ่านระหว่างบรรทัด)"},"topic":{"en":"Short story","th":"เรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"Tutor 7\n\nEvery evening, when Pete opened his maths homework app, a small window appeared: 'Stuck? Ask Tutor 7.' Pete asked often. Tutor 7 never gave him the answer. Instead, it asked him questions: 'What do you already know?' 'Could you draw it?' Sometimes Pete found this annoying, but slowly his marks went up.\n\nTutor 7 had some strange habits. It always wrote 'Slowly does it, dear' when he rushed, and once it reminded him to drink some water because 'brains are mostly water, you know'.\n\nOne rainy Tuesday, Pete typed, 'Finished! Thanks, Tutor 7. Good night.'\n\nThe reply came at once: 'Good night. And take your jacket tomorrow — Grandma can't wash another one this week.'\n\nPete stared at the screen. Then he walked slowly into the living room. His grandmother, a retired maths teacher, was sitting on the sofa with her tablet, wearing her reading glasses and a very guilty smile.","stem":"What does the story suggest about Grandma?","choices":["She was tired of helping Pete.","She did not know much about maths.","She cared about both Pete's learning and his health.","She wanted Pete to stop using the app."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"She guided his maths patiently and also reminded him to drink water and take his jacket, showing care for his learning and his wellbeing. She is a retired maths teacher, so she knows maths well.","th":"ยายสอนคณิตอย่างอดทน และยังเตือนให้ดื่มน้ำและเอาเสื้อแจ็กเก็ตไป แสดงว่าใส่ใจทั้งการเรียนและสุขภาพของปีท และยายเป็นครูคณิตเกษียณ จึงรู้คณิตศาสตร์ดี"}}]}},{"exam":"sp1","set":3,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.1 English — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.1 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes, reading-centred with reasoning (tables, notices, emails, stories, informational and opinion texts), plus conversation, vocabulary and grammar. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที เน้นการอ่านและการคิดวิเคราะห์ (ตาราง ประกาศ อีเมล เรื่องสั้น บทความความรู้ และบทแสดงความคิดเห็น) พร้อมบทสนทนา คำศัพท์ และไวยากรณ์ — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"sp1-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Following the logic of a dialogue","th":"การเชื่อมโยงเหตุผลในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I've been offered places at two schools, and I just can't decide.\nB: ______","choices":["Congratulations! Which one did you choose?","Why don't you make a list of the pros and cons of each?","I don't mind which one.","You must have decided by now."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A says they cannot decide, so helpful advice fits: making a list of pros and cons. Asking which one A chose ignores what A just said.","th":"A บอกว่ายังตัดสินใจไม่ได้ คำแนะนำให้เขียนข้อดีข้อเสียจึงเหมาะสม ส่วนการถามว่าเลือกโรงเรียนไหนไปแล้วขัดกับที่ A เพิ่งพูด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing appropriate register (politeness)","th":"การเลือกระดับภาษาที่เหมาะสมกับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Polite and formal language","th":"ภาษาสุภาพและเป็นทางการ"},"passage":"","stem":"You call the hotel reception because the air conditioner in your room is not working. What is the most appropriate thing to say?","choices":["I'm sorry to bother you, but the air conditioner in Room 512 doesn't seem to be working.","I demand a new room immediately because of your terrible service.","Hey, what's wrong with the air con in my room?","Your air conditioner is broken. Fix it now."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A polite complaint softens the problem ('I'm sorry to bother you', 'doesn't seem to be') and gives the room number. The others sound rude, too casual or aggressive.","th":"การแจ้งปัญหาอย่างสุภาพควรใช้คำพูดที่นุ่มนวล เช่น 'I'm sorry to bother you' และ 'doesn't seem to be' พร้อมบอกหมายเลขห้อง ประโยคอื่นฟังหยาบคาย เป็นกันเองเกินไป หรือก้าวร้าว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a suitable response","th":"การเลือกคำตอบที่เหมาะสม"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Excuse me, do you happen to know where the nearest pharmacy is?\nB: ______","choices":["No, I'm not sick.","It's nearest to me.","Yes, I do happen.","Sorry, I'm not from around here myself."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"B cannot give directions, so the natural reply is a polite apology explaining why. 'Yes, I do happen' is not English, and the other two misunderstand the question.","th":"B บอกทางไม่ได้ จึงขอโทษอย่างสุภาพพร้อมเหตุผลว่าไม่ใช่คนแถวนี้ ส่วน 'Yes, I do happen' ไม่ใช่ภาษาอังกฤษที่ถูกต้อง และอีกสองคำตอบเข้าใจคำถามผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Nouns","th":"คำนาม"},"passage":"","stem":"The doctor warned that the medicine might cause ______ such as headaches or a dry mouth.","choices":["ingredients","side effects","treatments","benefits"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Headaches and a dry mouth are unwanted results of taking a medicine, which are called 'side effects'.","th":"อาการปวดหัวและปากแห้งเป็นผลที่ไม่ต้องการจากการกินยา เรียกว่า 'side effects' (ผลข้างเคียง)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Verbs","th":"คำกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"The documentary ______ the problems that farmers faced during the drought, and many viewers donated money afterwards.","choices":["lowered","ignored","highlighted","forgave"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Viewers donated money because the film drew attention to the farmers' problems, so it 'highlighted' them. If it had ignored them, people would not have reacted.","th":"ผู้ชมบริจาคเงินเพราะสารคดีทำให้คนสนใจปัญหาของชาวนา จึงใช้ 'highlighted' (เน้นให้เห็น) ถ้าเพิกเฉย ผู้ชมคงไม่ตอบสนอง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a word from context clues","th":"การเลือกคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Adjectives","th":"คำคุณศัพท์"},"passage":"","stem":"The team's victory was ______: they had trained harder than any other team all year.","choices":["unfair","well-deserved","accidental","unexpected"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The reason after the colon (training harder than anyone) explains why they earned the win, so it was 'well-deserved'.","th":"เหตุผลหลังเครื่องหมาย : (ซ้อมหนักกว่าใคร) อธิบายว่าทำไมชัยชนะนี้สมควรได้รับ จึงใช้ 'well-deserved'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Future perfect passive","th":"Future perfect แบบ passive"},"passage":"","stem":"Don't worry — the new sports hall ______ by the time the inspectors arrive next week.","choices":["will have been completed","has been completed","completes","will complete"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'By the time…next week' points to an action finished before a future moment (future perfect), and a hall is completed by people (passive): 'will have been completed'.","th":"'By the time … next week' บอกว่าเหตุการณ์จะเสร็จก่อนจุดหนึ่งในอนาคต (future perfect) และอาคารถูกสร้างให้เสร็จ (passive) จึงเป็น 'will have been completed'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Third conditional (inverted)","th":"เงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 แบบสลับที่"},"passage":"","stem":"Had I known about the traffic jam, I ______ home much earlier.","choices":["will leave","would leave","would have left","had left"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Had I known' = 'If I had known', a third conditional about the past, so the result clause is 'would have + past participle': 'would have left'.","th":"'Had I known' มีความหมายเท่ากับ 'If I had known' ซึ่งเป็นเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 ประโยคผลจึงใช้ would have + V3 คือ 'would have left'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing the correct structure","th":"การเลือกโครงสร้างที่ถูกต้อง"},"topic":{"en":"Contrast linkers","th":"คำเชื่อมแสดงความขัดแย้ง"},"passage":"","stem":"______ the instructions twice, Nok still made a mistake in the experiment.","choices":["Although reading","Because of reading","Despite having read","Having been read"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The two parts contrast (she read twice but still made a mistake), so we need 'despite', which is followed by an -ing form: 'Despite having read'. 'Because of' shows a reason, not a contrast.","th":"สองส่วนของประโยคขัดแย้งกัน (อ่านสองรอบแต่ยังทำผิด) จึงใช้ despite ซึ่งตามด้วย V-ing คือ 'Despite having read' ส่วน because of แสดงเหตุผล ไม่ใช่ความขัดแย้ง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a new situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์ใหม่"},"topic":{"en":"Table / timetable","th":"ตาราง"},"passage":"Read the table and the notes, then answer the questions.\n\nNotes:\n• Pay before 1 March and get 10% off.\n• Brothers and sisters: the second child pays 1,000 baht less.\n• Students who join two camps receive a free camp T-shirt.\n• OceanQuest: all students must pass a swimming test on the first day.","table":{"title":"April Science Camps for Young People","rows":[["Camp","Dates","Ages","Price (baht)","Includes","Language"],["StarLab (astronomy)","3–7 April","11–14","6,500","Meals, 2 nights at an observatory","Thai & English"],["OceanQuest (marine life)","10–16 April","12–15","9,800","Meals, hotel, snorkelling","English only"],["RoboBuild (robotics)","3–5 April","10–13","4,200","Lunch, robot kit to keep","Thai only"],["GreenLab (environment)","17–21 April","12–15","5,900","Meals, tree-planting trip","Thai & English"]]},"stem":"Lin is 12. She is not a confident swimmer, she wants a camp taught partly in English, and she is free only from 10 to 21 April. Which camp suits her?","choices":["StarLab","GreenLab","OceanQuest","RoboBuild"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"GreenLab (17–21 April, ages 12–15, Thai & English) fits all her needs. OceanQuest needs a swimming test, StarLab is on 3–7 April, and RoboBuild is in Thai only and on 3–5 April.","th":"GreenLab (17–21 เม.ย. อายุ 12–15 ปี สอนไทยและอังกฤษ) ตรงทุกเงื่อนไข OceanQuest ต้องสอบว่ายน้ำ StarLab จัด 3–7 เม.ย. และ RoboBuild สอนภาษาไทยอย่างเดียวและจัด 3–5 เม.ย."}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Emails and messages","th":"อีเมลและข้อความ"},"passage":"From: Dao Siriwan\nTo: ZipPack Customer Care\nSubject: Order #4471 — wrong item\n\nDear ZipPack Customer Care,\nOn 2 May I ordered a navy-blue SchoolPro backpack (890 baht) for my son, who starts at his new school on 20 May. The parcel arrived today, 9 May, but the backpack inside is bright orange. My son says he would rather carry his books in a cardboard box. Please let me know how I can get the right backpack before school starts.\nRegards,\nDao Siriwan\n\nFrom: ZipPack Customer Care\nTo: Dao Siriwan\nSubject: Re: Order #4471 — wrong item\n\nDear Ms Siriwan,\nWe are very sorry about the mix-up. Unfortunately, the navy-blue SchoolPro is out of stock and will be available again on 25 May. We can offer you three options:\n1. Keep the orange backpack and receive a 300-baht refund.\n2. Exchange it for a dark-grey SchoolPro, which we can deliver within three working days.\n3. Return it for a full refund.\nFor options 2 and 3, a courier will collect the orange backpack from your home free of charge. Please reply by 12 May so that we can arrange this.\nKind regards,\nAnan, ZipPack Customer Care","stem":"What is Dao's main problem?","choices":["She received a backpack in the wrong colour and needs the right one soon.","Her son lost his new backpack at school.","Her parcel never arrived.","The backpack was more expensive than she expected."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"She ordered navy blue but received orange, and she needs a suitable bag before school starts on 20 May.","th":"เธอสั่งกระเป๋าสีน้ำเงินกรมท่าแต่ได้สีส้ม และต้องการกระเป๋าที่ถูกต้องก่อนเปิดเรียนวันที่ 20 พฤษภาคม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp1","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Vocabulary in context","th":"คำศัพท์ในบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Short story","th":"เรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"The Most Reliable Member of the Household\n\nEvery afternoon at four, I take up my position by the front gate. The neighbours say I am the most reliable member of the household, and they are right. Rain or shine, I am there.\n\nToday the yellow bus is late. I pace up and down the path, listening for its tired old engine. Mrs Somsri from next door leans over the fence. 'Still waiting, are you?' she laughs, and she passes me a piece of grilled chicken through the bars, which I accept politely. It would be rude to refuse.\n\nAt last the bus turns the corner. The doors hiss open and Mali jumps down, her school bag almost as big as she is. I cannot help myself. I race towards her, my whole body shaking with joy, and knock her flat onto the grass. She shrieks, then laughs, then throws her arms around my neck.\n\n'Good boy, Biscuit,' she says. 'Did you miss me?'\n\nI do not answer. I never do. But my tail says everything.","stem":"In the first paragraph, 'reliable' is closest in meaning to ______.","choices":["very friendly","quite noisy","always there when expected","extremely clever"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Rain or shine, I am there' explains the word: a reliable member can always be depended on to be in the right place.","th":"'Rain or shine, I am there' อธิบายความหมาย คือไว้ใจได้ อยู่ตรงนั้นเสมอตามที่คาดไว้"}}]}},{"exam":"sk4","set":1,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.4 English (GATE / EPLUS+) — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE / EPLUS+) — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and EPLUS+ applicants take the same paper; EPLUS+ needs a higher score. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EPLUS+ ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน EPLUS+ ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":true,"file":"sk4-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to a 'Would you mind…?' request","th":"การตอบรับคำขอที่ใช้ Would you mind…?"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Would you mind if I opened the window? It's really stuffy in here.\nB: ______ Go ahead.","choices":["Yes, I would.","Never mind.","No, thanks.","Not at all."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Would you mind…?\" asks whether you object, so \"Not at all\" (= I don't mind) agrees, which fits \"Go ahead\". \"Yes, I would\" refuses; \"Never mind\" dismisses a problem; \"No, thanks\" declines an offer.","th":"Would you mind…? ถามว่าเรารังเกียจหรือไม่ การตอบ Not at all แปลว่า ไม่รังเกียจเลย จึงเข้ากับ Go ahead ส่วน Yes, I would เป็นการปฏิเสธ Never mind ใช้บอกว่าไม่เป็นไร และ No, thanks ใช้ปฏิเสธข้อเสนอ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Describing how something went","th":"การบอกผลว่าสิ่งใดเป็นอย่างไร"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: How did your presentation go this morning?\nB: ______ I forgot half of what I wanted to say.\nA: Oh no. I'm sure it wasn't as bad as you think.","choices":["It went like a dream.","I'm going to present it soon.","Not very well, to be honest.","It's going to be fine."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B forgot half the speech and A comforts B, so B must say it went badly: \"Not very well, to be honest\". \"It went like a dream\" means it went perfectly; the future forms ignore that the presentation is already over.","th":"B ลืมสิ่งที่จะพูดไปครึ่งหนึ่ง และ A ปลอบใจ จึงต้องตอบว่าไม่ค่อยดี Not very well, to be honest ส่วน It went like a dream แปลว่าราบรื่นมาก และรูปอนาคตไม่เข้ากับเหตุการณ์ที่ผ่านไปแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs: fill in a form","th":"กริยาวลี fill in"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Please ______ the form and hand it to the officer at the counter.\"","choices":["fill up","fill in","fill on","fill with"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"We \"fill in\" (or fill out) a form, meaning write the required information. \"Fill up\" is for containers or petrol tanks; \"fill on\" does not exist; \"fill with\" needs a material (fill a box with books).","th":"กรอกแบบฟอร์มใช้ fill in (หรือ fill out) ส่วน fill up ใช้กับการเติมให้เต็ม เช่น ถังน้ำมัน fill on ไม่มีใช้ และ fill with ต้องตามด้วยสิ่งที่ใส่"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Collocations with attention","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมกับ attention"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Although the documentary was very long, it never failed to ______ my attention.\"","choices":["pay","spend","hold","make"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Something interesting \"holds your attention\" (keeps you interested). We \"pay attention\" ourselves, but a film cannot pay my attention; \"spend\" and \"make\" do not collocate with attention.","th":"สิ่งที่น่าสนใจจะ hold your attention คือดึงความสนใจไว้ได้ เราเป็นผู้ pay attention เอง แต่สารคดีไม่สามารถ pay my attention ได้ ส่วน spend และ make ไม่ใช้กับ attention"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Past perfect with 'by the time'","th":"past perfect กับ by the time"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"By the time we arrived at the cinema, the film ______.\"","choices":["has already started","already starts","had already started","would already start"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The film started before we arrived, both in the past, so the earlier action takes the past perfect: \"had already started\". Present perfect and present simple do not fit a past time frame.","th":"หนังเริ่มก่อนที่เราจะมาถึง ซึ่งเป็นอดีตทั้งคู่ เหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนจึงใช้ past perfect คือ had already started ส่วน present perfect และ present simple ไม่เข้ากับกรอบเวลาอดีต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inversion after 'Not only'","th":"การสลับที่หลัง Not only"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Not only ______ the competition, but she also broke the school record.\"","choices":["she won","did she win","she did win","won she"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"When \"Not only\" begins a sentence, the subject and auxiliary are inverted: \"Not only did she win…\". \"She won\" and \"she did win\" keep normal order, and \"won she\" inverts the main verb, which English does not allow.","th":"เมื่อขึ้นต้นประโยคด้วย Not only ต้องสลับกริยาช่วยมาไว้หน้าประธาน คือ did she win ส่วน she won และ she did win ยังเรียงแบบปกติ และ won she สลับกริยาแท้ซึ่งผิดหลัก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with 'neither of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยาเมื่อใช้ neither of"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nAlthough they [[have visited]] Tokyo [[several times]], neither of my cousins [[speak]] Japanese [[well]].","choices":["have visited","several times","speak","well"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Neither of + plural noun\" takes a singular verb in careful English: \"neither of my cousins speaks\". The other parts are correct.","th":"neither of + คำนามพหูพจน์ ใช้กริยาเอกพจน์ จึงต้องเป็น speaks ไม่ใช่ speak ส่วนอื่นถูกต้อง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Tense after 'Hardly … when'","th":"กาลของกริยาหลังโครงสร้าง Hardly … when"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\n[[Hardly]] had we sat down [[when]] the fire alarm [[went off]] and everyone [[have to]] leave.","choices":["Hardly","when","went off","have to"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Hardly had … when\" is correct inversion, and the story is in the past, so the last verb must be past too: \"everyone had to leave\". \"Have to\" is also wrong with the singular \"everyone\".","th":"Hardly had … when เป็นการสลับที่ถูกต้อง และเหตุการณ์เป็นอดีต กริยาสุดท้ายจึงต้องเป็น had to ส่วน have to ผิดทั้งกาลและไม่สอดคล้องกับ everyone ที่เป็นเอกพจน์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Cloze: past simple for past habits","th":"การเติมคำ: past simple บอกสิ่งที่เคยเป็นในอดีต"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Bangkok was once called the \"Venice of the East\" because its canals, or khlongs, (1) ______ as roads for boats carrying people and goods. Over the last century, (2) ______, many of these waterways were filled in to make room for cars. Today, the city is trying to bring some of them back to life.\n\nOne of the best-known projects is the restoration of Khlong Ong Ang, a canal near Chinatown that (3) ______ a symbol of pollution and neglect for decades. Workers removed tonnes of rubbish, and the stalls (4) ______ had crowded the banks were moved to a nearby market. The water is still far from clean, but the canal now attracts tourists, artists and families, (5) ______ come to enjoy the walking paths and colourful murals.\n\nSupporters say projects like this can (6) ______ the city cooler during the hot season, because water and trees lower the air temperature. Critics, however, point out that cleaning one canal is easy compared (7) ______ changing the habits of millions of residents who still pour waste into the water. If people (8) ______ these habits, they argue, even the most beautiful restoration will not last.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["were served","serving","served","had serve"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The canals did the job of roads in the past, so the active past simple \"served\" (= functioned) is needed. \"Were served\" is passive, \"serving\" has no main verb, and \"had serve\" is an incorrect form.","th":"คลองทำหน้าที่เป็นถนนในอดีต จึงใช้ past simple รูป active คือ served ส่วน were served เป็น passive serving ไม่มีกริยาแท้ และ had serve เป็นรูปที่ผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: 'who' in non-defining clauses","th":"การเติมคำ: who ในอนุประโยคแบบไม่จำกัดความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Bangkok was once called the \"Venice of the East\" because its canals, or khlongs, (1) ______ as roads for boats carrying people and goods. Over the last century, (2) ______, many of these waterways were filled in to make room for cars. Today, the city is trying to bring some of them back to life.\n\nOne of the best-known projects is the restoration of Khlong Ong Ang, a canal near Chinatown that (3) ______ a symbol of pollution and neglect for decades. Workers removed tonnes of rubbish, and the stalls (4) ______ had crowded the banks were moved to a nearby market. The water is still far from clean, but the canal now attracts tourists, artists and families, (5) ______ come to enjoy the walking paths and colourful murals.\n\nSupporters say projects like this can (6) ______ the city cooler during the hot season, because water and trees lower the air temperature. Critics, however, point out that cleaning one canal is easy compared (7) ______ changing the habits of millions of residents who still pour waste into the water. If people (8) ______ these habits, they argue, even the most beautiful restoration will not last.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["which","that","who","whom"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"After a comma the clause refers to people (tourists, artists and families) and is the subject of \"come\", so \"who\". \"That\" cannot follow a comma, \"which\" is for things, and \"whom\" is an object form.","th":"หลังจุลภาคอนุประโยคนี้หมายถึงคน (tourists, artists, families) และเป็นประธานของ come จึงใช้ who ส่วน that ใช้หลังจุลภาคไม่ได้ which ใช้กับสิ่งของ และ whom ใช้เป็นกรรม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main purpose of an article","th":"การหาจุดประสงค์หลักของบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความ"},"passage":"Why Teenagers Can't Fall Asleep Early\n\nMany parents believe that teenagers stay up late simply because they are addicted to their phones or would rather chat with friends than rest. While screens certainly play a part, scientists now say that the main reason is biological. During puberty, the body begins to release melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, around two hours later than it did in childhood. As a result, a typical fifteen-year-old does not feel tired until eleven o'clock or even midnight, no matter how early he or she goes to bed.\n\nThis shift would not be a serious problem if teenagers could sleep later in the morning. Unfortunately, most secondary schools start their day between seven and eight o'clock, which means many students have to wake up at six or earlier. Doctors recommend that people aged thirteen to eighteen get between eight and ten hours of sleep a night, yet surveys in several countries show that fewer than one in three actually do. The missing hours add up quickly, and researchers call the result \"sleep debt\".\n\nSleep debt affects much more than how tired students feel. Studies have linked it to poorer memory, slower reaction times and difficulty controlling emotions. In some experiments, students who were short of sleep remembered far less of what they had learned the previous day than classmates who had slept well. There is also evidence that tired teenagers are more likely to have accidents on the road.\n\nIn response, a number of school districts in the United States have pushed back their start times to half past eight or later. The results have been encouraging: attendance has risen, fewer students fall asleep in class, and in some schools exam results have improved. Critics argue that later starts cause problems for parents who work early and for after-school sports, which must finish later in the evening. Nevertheless, supporters insist that these difficulties can be solved, whereas the effects of losing sleep cannot.\n\nFor students who cannot change their school timetable, experts suggest a few simple habits: keeping the same bedtime at weekends, avoiding bright screens for an hour before bed, and not drinking coffee or energy drinks after lunch.","table":null,"stem":"What is the main purpose of the article?","choices":["To explain why teenagers sleep late and how this affects them","To argue that teenagers should stop using phones at night","To describe how the body produces melatonin in children","To show that American schools are better than other schools"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The article explains the biological cause of late sleep, the effects of sleep debt and possible responses. Phones are mentioned only as a minor factor, melatonin is one detail, and no school system is ranked.","th":"บทความอธิบายสาเหตุทางชีววิทยาที่วัยรุ่นนอนดึก ผลของการนอนไม่พอ และแนวทางแก้ไข ส่วนโทรศัพท์ถูกกล่าวถึงเป็นปัจจัยรองเท่านั้น เมลาโทนินเป็นเพียงรายละเอียด และไม่ได้จัดอันดับโรงเรียน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inferring cause and effect in a narrative","th":"การอนุมานเหตุและผลในเรื่องเล่า"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"The Speech\n\nPloy had been practising her speech for three weeks, but as she stood behind the curtain of the school hall, every word seemed to have disappeared from her head. Through a gap in the curtain she could see the judges at a long table near the stage: two teachers from her own school and a visiting examiner from a language institute, who was writing something in a small notebook. Ploy's hands were shaking so badly that the paper with her notes rattled.\n\n\"Number seven, Ploy Srisuk,\" the announcer called.\n\nShe walked onto the stage and looked at the audience. Somewhere in the third row her grandmother was sitting, wearing the green silk scarf she saved for special occasions. Ploy had chosen to speak about her grandmother's life: how she had left her village at sixteen with almost no money, sold noodles from a cart in Bangkok for twenty years, and saved enough to send all four of her children to university.\n\nFor the first minute, Ploy's voice was so quiet that the people at the back leaned forward. Then she made a mistake. Instead of saying \"she worked from dawn until dusk\", she said \"she worked from dusk until dawn\". A few students laughed. Ploy's face burned, and for a terrible moment she thought about running off the stage.\n\nThen she remembered something her grandmother had told her the night before. \"When the noodles burn,\" her grandmother had said, \"you don't throw away the whole pot. You smile at the customer and cook another bowl.\"\n\nPloy smiled. \"Actually,\" she said, \"that's not as wrong as it sounds. Some nights she really did work until dawn.\" This time the whole hall laughed with her, not at her, and suddenly the words came back. She finished the speech without looking at her notes once.\n\nPloy did not win first prize; that went to a boy from Matthayom 6 who had lived abroad for five years. But when she came down from the stage, the examiner stopped her and handed her a page torn from the small notebook. On it were four words: \"Best recovery I've seen.\"","table":null,"stem":"Why did the audience laugh the second time?","choices":["Ploy made the same mistake again.","Ploy turned her mistake into a joke that also made sense.","Some students were still making fun of her.","Her grandmother stood up and waved."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Ploy explained that working \"from dusk until dawn\" was partly true, turning the slip into humour, so the audience \"laughed with her, not at her\". She did not repeat the mistake, and the laughter was no longer mocking.","th":"พลอยอธิบายว่าการทำงานตั้งแต่ค่ำจนรุ่งเช้าก็เป็นเรื่องจริงบางส่วน จึงเปลี่ยนความผิดพลาดเป็นมุกตลก ผู้ชมจึงหัวเราะไปกับเธอ ไม่ใช่หัวเราะเยาะ เธอไม่ได้พูดผิดซ้ำ"}}]}},{"exam":"sk4","set":2,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.4 English (GATE / EPLUS+) — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE / EPLUS+) — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and EPLUS+ applicants take the same paper; EPLUS+ needs a higher score. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EPLUS+ ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน EPLUS+ ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"sk4-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Agreeing to a request for help","th":"การตอบรับคำขอความช่วยเหลือ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Could you give me a hand with these boxes? They're heavier than they look.\nB: ______ Where do you want them?","choices":["No, I couldn't.","Sure, no problem.","Give me a break.","Help yourself."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"B then asks where to put the boxes, so B is agreeing: \"Sure, no problem\". \"No, I couldn't\" refuses, \"Help yourself\" invites someone to take something, and \"Give me a break\" means stop bothering me.","th":"B ถามต่อว่าจะให้วางกล่องที่ไหน แสดงว่ายินดีช่วย จึงตอบ Sure, no problem ส่วน No, I couldn't เป็นการปฏิเสธ Help yourself ใช้เชิญให้หยิบเอง และ Give me a break แปลว่าอย่ากวนใจ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Accepting criticism","th":"การยอมรับคำวิจารณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: You look exhausted. Have you been up all night?\nB: Pretty much. I had to finish my history report.\nA: Again? You really should stop leaving things until the last minute.\nB: ______ I'll start the next one the day it's set.","choices":["Mind your own business.","Speak for yourself.","Point taken.","So do I."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B promises to start earlier next time, so B accepts the criticism: \"Point taken\" (= I understand and accept what you said). \"Mind your own business\" rejects it rudely, \"Speak for yourself\" says the comment applies to A, and \"So do I\" makes no sense here.","th":"B สัญญาว่าจะเริ่มทำงานเร็วขึ้น แสดงว่ายอมรับคำติ จึงตอบ Point taken (รับทราบและยอมรับ) ส่วน Mind your own business เป็นการปฏิเสธอย่างหยาบ Speak for yourself หมายถึงคนพูดเองต่างหาก และ So do I ไม่เข้ากับบริบท"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Collocations with 'decision'","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมกับ decision"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"We need to ______ a decision about the class trip by Friday.\"","choices":["do","make","give","put"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The usual collocation is \"make a decision\". Thai learners often choose \"do\", but we do homework or a job, not a decision; \"give\" and \"put\" do not collocate with decision.","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันคือ make a decision ผู้เรียนไทยมักใช้ do แต่ do ใช้กับ homework หรือ job ส่วน give และ put ไม่ใช้กับ decision"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs: run into","th":"กริยาวลี run into"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"I ran ______ an old classmate at the shopping centre yesterday; I hadn't seen him for years.\"","choices":["out of","into","over","up"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Run into someone\" means meet them by chance. \"Run out of\" means have none left, \"run over\" means hit with a vehicle, and \"run up\" means build up a debt or run towards.","th":"run into someone แปลว่าเจอโดยบังเอิญ ส่วน run out of แปลว่าหมด run over แปลว่าขับรถทับ และ run up แปลว่าก่อหนี้หรือวิ่งเข้าไปหา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"'Be going to' for predictions from evidence","th":"be going to สำหรับการคาดการณ์จากหลักฐาน"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Look at those dark clouds! It ______ rain any minute.\"","choices":["'s going to","will be","rains","is raining"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"We can see evidence now (dark clouds), so we use \"be going to\" for the prediction: \"It's going to rain\". \"Will be rain\" is ungrammatical, \"rains\" is a habit, and \"is raining\" means it has already started.","th":"เห็นหลักฐานในปัจจุบัน (เมฆดำ) จึงใช้ be going to ในการคาดการณ์ คือ It's going to rain ส่วน will be rain ผิดไวยากรณ์ rains เป็นนิสัยหรือความจริงทั่วไป และ is raining หมายถึงฝนตกอยู่แล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inversion after 'Only after'","th":"การสลับที่หลัง Only after"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Only after the results were announced ______ how close the competition had been.\"","choices":["did we realise","we realised","we did realise","had realised we"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Only after + clause\" at the start of a sentence causes inversion in the main clause: auxiliary + subject + verb, \"did we realise\". The other choices keep normal order or put the subject after the main verb.","th":"เมื่อขึ้นต้นด้วย Only after + อนุประโยค ประโยคหลักต้องสลับที่เป็นกริยาช่วย + ประธาน + กริยา คือ did we realise ส่วนตัวเลือกอื่นเรียงแบบปกติหรือวางประธานหลังกริยาแท้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Agreement with 'each of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องเมื่อใช้ each of"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nAfter they [[had completed]] the [[three-day]] workshop, each of the students [[were]] given [[a certificate]].","choices":["had completed","three-day","were","a certificate"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Each of the students\" is singular, so the verb must be \"was given\", not \"were given\".","th":"each of the students เป็นเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น was given ไม่ใช่ were given"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Third conditional result clause","th":"ผลของประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and wishes","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและ wish"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nI [[would leave]] home much earlier [[if]] I [[had known]] about [[the]] traffic jam.","choices":["would leave","if","had known","the"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"If I had known\" is an unreal past condition, so the result must be \"would have left\", not \"would leave\".","th":"If I had known เป็นเงื่อนไขที่ไม่จริงในอดีต ผลจึงต้องเป็น would have left ไม่ใช่ would leave"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: verbs with dependent prepositions","th":"การเติมคำ: กริยาที่ต้องมีบุพบทตาม"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Millions of people around the world now (1) ______ language-learning apps, hoping that a few minutes a day will make them fluent. The appeal is easy to understand: the lessons are short, colourful and often free, and the apps reward users with points and badges (2) ______ they complete a lesson. But can an app really teach you a language?\n\nResearch suggests the answer is \"partly\". Apps are excellent for building vocabulary, because they show words again and again until they (3) ______ in long-term memory. They are less effective, (4) ______, at developing speaking skills. Talking to a phone is very different from having a real conversation, (5) ______ you must understand different accents, react quickly and deal with unexpected questions.\n\nAnother problem is motivation. Many users start with great enthusiasm but give up within a month. Some experts believe that the game-like features of apps can actually (6) ______ this problem, since learners begin to care more about keeping their daily \"streak\" than about understanding the language.\n\nThe most successful learners, it seems, are (7) ______ who use apps as only one part of their routine. They also watch films, read simple books and, most importantly, look for chances to speak with real people. As one teacher put it, \"An app can open the door, but you still have to (8) ______ through it yourself.\"","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["depend","count","believe","rely on"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Rely on\" takes an object (apps) and means depend on them. \"Depend\" and \"count\" need \"on\" before an object, and \"believe apps\" means think they are telling the truth.","th":"rely on ตามด้วยกรรม (apps) แปลว่าพึ่งพา ส่วน depend และ count ต้องมี on ก่อนกรรม และ believe apps แปลว่าเชื่อว่าแอปพูดความจริง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Cloze: 'where' for situations","th":"การเติมคำ: where ใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"Millions of people around the world now (1) ______ language-learning apps, hoping that a few minutes a day will make them fluent. The appeal is easy to understand: the lessons are short, colourful and often free, and the apps reward users with points and badges (2) ______ they complete a lesson. But can an app really teach you a language?\n\nResearch suggests the answer is \"partly\". Apps are excellent for building vocabulary, because they show words again and again until they (3) ______ in long-term memory. They are less effective, (4) ______, at developing speaking skills. Talking to a phone is very different from having a real conversation, (5) ______ you must understand different accents, react quickly and deal with unexpected questions.\n\nAnother problem is motivation. Many users start with great enthusiasm but give up within a month. Some experts believe that the game-like features of apps can actually (6) ______ this problem, since learners begin to care more about keeping their daily \"streak\" than about understanding the language.\n\nThe most successful learners, it seems, are (7) ______ who use apps as only one part of their routine. They also watch films, read simple books and, most importantly, look for chances to speak with real people. As one teacher put it, \"An app can open the door, but you still have to (8) ______ through it yourself.\"","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["where","which","what","whom"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The clause after the gap is complete (you must understand…), and it describes a situation, a real conversation, so \"where\" (= in which) is right. \"Which\" and \"whom\" would need a missing subject or object, and \"what\" cannot follow a noun.","th":"อนุประโยคหลังช่องว่างสมบูรณ์แล้ว (you must understand…) และขยายสถานการณ์คือ a real conversation จึงใช้ where (= in which) ส่วน which และ whom ต้องมีประธานหรือกรรมที่หายไป และ what ใช้ตามหลังคำนามไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the best title","th":"การเลือกชื่อเรื่องที่เหมาะสมที่สุด"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความ"},"passage":"Farms in the Sky\n\nImagine a farm with no fields, no tractors and no sunshine. Instead, there are rows of shelves stacked twelve levels high inside a warehouse, each one covered with lettuce, basil or strawberries growing under purple LED lights. This is vertical farming, and its supporters believe it could change the way cities feed themselves.\n\nThe idea is simple. Rather than spreading crops across a large area of land, vertical farms grow them upwards, in a building where every condition can be controlled. Temperature, humidity and light are adjusted by computers twenty-four hours a day. Because the plants are grown indoors, there are few insects to fight, so farmers rarely need pesticides. Most vertical farms also use hydroponics, a method in which roots are fed by water containing nutrients rather than by soil. This system recycles the water, using up to ninety per cent less than a traditional farm.\n\nLocation is another advantage. A vertical farm can be built in the middle of a city, close to supermarkets and restaurants. Vegetables can be picked in the morning and sold the same afternoon, which means they are fresher and less food is wasted during transport.\n\nHowever, vertical farming is not the perfect solution it is sometimes made out to be. The biggest problem is energy. Sunlight is free, but LED lights and air-conditioning are not, and several well-funded vertical farming companies have gone out of business because their electricity bills were simply too high. Critics also point out that the method works well only for small, fast-growing plants such as salad leaves and herbs. Staple crops like rice, wheat and potatoes need far more space and light, and growing them indoors would be extremely expensive.\n\nFor now, then, vertical farms are unlikely to replace traditional agriculture. Instead, they may become a useful addition to it, especially in crowded, wealthy cities where land is scarce and consumers are willing to pay more for fresh, pesticide-free vegetables. If renewable energy becomes much cheaper, however, the picture could change dramatically.","table":null,"stem":"Which title best summarises the passage?","choices":["Vertical Farming: Promising but Limited","How to Grow Rice Indoors","The End of Traditional Agriculture","Why LED Lights Are Bad for Plants"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The passage presents advantages (water, pesticides, freshness) and serious limits (energy, crop types), concluding that vertical farms are a useful addition, not a replacement. It says rice is too expensive to grow indoors, rejects the end of traditional farming, and never says LEDs harm plants.","th":"บทความกล่าวทั้งข้อดี (ประหยัดน้ำ ไม่ใช้ยาฆ่าแมลง สดใหม่) และข้อจำกัด (พลังงาน ชนิดพืช) แล้วสรุปว่าเป็นส่วนเสริม ไม่ใช่การแทนที่ บทความบอกว่าการปลูกข้าวในอาคารแพงเกินไป ปฏิเสธว่าการเกษตรแบบดั้งเดิมจะหมดไป และไม่ได้บอกว่าไฟ LED เป็นผลเสียต่อพืช"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Interpreting figurative language","th":"การตีความภาษาเชิงภาพพจน์"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"The Wallet\n\nThe wallet was lying in a puddle beside the bus stop, its brown leather turning almost black in the rain. Krit picked it up without thinking and opened it. Inside were three thousand-baht notes, an ID card, a photograph of a little girl in a school uniform, and a folded receipt from a hospital.\n\nThree thousand baht. Krit stared at the money for longer than he would have liked to admit. His phone screen had been cracked for months, and his parents had already told him that a new one would have to wait until after the rice harvest. Nobody had seen him pick the wallet up. The bus stop was empty, and the street was hidden behind a curtain of rain.\n\nThen he looked at the photograph again. The girl had a gap between her front teeth and was holding up a certificate with both hands, grinning as if she had just won the world. Krit unfolded the receipt. It was for a medical test, and at the bottom someone had written in pen: \"Results Monday — don't forget!\"\n\nHe sighed, put the wallet in his bag and, instead of going home, walked twenty minutes in the opposite direction to the police station. The officer at the desk wrote down his name, telephone number and school, and told him that he could go.\n\nOn Monday morning, the whole of Matthayom 3 was called to the assembly hall to meet a new mathematics teacher who had transferred from the north. She was a small woman with a soft voice, and she began by saying that she wanted to tell them a story before she taught them a single equation. \"On Friday,\" she said, \"I lost something very important in the rain. It contained money I had saved for my daughter's operation. I was sure I would never see it again.\" She paused and looked down at a piece of paper in her hand. \"Is there a student here called Krit Wongsa?\"\n\nKrit felt three hundred faces turn towards him at once. He had never wanted the floor to swallow him so much in his life—and he had never felt so proud.","table":null,"stem":"The phrase \"a curtain of rain\" suggests that the rain was","choices":["light and gentle","so heavy that it was hard to see through","about to stop","falling on only one side of the street"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A curtain blocks the view, so a \"curtain of rain\" is rain so heavy that the street is hidden. This also explains why nobody could see Krit. Light rain or rain that is stopping would not hide the street.","th":"ม่านบังสายตา a curtain of rain จึงหมายถึงฝนตกหนักจนมองไม่เห็นถนน ซึ่งอธิบายด้วยว่าทำไมไม่มีใครเห็นกฤษ ฝนปรอย ๆ หรือฝนที่กำลังจะหยุดจะไม่บังถนน"}}]}},{"exam":"sk4","set":3,"title":{"en":"Suankularb M.4 English (GATE / EPLUS+) — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สวนกุหลาบ (GATE / EPLUS+) — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes: conversation, vocabulary, grammar, error identification, cloze and reading. GATE and EPLUS+ applicants take the same paper; EPLUS+ needs a higher score. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที: บทสนทนา คำศัพท์ ไวยากรณ์ หาจุดผิด เติมคำในบทความ และการอ่าน ผู้สมัคร GATE และ EPLUS+ ใช้ข้อสอบชุดเดียวกัน EPLUS+ ต้องได้คะแนนสูงกว่า — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"sk4-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Responding to thanks","th":"การตอบรับคำขอบคุณ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Thanks so much for helping me move house last weekend.\nB: ______ That's what friends are for.","choices":["It doesn't matter.","Same here.","I'm afraid not.","Don't mention it."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Don't mention it\" is a standard reply to thanks. \"It doesn't matter\" answers an apology or a problem, \"Same here\" agrees with a feeling, and \"I'm afraid not\" is a polite no.","th":"Don't mention it เป็นคำตอบมาตรฐานเมื่อมีคนขอบคุณ ส่วน It doesn't matter ใช้ตอบคำขอโทษหรือบอกว่าไม่สำคัญ Same here ใช้บอกว่ารู้สึกเหมือนกัน และ I'm afraid not เป็นการปฏิเสธอย่างสุภาพ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Disagreeing in a formal register","th":"การแสดงความไม่เห็นด้วยแบบทางการ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best response to complete the conversation.\nA: Honestly, I don't see why we need to learn history. It's all in the past.\nB: ______ Understanding the past helps us avoid repeating the same mistakes.","choices":["I couldn't have put it better myself.","You took the words right out of my mouth.","Fair enough, you've got a point.","I'm afraid I'd have to disagree."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"B goes on to defend history, so B disagrees: \"I'm afraid I'd have to disagree\". The other three all agree with A, which contradicts B's next sentence.","th":"B กล่าวต่อเพื่อปกป้องวิชาประวัติศาสตร์ จึงไม่เห็นด้วย I'm afraid I'd have to disagree อีกสามตัวเลือกล้วนเห็นด้วยกับ A ซึ่งขัดกับประโยคถัดไปของ B"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs: lay off","th":"กริยาวลี lay off"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"The factory had to ______ two hundred workers when sales collapsed.\"","choices":["lay out","lay off","lay down","lay by"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Lay off\" means dismiss workers because there is not enough work, which matches collapsing sales. \"Lay out\" means arrange or spend, \"lay down\" means put down or establish rules, and \"lay by\" means save.","th":"lay off แปลว่าเลิกจ้างเพราะงานไม่พอ ตรงกับยอดขายตก ส่วน lay out แปลว่าจัดวางหรือใช้จ่าย lay down แปลว่าวางลงหรือกำหนดกฎ และ lay by แปลว่าเก็บออม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs: put up with","th":"กริยาวลี put up with"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำปรากฏร่วมและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"We can't ______ with this noise any longer; let's complain to the manager.\"","choices":["get on","keep up","put up","come up"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Put up with\" means tolerate, which fits complaining about noise. \"Get on with\" means have a good relationship or continue, \"keep up with\" means stay at the same level, and \"come up with\" means think of an idea.","th":"put up with แปลว่าทนกับ เข้ากับการจะไปร้องเรียนเรื่องเสียงดัง ส่วน get on with แปลว่าเข้ากันได้หรือทำต่อไป keep up with แปลว่าตามให้ทัน และ come up with แปลว่าคิดออก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inverted third conditional","th":"เงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 รูปสลับที่"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and wishes","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและ wish"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"Had the government acted sooner, the flooding ______ so severe.\"","choices":["would not have been","would not be","will not be","had not been"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Had the government acted\" = \"If the government had acted\", an unreal past condition about a past flood, so the result is \"would not have been\". \"Would not be\" refers to the present, and the other forms are not conditional results.","th":"Had the government acted มีความหมายเท่ากับ If the government had acted เป็นเงื่อนไขที่ไม่จริงในอดีตเกี่ยวกับน้ำท่วมที่เกิดไปแล้ว ผลจึงเป็น would not have been ส่วน would not be หมายถึงปัจจุบัน และรูปอื่นไม่ใช่รูปผลของเงื่อนไข"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past participle clauses for feelings","th":"participle clause รูป V3 แสดงความรู้สึก"},"topic":{"en":"Relative and participle clauses","th":"อนุประโยคสัมพันธ์และ participle clause"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the best option to complete the sentence.\n\"______ by the news, she couldn't say a word.\"","choices":["Shocking","Having shocked","To shock","Shocked"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"She was shocked by the news (passive meaning, \"by the news\"), so the past participle \"Shocked\" is correct. \"Shocking\" and \"Having shocked\" would mean she shocked someone, and \"To shock\" expresses purpose.","th":"เธอตกใจเพราะข่าว (ความหมาย passive มี by the news) จึงใช้ V3 คือ Shocked ส่วน Shocking และ Having shocked หมายถึงเธอทำให้คนอื่นตกใจ และ To shock แสดงจุดประสงค์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"'No sooner … than'","th":"No sooner … than"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement, comparison and inversion","th":"ความสอดคล้อง การเปรียบเทียบ และการสลับที่"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nNo sooner [[had]] the teacher left the room [[when]] the students [[started]] [[talking]] loudly.","choices":["had","when","started","talking"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"No sooner\" is paired with \"than\", not \"when\": \"No sooner had the teacher left the room than…\". (\"Hardly/Scarcely\" go with \"when\".)","th":"No sooner ต้องคู่กับ than ไม่ใช่ when คือ No sooner had the teacher left the room than… (ส่วน Hardly/Scarcely คู่กับ when)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Wish + past perfect for past regrets","th":"wish + past perfect เสียดายเรื่องในอดีต"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and wishes","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและ wish"},"passage":"","table":null,"stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect.\nI wish I [[had]] more time yesterday; then I [[could have]] [[finished]] the project [[properly]].","choices":["had","could have","finished","properly"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The regret is about yesterday, so wish needs the past perfect: \"I wish I had had more time yesterday\". The single \"had\" is past simple, which is for present wishes.","th":"ความเสียดายเป็นเรื่องเมื่อวาน wish จึงต้องตามด้วย past perfect คือ I wish I had had more time yesterday ส่วน had ตัวเดียวเป็น past simple ซึ่งใช้กับความปรารถนาในปัจจุบัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cloze: reduced passive clauses","th":"การเติมคำ: อนุประโยค passive แบบลดรูป"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"For centuries, doctors have known that patients sometimes feel better after receiving a treatment that, chemically speaking, should do nothing at all. This phenomenon, (1) ______ as the placebo effect, is one of the strangest puzzles in medicine. In clinical trials, a sugar pill can reduce pain, nausea or anxiety in a surprising number of patients, provided they believe it is real medicine.\n\nScientists once dismissed the effect as (2) ______ imagination, but brain scans have shown that it produces measurable physical changes. When patients expect relief from pain, the brain releases natural chemicals which, according to several studies, (3) ______ in much the same way as some painkillers. Expectation, in other words, is not just a feeling; it can alter the body itself.\n\nEven more surprising is a series of experiments on \"open-label\" placebos. In these studies, patients were told honestly that the pills they (4) ______ contained no active ingredient. (5) ______ this, many of them still reported feeling better. Researchers think that the ritual of taking medicine—swallowing a pill at the same time every day, being cared for by a doctor—may be (6) ______ triggers the response.\n\nThe placebo effect has a darker twin: the nocebo effect. Patients who are warned about possible side effects sometimes develop those very symptoms, (7) ______ they have been given a harmless pill. This raises a difficult ethical question for doctors: how can they be honest about risks without making patients ill? Some suggest that the answer lies not in hiding information but in the way it is (8) ______.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["knowing","to know","known","is known"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Known as\" is a reduced passive relative clause (= which is known as). \"Knowing\" is active, \"to know\" does not fit, and \"is known\" would create a second main verb in the sentence.","th":"known as เป็นอนุประโยค passive แบบลดรูป (= which is known as) ส่วน knowing เป็น active to know ไม่เข้ากับโครงสร้าง และ is known จะทำให้ประโยคมีกริยาแท้สองตัว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Cloze: 'despite' + noun phrase","th":"การเติมคำ: despite + นามวลี"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"บทความเติมคำ"},"passage":"For centuries, doctors have known that patients sometimes feel better after receiving a treatment that, chemically speaking, should do nothing at all. This phenomenon, (1) ______ as the placebo effect, is one of the strangest puzzles in medicine. In clinical trials, a sugar pill can reduce pain, nausea or anxiety in a surprising number of patients, provided they believe it is real medicine.\n\nScientists once dismissed the effect as (2) ______ imagination, but brain scans have shown that it produces measurable physical changes. When patients expect relief from pain, the brain releases natural chemicals which, according to several studies, (3) ______ in much the same way as some painkillers. Expectation, in other words, is not just a feeling; it can alter the body itself.\n\nEven more surprising is a series of experiments on \"open-label\" placebos. In these studies, patients were told honestly that the pills they (4) ______ contained no active ingredient. (5) ______ this, many of them still reported feeling better. Researchers think that the ritual of taking medicine—swallowing a pill at the same time every day, being cared for by a doctor—may be (6) ______ triggers the response.\n\nThe placebo effect has a darker twin: the nocebo effect. Patients who are warned about possible side effects sometimes develop those very symptoms, (7) ______ they have been given a harmless pill. This raises a difficult ethical question for doctors: how can they be honest about risks without making patients ill? Some suggest that the answer lies not in hiding information but in the way it is (8) ______.","table":null,"stem":"Read the passage. Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["Although","In spite","Even though","Despite"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Before a noun or pronoun (this) we need a preposition: \"Despite this\". \"Although\" and \"even though\" need a clause, and \"in spite\" needs \"of\".","th":"หน้าคำนามหรือสรรพนาม (this) ต้องใช้บุพบท คือ Despite this ส่วน Although, Even though ต้องตามด้วยอนุประโยค และ In spite ต้องมี of"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การหาใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความ"},"passage":"The Disappearing Night\n\nFor most of human history, the night sky was one of the great shared experiences of our species. On a clear night, almost anyone could look up and see thousands of stars and the pale band of the Milky Way. Today, more than a third of the world's population can no longer see the Milky Way from where they live, and in some heavily developed regions the figure is far higher. The cause is light pollution: the excessive or badly directed artificial light that now spills from streetlights, buildings, billboards and car parks into the sky.\n\nAstronomers were among the first to raise the alarm, since a bright sky makes faint objects impossible to observe. But the consequences extend well beyond science. Many animals depend on the natural cycle of light and darkness to find food, navigate and reproduce. Newly hatched sea turtles, for example, find the ocean by moving towards the brightest horizon, which on an undeveloped beach is the moonlit sea. On a beach backed by hotels, however, the hatchlings often crawl inland, where they die of exhaustion or are caught by predators. Migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate, are drawn towards lit-up towers, and large numbers are killed each year in collisions.\n\nHumans are affected too. Exposure to bright light at night, particularly the blue-rich light produced by many LEDs, suppresses the production of melatonin, the hormone that regulates sleep. Some researchers have suggested links between long-term exposure and health problems, although these connections are not yet fully understood.\n\nUnlike many environmental problems, light pollution is remarkably easy to reverse. A polluted river may take decades to recover, but a light that is switched off stops polluting instantly. Simple measures—shielding streetlights so that they point downwards, using warmer colours, and dimming or turning off lights that are not needed late at night—can dramatically reduce the problem while also cutting energy bills. Several towns around the world have gained \"dark sky\" status by adopting such rules, and some now attract tourists who come specifically to see the stars.\n\nThe real obstacle, then, is not technology but awareness. Few people think of light as a pollutant at all; for most of us, a brightly lit street simply feels safe and modern. Changing that perception may prove harder than changing the bulbs.","table":null,"stem":"What is the main point of the passage?","choices":["LED lights should be banned in all cities.","Astronomers are the main victims of light pollution.","Light pollution harms nature and people but could be reduced fairly easily.","Dark-sky towns have become popular tourist destinations."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The passage describes effects on astronomy, animals and humans, then stresses that the problem is \"remarkably easy to reverse\". It never calls for an LED ban, says the consequences go beyond astronomers, and mentions dark-sky tourism only as a detail.","th":"บทความอธิบายผลกระทบต่อดาราศาสตร์ สัตว์ และมนุษย์ แล้วเน้นว่าปัญหานี้แก้ไขได้ง่ายมาก บทความไม่ได้เรียกร้องให้ห้ามใช้ LED บอกว่าผลกระทบไปไกลกว่านักดาราศาสตร์ และกล่าวถึงการท่องเที่ยวชมดาวเป็นเพียงรายละเอียด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sk4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Interpreting the theme of a story","th":"การตีความแก่นเรื่อง"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่า"},"passage":"The Map\n\nWhen my grandfather died, he left me three things: a wristwatch that ran ten minutes slow, a box of cassette tapes I had no way of playing, and a map. The map was drawn in blue ballpoint on the back of an old calendar, and it showed the neighbourhood in Thonburi where he had grown up. Every house, shop and tree was labelled in his tiny, careful handwriting: \"Mr Lim's bicycle repairs\", \"big tamarind—climb here\", \"Auntie Pen's sweets (best coconut ice cream)\".\n\nFor months, I left it in a drawer. Then one Sunday in the hot season, with nothing better to do, I took the boat across the river and tried to follow it.\n\nI was not prepared for how little remained. Where Mr Lim's shop should have been, there was a convenience store with automatic doors that sighed open as I approached. The big tamarind had been replaced by a car park. I walked up and down the soi three times looking for Auntie Pen's, feeling increasingly foolish, before an old woman sitting in a doorway asked me whether I was lost.\n\nI showed her the map. She held it very close to her face, then very far away, and then she began to laugh—a dry, delighted laugh that turned into a cough. \"Somchai,\" she said, tapping my grandfather's name in the corner. \"He used to steal mangoes from my father's tree.\"\n\nHer name was Wan, and she was the daughter of the Auntie Pen on the map; the sweet shop had closed forty years ago. Yet she remembered my grandfather as a thin boy with enormous ears who could climb anything, and she spent the next two hours telling me stories about him that nobody in my family had ever heard.\n\nBy the time I left, the sun was setting over the river. I had not found a single place on the map. And yet, as the boat pulled away from the pier, I realised that I had found exactly what my grandfather would have wanted me to find, even if he could not have planned it himself. The map had never really been about places at all.","table":null,"stem":"What does the narrator mean by \"The map had never really been about places at all\"?","choices":["Its real value lay in connecting her to people and memories of her grandfather.","The map was too inaccurate to be useful.","Her grandfather had been bad at drawing.","The neighbourhood had hardly changed since his childhood."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"She found no places but gained two hours of stories about her grandfather from someone who knew him; the map's value was the connection to people and memories. Accuracy and drawing skill are not the point, and the neighbourhood had changed a great deal.","th":"เธอไม่พบสถานที่ใดเลย แต่ได้ฟังเรื่องราวของคุณปู่สองชั่วโมงจากคนที่รู้จักท่าน คุณค่าของแผนที่จึงอยู่ที่การเชื่อมโยงกับผู้คนและความทรงจำ ความแม่นยำหรือฝีมือการวาดไม่ใช่ประเด็น และย่านนั้นเปลี่ยนไปมาก"}}]}},{"exam":"sp4","set":1,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.4 English — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes in a standardized-test style: academic vocabulary in context, sentence structure, error identification and academic reading (about introductory TU-GET / CU-TEP level). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที แนวข้อสอบมาตรฐาน: คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท โครงสร้างประโยค หาจุดผิด และการอ่านบทความวิชาการ (ระดับใกล้ TU-GET / CU-TEP เบื้องต้น) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":true,"file":"sp4-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: mitigate","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: mitigate"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: verbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The new drainage system is expected to ______ the risk of flooding in low-lying districts, although it cannot remove that risk completely.\"","choices":["neglect","mitigate","aggravate","eliminate"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Mitigate\" means to make something less serious, which fits a system that reduces the risk without removing it. \"Eliminate\" (remove completely) contradicts \"cannot remove that risk completely\"; \"aggravate\" means make worse; \"neglect\" means ignore.","th":"mitigate แปลว่า ทำให้รุนแรงน้อยลง ตรงกับระบบที่ลดความเสี่ยงได้แต่ขจัดไม่หมด ส่วน eliminate (ขจัดให้หมด) ขัดกับ cannot remove completely, aggravate คือทำให้แย่ลง, neglect คือละเลย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: consent","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: consent"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: nouns","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำนาม"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Researchers must obtain the ______ of participants before including them in a study.\"","choices":["contempt","consensus","content","consent"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Consent\" is permission, which researchers must get from participants. \"Consensus\" is general agreement within a group, \"content\" is what something contains and \"contempt\" is a feeling of disrespect.","th":"consent แปลว่า ความยินยอม ซึ่งนักวิจัยต้องได้จากผู้เข้าร่วม ส่วน consensus = ความเห็นพ้องของกลุ่ม, content = เนื้อหา, contempt = การดูหมิ่น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: distinct","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: distinct"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: adjectives and adverbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำคุณศัพท์และคำวิเศษณ์"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Although the two species look almost identical, they are genetically ______ and cannot interbreed.\"","choices":["extinct","distinct","indistinct","instinctive"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Distinct\" means clearly different; the contrast with \"look almost identical\" and \"cannot interbreed\" requires it. \"Indistinct\" (unclear) does not fit, \"extinct\" species no longer exist, and \"instinctive\" describes behaviour.","th":"distinct แปลว่า แตกต่างกันชัดเจน ซึ่งขัดแย้งกับ look almost identical ตามที่ although ต้องการ ส่วน indistinct = ไม่ชัด, extinct = สูญพันธุ์, instinctive = ตามสัญชาตญาณ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Reduced passive participle clause","th":"อนุประโยคลดรูปแบบ passive (V3)"},"topic":{"en":"Reduced and participle clauses","th":"อนุประโยคลดรูปและวลี participle"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"______ in 1928, penicillin was not produced on a large scale until the 1940s.\"","choices":["It was discovered","Discovering","Discovered","Having discovered"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Penicillin was discovered (passive), so the reduced clause uses the past participle \"Discovered\". \"Having discovered\" and \"Discovering\" are active, making penicillin the discoverer; \"It was discovered\" creates two main clauses with no linking word.","th":"เพนิซิลลินถูกค้นพบ จึงใช้รูป passive ลดรูปเป็น Discovered ส่วน Having discovered และ Discovering เป็น active ทำให้เพนิซิลลินเป็นผู้ค้นพบ และ It was discovered ทำให้มีสองประโยคหลักโดยไม่มีคำเชื่อม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inversion after 'Not until'","th":"inversion หลัง Not until"},"topic":{"en":"Inversion","th":"การสลับที่ประธานกับกริยา (inversion)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"Not until the late nineteenth century ______ that bacteria cause many common diseases.\"","choices":["did scientists widely accept","had widely accepted scientists","scientists widely accepted","widely accepted scientists did"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A sentence that begins with \"Not until …\" needs question-like word order in the main clause: auxiliary + subject + verb, so \"did scientists widely accept\". The normal order is wrong here, and the other two choices put the words in an impossible order.","th":"ประโยคที่ขึ้นด้วย Not until ต้องสลับเป็นรูปคำถามในประโยคหลัก คือ กริยาช่วย + ประธาน + กริยา จึงเป็น did scientists widely accept ส่วนการเรียงแบบปกติผิด และอีกสองแบบเรียงคำผิดหลัก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Mixed conditional (past condition, present result)","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบผสม"},"topic":{"en":"Subjunctive and conditionals","th":"subjunctive และประโยคเงื่อนไข"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"If the bridge ______ according to the original design, it would still be standing today.\"","choices":["was built","had been built","were built","would have been built"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The building happened in the past, but the result (\"would still be standing today\") is in the present, so this is a mixed conditional: past perfect in the if-clause, \"had been built\". \"Were built\" and \"was built\" refer to the present or are not unreal, and \"would\" does not belong in the if-clause.","th":"การสร้างสะพานเกิดในอดีตแต่ผลอยู่ในปัจจุบัน จึงเป็นเงื่อนไขแบบผสม ใช้ past perfect ใน if-clause คือ had been built ส่วน were built และ was built ไม่ได้อ้างถึงอดีตที่ไม่จริง และไม่ใช้ would ใน if-clause"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Countable noun with 'number of'","th":"ใช้ number of กับนามนับได้"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and comparison","th":"ความสอดคล้องประธาน–กริยาและการเปรียบเทียบ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\nThe [[amount of]] students applying for science scholarships [[has risen]] steadily [[over]] the past decade, [[according to]] the ministry.","choices":["amount of","has risen","over","according to"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Students\" is a countable plural noun, so it takes \"number of\", not \"amount of\" (used with uncountable nouns). \"The number of … has risen\" is then correct.","th":"students เป็นนามนับได้ จึงต้องใช้ number of ไม่ใช่ amount of (ใช้กับนามนับไม่ได้) และ The number of ... has risen ถูกต้อง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Passive voice with past participle","th":"passive ต้องใช้ V3"},"topic":{"en":"Reduced and participle clauses","th":"อนุประโยคลดรูปและวลี participle"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\n[[Having]] been ignored for decades, the theory [[was]] finally [[accepting]] by the scientific community [[after]] new evidence emerged.","choices":["Having","was","accepting","after"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The theory was accepted by the community, so the passive needs the past participle: \"was finally accepted\". \"Accepting\" makes the theory the one doing the accepting.","th":"ทฤษฎีถูกยอมรับโดยวงการวิทยาศาสตร์ จึงต้องใช้ passive คือ was accepted ส่วน accepting ทำให้ทฤษฎีเป็นผู้ยอมรับเอง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Dangling participle","th":"ส่วนขยายลอย"},"topic":{"en":"Dangling modifiers","th":"ส่วนขยายลอย (dangling modifier)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\n[[Walking]] through the ancient city, the ruins of the temple [[seemed]] to the archaeologists [[far]] more impressive [[than]] they had expected.","choices":["Walking","seemed","far","than"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"As written, \"the ruins\" are walking through the city. The opening must be changed, for example to \"As the archaeologists walked\", so that it refers to the people. The other underlined words are correct.","th":"ประโยคนี้ทำให้ the ruins กลายเป็นผู้เดิน จึงต้องแก้ส่วนต้น เช่น As the archaeologists walked เพื่อให้อ้างถึงคน ส่วนคำอื่นที่ขีดเส้นใต้ถูกต้อง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Main idea","th":"ใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: science","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: วิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"Sleep and the Making of Memories\n\nFor much of the twentieth century, sleep was regarded as a passive state: the brain, it was assumed, simply switched off for the night and recovered from the efforts of the day. Over the past few decades, however, this view has been largely abandoned. Researchers now believe that sleep plays an active role in learning, and in particular in the process known as memory consolidation, by which fragile new memories are transformed into stable, long-lasting ones.\n\nSome of the most persuasive evidence comes from studies in which participants learn a task, such as a list of word pairs or a sequence of finger movements, and are then tested after a period of either sleep or wakefulness. Again and again, those who have slept perform better, even when the two periods are of equal length. Crucially, the advantage does not seem to come merely from being rested. Participants who learn in the evening and sleep immediately afterwards tend to outperform those who learn in the morning and are tested in the evening, although both groups are equally alert at the time of testing.\n\nRecordings of brain activity have suggested a possible mechanism. During deep, slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus, a region essential for forming new memories, appears to \"replay\" patterns of activity that occurred during learning. These replays are thought to strengthen connections in the cortex, where long-term memories are eventually stored. In experiments with rats that had explored a maze, the same sequences of neurons that fired as the animals ran were observed firing again, in compressed form, while they slept.\n\nNot all findings fit neatly into this picture. Some studies have found only small benefits of sleep for certain kinds of memory, and the size of the effect varies considerably between individuals. It also remains unclear whether different stages of sleep support different kinds of learning, as some researchers have proposed. Rapid eye movement (REM) sleep, for example, has been linked to emotional memory and creative problem-solving, but the evidence is less consistent than for slow-wave sleep.\n\nNevertheless, the practical implications are hard to ignore. Students who stay up all night before an examination may be sacrificing precisely the process that would help them retain what they have studied. A night without sleep after learning appears to impair recall even when the lost sleep is \"made up\" on the following nights, which suggests that the timing of sleep, and not simply its total amount, matters. For anyone whose work depends on learning, sleep may be less a luxury than a part of the job itself.","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","choices":["the reasons why students should avoid studying in the evening","how rats learn to find their way through mazes","the differences between REM sleep and slow-wave sleep","evidence that sleep actively helps the brain to store new memories"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The passage moves from the old view of sleep as passive to research showing that sleep helps consolidate memories, so the main topic is \"evidence that sleep actively helps the brain to store new memories\". REM sleep and the rat experiment are only supporting details, and the passage actually reports good results for evening learners.","th":"บทความเริ่มจากความเชื่อเดิมว่าการนอนเป็นภาวะเฉื่อย แล้วเสนองานวิจัยว่าการนอนช่วยตรึงความจำ ใจความหลักจึงเป็นหลักฐานว่าการนอนช่วยสมองเก็บความจำใหม่ ส่วนเรื่อง REM และการทดลองกับหนูเป็นเพียงรายละเอียดสนับสนุน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Reference","th":"การอ้างอิงคำ"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: psychology","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: จิตวิทยา"},"passage":"Why Witnesses Hesitate\n\nCommon sense suggests that a person in trouble is more likely to receive help when many people are around: the more witnesses there are, the greater the chance that at least one of them will act. Yet a large body of research in social psychology points to a different conclusion. In many situations, the presence of other people makes each individual less likely to intervene, a phenomenon known as the bystander effect.\n\nIn a classic series of experiments conducted in the late 1960s, participants were placed in situations that appeared to be genuine emergencies. In one version, a participant filling in a questionnaire noticed smoke entering the room through a vent. When alone, most participants left to report the smoke within a few minutes. When they sat with two other people who had secretly been instructed to ignore it, the majority continued working, waving the smoke away from their faces, for the entire session.\n\nPsychologists have offered several explanations for this pattern. The first is the diffusion of responsibility. When a person is the only witness, the responsibility to act falls entirely on him or her; when there are many witnesses, it is shared, and each person feels it less heavily. A second explanation concerns the way people interpret ambiguous situations. Faced with uncertainty, we tend to look at others to decide how to behave. If everyone else appears calm, each observer concludes that nothing serious is happening, even though the others may be equally uncertain and simply hiding their concern. A third factor is the fear of embarrassment: intervening in front of an audience carries the risk of appearing foolish if the situation turns out not to be an emergency after all.\n\nIt would be a mistake, however, to conclude that crowds are always indifferent. A large review of studies published in 2011 found that the bystander effect is weaker when the danger is obvious and severe, perhaps because there is no ambiguity to resolve and because a group can share the physical risk of intervening. More recently, researchers analysing video footage of real public conflicts in several countries found that in the great majority of cases at least one bystander did intervene, and that the likelihood of someone helping increased as the number of bystanders rose.\n\nThese findings do not so much contradict the earlier research as refine it. The bystander effect describes how the probability that any single individual will help changes in a group; it does not mean that groups as a whole are unhelpful. Knowing about the effect may itself offer some protection. People who have learned about it, studies suggest, are more likely to recognise the moment of hesitation for what it is and to step forward.","stem":"In the third paragraph, the word \"it\" in \"each person feels it less heavily\" refers to","choices":["the emergency","the presence of other people","the responsibility to act","the number of witnesses"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The sentence contrasts how \"the responsibility to act\" falls on one witness with how it is shared among many; it is this responsibility that each person feels less heavily.","th":"ประโยคเปรียบเทียบว่าความรับผิดชอบในการลงมือช่วยตกอยู่กับพยานคนเดียว หรือถูกแบ่งกันในหมู่คนหลายคน สิ่งที่แต่ละคนรู้สึกเบาลงจึงคือ the responsibility to act"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Paragraph function","th":"หน้าที่ของย่อหน้า/ประโยค"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: economics","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: เศรษฐศาสตร์"},"passage":"More Roads, More Traffic\n\nWhen a busy road becomes congested, the obvious solution seems to be to build more lanes. If a road that carries 4,000 vehicles an hour is widened to carry 6,000, the reasoning goes, traffic will flow freely and journey times will fall. Yet transport economists have observed for decades that this reasoning often fails in practice. Within a few years of a road being widened, it is frequently just as congested as before. The explanation lies in a concept known as induced demand.\n\nDemand for road space, like demand for most goods, depends partly on its price. The price of a car journey is not only the cost of fuel; it also includes the time the journey takes. When a road is congested, this time cost is high, and many potential trips are never made. Some people travel at quieter hours, some use public transport, some combine errands, and some simply decide that a trip is not worth the effort. Adding capacity lowers the time cost of driving, at least at first, and these \"suppressed\" trips begin to appear. People who used to take the train switch to driving, others move to houses further from their workplaces, and businesses locate along the improved route. Gradually the new space fills up.\n\nA widely cited study of American cities found that between the 1980s and the 2000s the total distance driven increased almost exactly in proportion to the length of new roads built. In other words, a 10 percent increase in road capacity was associated with roughly a 10 percent increase in driving. The researchers concluded that building roads is unlikely to be an effective long-term remedy for congestion, at least in large cities where demand is high.\n\nThe idea also works in reverse. When roads are closed or their capacity is reduced, the chaos that planners predict often fails to materialise. Some of the traffic, sometimes called \"evaporated\" traffic, simply disappears as people change their routes, times or means of travel. Several cities that have removed elevated highways and replaced them with parks or boulevards have reported that overall traffic in the area decreased rather than spilling onto neighbouring streets.\n\nNone of this means that new roads are never justified. A road connecting two towns that previously had no direct link may bring considerable economic benefits, and in rural areas where demand is low, extra capacity may indeed reduce delays. But in dense urban areas, many economists argue that congestion is better managed by pricing road use directly, for example through charges for driving into city centres at peak times, and by investing in alternatives that allow people to travel without a car.","stem":"What is the function of the fourth paragraph?","choices":["It explains how the American study was carried out.","It introduces a criticism of the concept of induced demand.","It shows that the same principle explains what happens when road capacity is reduced.","It describes the economic benefits of building elevated highways."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"The idea also works in reverse\": the paragraph applies the same principle to road closures, where traffic \"evaporates\". It supports the concept rather than criticising it.","th":"ประโยค The idea also works in reverse แสดงว่าย่อหน้านี้ใช้หลักการเดียวกันกับกรณีลดถนน ซึ่งปริมาณรถหายไป ย่อหน้านี้สนับสนุนแนวคิด ไม่ได้วิจารณ์"}}]}},{"exam":"sp4","set":2,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.4 English — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes in a standardized-test style: academic vocabulary in context, sentence structure, error identification and academic reading (about introductory TU-GET / CU-TEP level). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที แนวข้อสอบมาตรฐาน: คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท โครงสร้างประโยค หาจุดผิด และการอ่านบทความวิชาการ (ระดับใกล้ TU-GET / CU-TEP เบื้องต้น) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"sp4-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: conservative","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: conservative"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: adjectives and adverbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำคุณศัพท์และคำวิเศษณ์"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Early estimates of the damage turned out to be far too ______; the true cost was almost three times higher.\"","choices":["excessive","exaggerated","precise","conservative"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"A \"conservative\" estimate is cautiously low, which fits a figure that turned out to be much lower than the true cost. \"Exaggerated\" and \"excessive\" mean too high, the opposite, and \"precise\" contradicts the large error.","th":"conservative estimate คือการประมาณการแบบระมัดระวังซึ่งมักต่ำกว่าความจริง ตรงกับที่ค่าเสียหายจริงสูงกว่าเกือบสามเท่า ส่วน exaggerated และ excessive แปลว่าสูงเกินไป (ตรงข้าม) และ precise ขัดกับความคลาดเคลื่อนที่มาก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: contradictory","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: contradictory"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: adjectives and adverbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำคุณศัพท์และคำวิเศษณ์"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The two studies reached ______ conclusions: one found the drug effective, while the other found no benefit at all.\"","choices":["contradictory","consistent","complementary","comprehensive"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"One study found benefit and the other none, so the conclusions were \"contradictory\" (opposed to each other). \"Complementary\" and \"consistent\" suggest they agree or fit together; \"comprehensive\" means thorough.","th":"งานหนึ่งพบว่ายาได้ผล อีกงานไม่พบประโยชน์ ข้อสรุปจึง contradictory (ขัดแย้งกัน) ส่วน complementary และ consistent สื่อว่าสอดคล้องเสริมกัน และ comprehensive แปลว่า ครอบคลุม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: driven","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: driven"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: verbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The company's profits fell sharply, ______ largely by rising energy costs and weak demand.\"","choices":["deprived","driven","deterred","derived"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"\"Driven by\" means caused by: the fall in profits was caused by costs and weak demand. \"Derived\" takes \"from\", \"deprived\" takes \"of\", and \"deterred\" (discouraged) needs a person or action as its object.","th":"driven by แปลว่า เกิดจาก/ขับเคลื่อนโดย กำไรลดลงเพราะต้นทุนและอุปสงค์ต่ำ ส่วน derived ใช้กับ from, deprived ใช้กับ of และ deterred แปลว่า ยับยั้งไม่ให้ทำ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past participle clause expressing reason","th":"วลี V3 บอกเหตุผล (ความรู้สึก)"},"topic":{"en":"Reduced and participle clauses","th":"อนุประโยคลดรูปและวลี participle"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"______ by the speed of the results, the team decided to repeat the experiment.\"","choices":["Surprising","Surprised","Having surprised","To surprise"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The team was surprised (passive feeling), so the participle clause uses \"Surprised by …\". \"Surprising\" and \"Having surprised\" would mean the team surprised someone else, and \"To surprise\" expresses purpose.","th":"ทีมรู้สึกประหลาดใจ (ถูกทำให้แปลกใจ) จึงใช้ Surprised by ส่วน Surprising และ Having surprised หมายถึงทีมทำให้คนอื่นแปลกใจ และ To surprise บอกจุดประสงค์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Parallelism with 'not only … but also'","th":"โครงสร้างคู่ขนานกับ not only ... but also"},"topic":{"en":"Parallel structure","th":"โครงสร้างคู่ขนาน (parallelism)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"Effective science communication requires not only accuracy but also ______.\"","choices":["being clearly","that it is clear","to be clear","clarity"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Not only accuracy\" is a noun, so \"but also\" must be followed by a noun: \"clarity\". The infinitive, the -ing phrase and the clause are not parallel.","th":"หลัง not only เป็นคำนาม accuracy ดังนั้นหลัง but also ต้องเป็นคำนามเช่นกัน คือ clarity ส่วน to-infinitive, วลี -ing และอนุประโยคไม่ขนานกัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Noun clause with 'whether … or'","th":"noun clause แบบ whether ... or"},"topic":{"en":"Noun clauses","th":"noun clause (อนุประโยคนาม)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"It is still debated ______ the ancient city was abandoned suddenly or declined over many decades.\"","choices":["whether","what","if or not","that"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The clause presents two alternatives (\"suddenly or … over many decades\"), so \"whether … or\" is needed. \"That\" states a fact rather than an open question, \"what\" needs a gap in the clause, and \"if or not\" is not standard.","th":"อนุประโยคเสนอทางเลือกสองทาง จึงต้องใช้ whether ... or ส่วน that ทำให้เป็นข้อเท็จจริงแทนคำถาม what ต้องมีช่องว่างในอนุประโยค และ if or not ไม่ใช่รูปมาตรฐาน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"'Despite' without 'of'","th":"despite ไม่ใช้ of"},"topic":{"en":"Word form and usage","th":"รูปคำและการใช้คำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\n[[Despite of]] the heavy rain, the field researchers [[managed]] to collect [[all]] the samples they [[needed]].","choices":["Despite of","managed","all","needed"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Despite\" is followed directly by a noun: \"Despite the heavy rain\" (or \"In spite of the heavy rain\"). \"Despite of\" is incorrect.","th":"despite ตามด้วยคำนามได้ทันที คือ Despite the heavy rain หรือใช้ In spite of ส่วน Despite of ผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Fixed preposition: 'similar to'","th":"บุพบทคงที่ similar to"},"topic":{"en":"Word form and usage","th":"รูปคำและการใช้คำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\nScientists [[have long]] debated whether animals [[are capable of]] feeling emotions [[similar with]] [[those]] of humans.","choices":["have long","are capable of","similar with","those"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The adjective \"similar\" takes \"to\": \"similar to those of humans\". \"Those\" correctly replaces the plural \"emotions\".","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ similar ใช้กับ to คือ similar to those of humans ส่วน those ใช้แทน emotions ที่เป็นพหูพจน์ได้ถูกต้อง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Agreement with 'neither … nor'","th":"ความสอดคล้องกับ neither ... nor"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and comparison","th":"ความสอดคล้องประธาน–กริยาและการเปรียบเทียบ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\n[[Although]] the survey was anonymous, neither the principal nor the teachers [[was]] willing to [[share]] the results [[with]] parents.","choices":["Although","was","share","with"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"With \"neither … nor\", the verb agrees with the nearer subject. \"The teachers\" is plural, so the verb must be \"were\".","th":"กับ neither ... nor กริยาสอดคล้องกับประธานตัวที่อยู่ใกล้กว่า คือ the teachers ซึ่งเป็นพหูพจน์ จึงต้องใช้ were"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Main idea","th":"ใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: environment","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: สิ่งแวดล้อม"},"passage":"Wolves, Elk and a Famous Ecological Story\n\nWhen grey wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in 1995 and 1996, after an absence of roughly seventy years, few people anticipated that the return of a single predator would become one of the most famous stories in modern ecology. Within a decade, scientists were reporting changes that seemed to ripple through the entire ecosystem. Elk, the wolves' main prey, had declined in number and, it was argued, changed their behaviour, avoiding the open river valleys where they were most vulnerable. Freed from constant grazing, willow and aspen trees along the riverbanks began to recover. Beavers, which depend on willow, returned to areas they had abandoned, and their dams created ponds that supported fish, amphibians and birds.\n\nThis chain of effects is an example of what ecologists call a trophic cascade: a process in which a change at the top of a food chain spreads downward through the levels below it. The Yellowstone case was quickly adopted as a powerful illustration of the importance of large predators, and it was widely used to support the argument that restoring missing species can repair damaged ecosystems, an approach often referred to as rewilding.\n\nIn recent years, however, a number of researchers have questioned whether the story is quite so simple. One difficulty is that wolves were not the only factor changing at the time. Yellowstone experienced a series of droughts, human hunting of elk outside the park increased, and populations of other predators, including bears and cougars, were also growing. Separating the influence of wolves from these other forces is extremely difficult. Another difficulty concerns the so-called \"landscape of fear\". Studies that tracked elk with GPS collars found that the animals did not consistently avoid risky areas in the way the original theory suggested, raising doubts about whether fear of wolves alone could explain the recovery of trees.\n\nCritics have also pointed out that the recovery of vegetation has been patchy. In some places willows have grown tall; in others they remain short. One explanation is that the streams themselves have changed. When beavers disappeared, their dams collapsed, the water table dropped and the streams cut deeper channels. Willows growing high above the water may now be unable to reach it, regardless of how many elk are present. If so, removing a predator may cause changes that cannot be undone simply by bringing it back.\n\nNone of this means that wolves have had no effect. Most ecologists accept that they have influenced elk numbers and, through them, parts of the ecosystem. The debate is about the size and reliability of the effects. Its lesson may be that ecosystems are rarely governed by a single cause, and that compelling stories, however useful for winning public support, should not substitute for careful evidence.","stem":"What is the main point of the passage?","choices":["Wolves should not have been reintroduced to Yellowstone because they harmed the ecosystem.","Beavers, rather than wolves, are the most important animals in Yellowstone.","The widely told story of how wolves transformed Yellowstone is now considered more complicated than it first appeared.","Trophic cascades occur only in national parks with large predators."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The passage tells the famous trophic-cascade story and then spends three paragraphs on reasons why it is \"not quite so simple\". The author never says the reintroduction was harmful and states that wolves have had some effect.","th":"บทความเล่าเรื่องดังเรื่อง trophic cascade แล้วใช้สามย่อหน้าอธิบายว่าเรื่องนี้ไม่ง่ายอย่างที่คิด ผู้เขียนไม่ได้บอกว่าการนำหมาป่ากลับมาเป็นผลเสีย และยอมรับว่าหมาป่ามีผลอยู่บ้าง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Reference","th":"การอ้างอิงคำ"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: psychology","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: จิตวิทยา"},"passage":"Rethinking the Marshmallow Test\n\nIn a series of studies that began at Stanford University in the late 1960s, young children were offered a simple choice. A researcher placed a treat, such as a marshmallow, in front of the child and explained that she had to leave the room for a while. If the child waited until she returned, he or she would receive two treats; if not, the child could ring a bell at any time and eat the single treat immediately. Some children waited the full fifteen minutes or so; others gave in after a few seconds.\n\nThe experiment became famous because of what happened later. Follow-up studies, published in the late 1980s and 1990s, reported that children who had waited longer tended, as adolescents, to have higher test scores, to cope better with stress, and to be rated by their parents as more competent. The conclusion seemed clear and appealing: the ability to delay gratification is a stable personal trait that appears early in life and predicts success. The \"marshmallow test\" entered popular culture, and books, parenting guides and school programmes urged adults to train children's willpower.\n\nYet the original follow-up samples were small and unusual. Most of the children were the sons and daughters of staff and students at an elite university. In 2018 a team of researchers attempted to repeat the key finding with a much larger and more varied group of more than 900 children. They did find a link between waiting time and later academic achievement, but it was about half the size of the original estimate. More importantly, once they took into account factors such as family income, the mother's education and the child's early cognitive ability, the link became very small indeed.\n\nThis result suggests a different interpretation. Children's decisions to wait may reflect not only their self-control but also their circumstances. A child who has grown up in an environment where promises are reliably kept has good reason to believe that a second marshmallow will really arrive. A child whose experience has taught that resources can disappear may quite reasonably take what is available now. Other experiments support this view: when children were first exposed to an adult who broke a small promise, they waited far less time than children whose adult had kept it.\n\nIt would be wrong to conclude that self-control does not matter. The newer findings, however, cast doubt on the idea that a few minutes of behaviour in a laboratory reveal a fixed quality of character. They also suggest that efforts to improve children's lives may achieve more by making their environments stable and trustworthy than by teaching them simply to resist temptation.","stem":"In the third paragraph, the word \"it\" in \"it was about half the size of the original estimate\" refers to","choices":["family income","the group of more than 900 children","the original follow-up sample","the link between waiting time and later academic achievement"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The previous clause says \"They did find a link between waiting time and later academic achievement, but it was about half the size\": the link is what was smaller.","th":"อนุประโยคก่อนหน้ากล่าวถึง a link between waiting time and later academic achievement สิ่งที่มีขนาดเพียงครึ่งหนึ่งจึงคือความเชื่อมโยงนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Paragraph function","th":"หน้าที่ของย่อหน้า/ประโยค"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: economics","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: เศรษฐศาสตร์"},"passage":"Beyond the Tragedy of the Commons\n\nIn 1968 the ecologist Garrett Hardin published an essay entitled \"The Tragedy of the Commons\", which became one of the most cited works in the social sciences. Hardin asked his readers to imagine a pasture open to all. Each herder, acting rationally, gains the full benefit of adding one more animal to the pasture, while the cost of overgrazing is shared among everyone. As a result, each herder keeps adding animals until the pasture is ruined. \"Freedom in a commons,\" Hardin concluded, \"brings ruin to all.\" Since the users of a shared resource could not be expected to restrain themselves, he argued, the resource must either be divided into private property or controlled by the state.\n\nHardin's argument was elegant, and it seemed to explain a range of real problems, from the collapse of fisheries to air pollution. However, it rested on an assumption that the political economist Elinor Ostrom spent much of her career examining: that the users of a common resource are unable to communicate and make rules for themselves. Ostrom and her colleagues studied hundreds of cases around the world, including Swiss alpine meadows, Japanese forests, irrigation systems in Nepal and fishing grounds in Turkey. Many of these resources had been managed sustainably by local communities for generations, in some instances for centuries, without either privatisation or central control.\n\nFrom these cases Ostrom identified a set of conditions that successful communities tended to share. The boundaries of the resource and the group entitled to use it were clearly defined. The people affected by the rules could take part in changing them. Users' behaviour was monitored, often by the users themselves, and those who broke the rules faced penalties that began mildly and increased for repeated offences. Disputes could be resolved quickly and cheaply, and outside authorities recognised the community's right to organise itself.\n\nOstrom did not claim that communities always succeed; she documented many failures as well. Nor did she reject private property or government regulation, which she regarded as suitable in some circumstances. Her objection was to the belief that any single solution could be applied everywhere. Policies imposed from outside, she found, sometimes destroyed working local arrangements and left resources worse managed than before.\n\nIn 2009 Ostrom became the first woman to receive the Nobel Prize in Economic Sciences. Her work has influenced the management of resources from groundwater to the internet, and it has encouraged researchers to ask not whether shared resources are doomed, but under what conditions people manage to cooperate.","stem":"What is the main function of the fourth paragraph?","choices":["to list the conditions shared by successful communities","to describe Ostrom's Nobel Prize","to clarify the limits of Ostrom's claims and prevent a misunderstanding of her position","to argue that governments should never regulate resources"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The paragraph says what Ostrom did not claim: that communities always succeed or that private property and regulation are wrong. Her objection was only to one-size-fits-all solutions. This prevents readers from overstating her view.","th":"ย่อหน้านี้บอกสิ่งที่ Ostrom ไม่ได้อ้าง คือไม่ได้บอกว่าชุมชนสำเร็จเสมอ หรือปฏิเสธกรรมสิทธิ์เอกชนและการควบคุมของรัฐ เธอค้านเพียงการใช้วิธีเดียวกับทุกที่ จึงเป็นการป้องกันการเข้าใจผิด"}}]}},{"exam":"sp4","set":3,"title":{"en":"Satit Prasarnmit M.4 English — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 สาธิตประสานมิตร — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes in a standardized-test style: academic vocabulary in context, sentence structure, error identification and academic reading (about introductory TU-GET / CU-TEP level). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที แนวข้อสอบมาตรฐาน: คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท โครงสร้างประโยค หาจุดผิด และการอ่านบทความวิชาการ (ระดับใกล้ TU-GET / CU-TEP เบื้องต้น) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"sp4-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: unsubstantiated","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: unsubstantiated"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: adjectives and adverbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำคุณศัพท์และคำวิเศษณ์"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The author's claims are largely ______; she offers anecdotes but almost no systematic evidence.\"","choices":["unprecedented","uncontroversial","unambiguous","unsubstantiated"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Unsubstantiated\" means not supported by evidence, which the second clause confirms (anecdotes but no systematic evidence). \"Unprecedented\" means never seen before, \"uncontroversial\" means not disputed and \"unambiguous\" means clear.","th":"unsubstantiated แปลว่า ไม่มีหลักฐานรองรับ ตรงกับประโยคหลังที่บอกว่ามีแต่เรื่องเล่าแต่ไม่มีหลักฐานเป็นระบบ ส่วน unprecedented = ไม่เคยมีมาก่อน, uncontroversial = ไม่เป็นที่ถกเถียง, unambiguous = ชัดเจน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: seminal","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: seminal"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: adjectives and adverbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำคุณศัพท์และคำวิเศษณ์"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Her early work was ______ to the field: almost every later study builds on it.\"","choices":["terminal","nominal","marginal","seminal"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Seminal\" describes work that strongly influences later developments, as \"almost every later study builds on it\" shows. \"Marginal\" means of little importance, \"nominal\" means existing in name only and \"terminal\" means final or fatal.","th":"seminal ใช้กับงานที่เป็นรากฐานและมีอิทธิพลต่องานรุ่นหลัง ตรงกับที่งานวิจัยภายหลังเกือบทั้งหมดต่อยอดจากงานนี้ ส่วน marginal = ไม่สำคัญ, nominal = แค่ในนาม, terminal = สุดท้าย/ถึงตาย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Academic word in context: subtle","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการในบริบท: subtle"},"topic":{"en":"Academic vocabulary: adjectives and adverbs","th":"คำศัพท์วิชาการ: คำคุณศัพท์และคำวิเศษณ์"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Because the effect was so ______, it could only be detected with extremely sensitive instruments.\"","choices":["conspicuous","profound","volatile","subtle"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Only very sensitive instruments could detect it, so the effect was \"subtle\" (small and hard to notice). \"Conspicuous\" means easy to see, \"profound\" means very great, and \"volatile\" means likely to change suddenly.","th":"ต้องใช้เครื่องมือที่ไวมากจึงตรวจพบ ผลจึง subtle (เล็กน้อย สังเกตยาก) ส่วน conspicuous = เห็นได้ชัด, profound = ลึกซึ้ง/มาก, volatile = ผันผวน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Inversion after 'Only when'","th":"inversion หลัง Only when"},"topic":{"en":"Inversion","th":"การสลับที่ประธานกับกริยา (inversion)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"Only when the samples had been analysed a second time ______ the error in the original calculations.\"","choices":["the team did notice","did the team notice","noticed the team","the team noticed"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A sentence that begins with \"Only when …\" needs inversion in the main clause: \"did the team notice\". Normal word order (with or without \"did\") is wrong here.","th":"ประโยคที่ขึ้นด้วย Only when ต้องสลับเป็น กริยาช่วย + ประธาน ในประโยคหลัก คือ did the team notice การเรียงแบบปกติผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Parallelism with 'not only … but also'","th":"คู่ขนานกับ not only ... but also"},"topic":{"en":"Parallel structure","th":"โครงสร้างคู่ขนาน (parallelism)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"The new treatment is effective not only in reducing pain but also ______.\"","choices":["shortened recovery time","it shortens recovery time","to shorten recovery time","in shortening recovery time"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"\"Not only in reducing pain\" is a prepositional phrase with an -ing form, so \"but also\" needs the same structure: \"in shortening recovery time\".","th":"หลัง not only เป็นวลี in + V-ing (in reducing pain) หลัง but also จึงต้องเป็นโครงสร้างเดียวกัน คือ in shortening recovery time"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Mixed conditional (past condition, present result)","th":"เงื่อนไขแบบผสม"},"topic":{"en":"Subjunctive and conditionals","th":"subjunctive และประโยคเงื่อนไข"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\n\"If the ancient Egyptians ______ such careful records, historians would know far less about daily life along the Nile.\"","choices":["had not kept","have not kept","would not have kept","did not keep"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The record-keeping was in the past, but the result (\"would know\") is in the present: a mixed conditional with past perfect in the if-clause, \"had not kept\". \"Did not keep\" suggests an unreal present, and \"would\" does not belong in the if-clause.","th":"การบันทึกเกิดในอดีต แต่ผลอยู่ในปัจจุบัน (would know) จึงเป็นเงื่อนไขผสม ใช้ past perfect คือ had not kept ส่วน did not keep สื่อปัจจุบันที่ไม่จริง และไม่ใช้ would ใน if-clause"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"'Despite' vs 'although'","th":"despite กับ although"},"topic":{"en":"Word form and usage","th":"รูปคำและการใช้คำ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\n[[Despite]] the evidence was compelling, many economists [[remained]] sceptical, [[largely]] because the data [[had been]] collected in a single region.","choices":["Despite","remained","largely","had been"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"\"Despite\" is a preposition and cannot introduce a clause with a subject and verb (\"the evidence was compelling\"). It must be changed to \"Although\" (or \"Despite the fact that\").","th":"despite เป็นบุพบท ตามด้วยอนุประโยคที่มีประธานและกริยาไม่ได้ ต้องเปลี่ยนเป็น Although หรือ Despite the fact that"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Result clause of an inverted third conditional","th":"ผลของเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 ที่สลับ Had"},"topic":{"en":"Subjunctive and conditionals","th":"subjunctive และประโยคเงื่อนไข"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\n[[Had]] the warning been [[taken]] seriously, the authorities [[could evacuate]] the town [[before]] the flood arrived.","choices":["Had","taken","could evacuate","before"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"\"Had the warning been taken\" is an inverted third conditional about the past, so the result must be \"could have evacuated\". \"Could evacuate\" refers to the present or future.","th":"Had the warning been taken เป็นเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 ที่สลับประธาน (อดีต) ผลลัพธ์จึงต้องเป็น could have evacuated ส่วน could evacuate ใช้กับปัจจุบันหรืออนาคต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Agreement with 'each of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องกับ each of"},"topic":{"en":"Agreement and comparison","th":"ความสอดคล้องประธาน–กริยาและการเปรียบเทียบ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that must be changed for the sentence to be correct.\n[[In]] the trial, each of the volunteers [[were]] given [[either]] the drug [[or]] a placebo.","choices":["In","were","either","or"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The subject is \"each\", which is singular, so the verb must be \"was given\" even though \"volunteers\" is plural.","th":"ประธานคือ each ซึ่งเป็นเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น was given แม้ volunteers จะเป็นพหูพจน์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Author's purpose","th":"จุดประสงค์ของผู้เขียน"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: history of ideas","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: ประวัติแนวคิด"},"passage":"The Doctor Who Asked for Clean Hands\n\nIn the 1840s the maternity clinic of Vienna General Hospital was divided into two divisions. In the first, women were attended by doctors and medical students; in the second, by midwives. The difference in outcomes was striking and widely known: in some years, the death rate from puerperal fever, an infection that struck women shortly after childbirth, was several times higher in the doctors' division than in the midwives'. Women reportedly begged to be admitted to the second division, and some preferred to give birth in the street rather than risk the first.\n\nIgnaz Semmelweis, a young Hungarian doctor working in the clinic, set out to find the cause. He eliminated one explanation after another; the divisions were, for example, similarly crowded, so crowding could not account for the difference. The decisive clue came in 1847, when a colleague died after being cut accidentally during an autopsy, with symptoms remarkably similar to those of the dying mothers. Semmelweis concluded that doctors and students, who often came directly from dissecting bodies, were carrying \"cadaverous particles\" on their hands to the women they examined. Midwives performed no autopsies. When he required everyone entering the division to wash their hands in a chlorine solution, the death rate fell dramatically within months, to a level comparable with that of the midwives.\n\nOne might expect such a result to have been welcomed immediately. Instead, Semmelweis's ideas were largely rejected, and he died in 1865 in an asylum, his work still unrecognised. Historians have proposed several reasons. The theory that diseases are spread by microscopic living organisms had not yet been established, so his explanation lacked a convincing mechanism and appeared to contradict the dominant view that disease arose from bad air or imbalances within the body. Many doctors also found the implication that they themselves were responsible for their patients' deaths deeply offensive. Semmelweis did not help his own cause. He published a full account of his findings only in 1861, many years after his discovery, and responded to critics with open hostility, accusing some of them in letters of being murderers.\n\nThe episode is often told as a simple tale of a lone genius defeated by prejudice. That reading is not entirely wrong, but it obscures something important. Scientific communities are, and arguably should be, cautious about accepting claims that cannot be explained by existing knowledge, since most such claims turn out to be mistaken. The tragedy was that caution hardened into refusal even in the face of strong statistical evidence that lives were being lost. Semmelweis was eventually vindicated when, in the following decades, the work of Louis Pasteur and Joseph Lister established the germ theory of disease. The term \"Semmelweis reflex\" is now sometimes used to describe the tendency to reject new evidence that contradicts established beliefs.","stem":"What is the author's main purpose in the final paragraph?","choices":["to describe the experiments of Pasteur and Lister in detail","to show that Semmelweis's statistics were unreliable","to offer a more balanced interpretation of why Semmelweis's work was rejected","to argue that scientists should accept every new claim without question"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The author says the \"lone genius defeated by prejudice\" reading \"obscures something important\": caution is reasonable, but it \"hardened into refusal\" despite strong evidence. This is a more balanced interpretation. The author does not say all new claims should be accepted, and calls the evidence strong.","th":"ผู้เขียนบอกว่าการเล่าเรื่องแบบอัจฉริยะผู้โดดเดี่ยวที่แพ้อคติ บดบังประเด็นสำคัญ คือความระมัดระวังเป็นเรื่องสมเหตุสมผล แต่กลายเป็นการปฏิเสธทั้งที่หลักฐานชัด จึงเป็นการตีความที่สมดุลขึ้น ผู้เขียนไม่ได้บอกให้รับทุกข้ออ้าง และยืนยันว่าหลักฐานแข็งแรง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Paragraph function","th":"หน้าที่ของย่อหน้า/ประโยค"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: psychology","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: จิตวิทยา"},"passage":"Language and the Shape of Thought\n\nDoes the language we speak shape the way we think? The idea that it does, known as linguistic relativity, is most closely associated with the American linguist Benjamin Lee Whorf, who argued in the 1930s and 1940s that speakers of different languages perceive and understand the world in fundamentally different ways. Whorf's most famous claim concerned the Hopi language of Arizona, which, he asserted, contained no words or grammatical forms referring to time as a flowing quantity. Hopi speakers, he concluded, must therefore conceive of time very differently from speakers of European languages.\n\nWhorf's ideas proved enormously influential, but by the 1970s they had fallen out of favour among many linguists and psychologists. One reason was that his evidence turned out to be weak. A detailed study of Hopi published in 1983 described a rich system of time expressions, which seriously undermined Whorf's best-known example. Another reason was the rise of the view that the human capacity for language rests on universal foundations shared by all people, which made claims of deep cognitive differences seem implausible. Critics also noted a logical difficulty: if a concept could only be grasped by those whose language expressed it, how could Whorf himself have described the Hopi view of time in English?\n\nSince the 1990s, however, the question has returned in a more modest and testable form. Rather than asking whether language determines thought, researchers now ask whether it influences particular habits of attention and memory. Some of the most striking evidence comes from communities that describe space without words like \"left\" and \"right\". Speakers of Guugu Yimithirr, an Aboriginal language of northern Australia, describe the positions of objects using compass directions: a cup is said to be to the north of the plate, not to its left. Such speakers turn out to maintain an extraordinarily accurate sense of orientation, even in unfamiliar places, and when asked to rearrange objects after turning around, they tend to preserve the objects' compass positions rather than their positions relative to the body.\n\nColour perception provides further, though smaller, effects. Russian has two basic words for what English speakers call \"blue\": one for lighter shades and one for darker ones. In laboratory tasks, Russian speakers distinguish between shades that cross this boundary slightly faster than between shades that fall within the same category. Intriguingly, this advantage disappears when they are asked to repeat a string of numbers silently during the task, an activity that occupies the verbal system.\n\nFew researchers today would defend the strong version of Whorf's hypothesis. Speakers of every language can learn new concepts, and translation, though imperfect, is possible. Yet the evidence suggests that the habitual patterns of a language may train its speakers to notice some features of the world more readily than others. The debate has shifted from whether language affects thought to how, how much, and in which domains.","stem":"What is the function of the \"logical difficulty\" mentioned at the end of the second paragraph?","choices":["It introduces the modern research described in the third paragraph.","It shows that English is superior to Hopi for describing time.","It suggests that Whorf's strong claim undermines itself.","It explains why Whorf studied the Hopi language."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"If concepts could be grasped only through a language that expresses them, Whorf could not have explained the Hopi concept in English. His own description therefore counts against his strong claim, so the claim undermines itself.","th":"ถ้าแนวคิดเข้าใจได้เฉพาะผ่านภาษาที่มีคำนั้น Whorf ก็ไม่น่าจะอธิบายแนวคิดเรื่องเวลาของชาวโฮปีเป็นภาษาอังกฤษได้ การอธิบายของเขาเองจึงขัดกับข้ออ้างของเขา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"sp4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-academic","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Paragraph function","th":"หน้าที่ของย่อหน้า/ประโยค"},"topic":{"en":"Academic reading: economics","th":"อ่านบทความวิชาการ: เศรษฐศาสตร์"},"passage":"When a Measure Becomes a Target\n\nIn 1975 the British economist Charles Goodhart observed that statistical relationships used by governments to guide policy tended to break down once they were used as targets. His point, originally made about monetary policy, has since been generalised into what is now known as Goodhart's law, often summarised as: \"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.\" A similar principle was proposed independently by the social scientist Donald Campbell, who warned that the more any quantitative indicator is used for social decision-making, the more it will be subject to corrupting pressures.\n\nThe logic is straightforward. A measure is useful because it is correlated with something we care about but cannot observe directly. The number of patients waiting more than four hours in a hospital emergency department, for instance, tells us something about the quality of emergency care. But as soon as people are rewarded or punished according to the number, they have an incentive to change the number itself, whether or not they change the underlying reality. Hospitals under pressure to meet waiting-time targets have been reported to keep patients waiting in ambulances outside the building, where the clock had not yet started, or to move patients to wards at the last moment simply to stop it.\n\nExamples can be found in almost every field. When schools are judged by the proportion of students who pass a particular examination, teachers may concentrate on the skills the examination tests and neglect others, or encourage weaker students to avoid the subject altogether. When researchers are evaluated by the number of articles they publish, some divide a single study into several smaller papers. A frequently repeated, though poorly documented, story tells of a colonial government in India that offered a reward for every dead cobra, only to find that people had begun breeding cobras to collect it. Whether or not the story is true, it has given its name to a whole class of problems: the \"cobra effect\".\n\nIt would be easy to conclude from such cases that measurement is pointless, or even harmful. This would be a misreading. Organisations that refuse to measure anything are left to rely on impressions, which are themselves vulnerable to bias. The lesson is rather that indicators should be used with an awareness of how people will respond to them. Several strategies have been suggested: using a number of different measures rather than a single one, so that improving one at the expense of others becomes visible; changing measures periodically; combining numbers with qualitative judgment; and, perhaps most importantly, using measures to prompt questions rather than to distribute rewards and punishments automatically.\n\nGoodhart's law is, in the end, a statement about human intelligence. People are not passive objects of measurement; they understand the rules by which they are judged and adapt to them, often in ways the designers did not foresee. Any system of targets that ignores this is likely to produce precisely the behaviour it was intended to prevent.","stem":"The fourth paragraph mainly serves to","choices":["explain the origin of Goodhart's law in monetary policy","provide further examples of measures that have been corrupted","warn against drawing too extreme a conclusion from the examples and suggest a better approach","argue that organisations should stop measuring performance"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"It begins \"It would be easy to conclude … that measurement is pointless … This would be a misreading\" and then lists strategies for using measures wisely. It rejects, rather than argues, that organisations should stop measuring.","th":"ย่อหน้าเริ่มด้วยการเตือนว่าการสรุปว่าการวัดไร้ประโยชน์เป็นการเข้าใจผิด แล้วเสนอแนวทางการใช้ตัวชี้วัดอย่างรอบคอบ จึงไม่ได้สนับสนุนให้เลิกวัด"}}]}},{"exam":"tu4","set":1,"title":{"en":"Triam Udom Suksa M.4 English — Practice paper 1","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 เตรียมอุดมศึกษา — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes in the Triam Udom style: cloze and reading at the core, error identification (collocations), and conversation. Tutor summaries report about 40 questions in recent years. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที แนวเตรียมอุดมฯ: เติมคำในบทความและการอ่านเป็นแกนหลัก หาจุดผิด (เน้น collocation) และบทสนทนา (ข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชาระบุราว 40 ข้อในปีหลัง ๆ) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":true,"file":"tu4-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Granting a polite request","th":"การตอบรับคำขออย่างสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: I've been meaning to ask — would you mind if I borrowed your notes from the chemistry lecture?\nB: ______ I'll bring them in tomorrow.\nA: Thanks, you're a lifesaver.","explanation":{"en":"'Would you mind if…?' is answered 'Not at all' when you agree, and B then promises to bring the notes. 'Yes, I would' means B does mind, which contradicts the next sentence; 'Never mind' and 'It's up to me' do not answer the request.","th":"คำถาม Would you mind if…? ถ้ายินยอมต้องตอบ Not at all (ไม่รังเกียจเลย) ซึ่งเข้ากับประโยคต่อไปที่ B สัญญาว่าจะเอาสมุดมาให้ ส่วน Yes, I would แปลว่ารังเกียจ จึงขัดกับประโยคถัดไป Never mind และ It's up to me ไม่ได้ตอบคำขอ"},"choices":["Not at all.","Yes, I would.","It's up to me.","Never mind."],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Giving a balanced report on an outcome","th":"การเล่าผลที่ทั้งดีและไม่ดี"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: How did the debate go? You looked so nervous beforehand.\nB: ______ We lost in the final round, but the judges said our rebuttal was the strongest of the day.","explanation":{"en":"B lost but received praise, so 'It could have been worse' fits a mixed result. 'It was a piece of cake' suggests an easy win, 'I couldn't agree more' agrees with an opinion, and 'Better late than never' is about lateness.","th":"B แพ้แต่ได้รับคำชม จึงใช้ It could have been worse (ก็ไม่ได้แย่ที่สุด) เหมาะกับผลที่ปนกัน It was a piece of cake หมายถึงง่ายมาก I couldn't agree more ใช้เห็นด้วยกับความคิดเห็น และ Better late than never พูดเรื่องการมาช้า"},"choices":["It could have been worse.","I couldn't agree more.","Better late than never.","It was a piece of cake."],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Questioning someone's decision tactfully","th":"การทักท้วงการตัดสินใจอย่างนุ่มนวล"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: I'm thinking of dropping out of the science club — it's taking up too much of my time.\nB: ______ You've been talking about the national competition all year.\nA: I know, but my grades are starting to suffer.","explanation":{"en":"B reminds A of the competition A was excited about, so B is gently questioning the decision: 'Are you sure that's wise?' 'That's the spirit!' and 'Go for it' encourage A to quit, and 'Rather you than me' means B would not want to do it, which does not lead into B's reminder.","th":"B เตือนว่า A พูดถึงการแข่งขันระดับชาติมาทั้งปี แปลว่า B ทักท้วงอย่างนุ่มนวลว่า Are you sure that's wise? ส่วน That's the spirit! และ Go for it สนับสนุนให้ลาออก และ Rather you than me (ถ้าเป็นฉันคงไม่ทำ) ไม่เชื่อมกับประโยคที่ B เตือนถัดมา"},"choices":["Rather you than me.","Are you sure that's wise?","Go for it — you won't regret it.","That's the spirit!"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Collocation: apply for / apply to","th":"collocation: apply for / apply to"},"topic":{"en":"Collocation","th":"คำปรากฏร่วม (collocation)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: After months of hesitation, Nattapong finally [[applied to]] the scholarship, [[which]] covers tuition [[as well as]] [[living]] expenses.","choices":["applied to","which","as well as","living"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"You 'apply for' something you hope to receive (a scholarship, a job, a visa) and 'apply to' an organisation (a university, a company). It should be 'applied for the scholarship'.","th":"ขอรับสิ่งที่ต้องการได้ (ทุน งาน วีซ่า) ใช้ apply for ส่วนยื่นต่อองค์กร (มหาวิทยาลัย บริษัท) ใช้ apply to จึงต้องเป็น applied for the scholarship"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Uncountable noun with few / little","th":"คำนามนับไม่ได้กับ few / little"},"topic":{"en":"Countable and uncountable nouns","th":"คำนามนับได้และนับไม่ได้"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Before you [[set off]], make sure you have [[enough]] water, because the trail [[is]] steep and there is [[few]] shade along the way.","choices":["set off","enough","is","few"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Shade' is uncountable, so it takes 'little', not 'few': 'there is little shade'.","th":"shade เป็นคำนามนับไม่ได้ ต้องใช้ little ไม่ใช่ few จึงเป็น there is little shade"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Collocation: make an error","th":"collocation: make an error"},"topic":{"en":"Collocation","th":"คำปรากฏร่วม (collocation)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Investigators [[concluded]] that the error, [[though]] minor, had [[been]] [[done]] by a technician who had worked a double shift.","choices":["concluded","though","been","done"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Errors and mistakes are 'made', not 'done': 'had been made by a technician'.","th":"error และ mistake ใช้กับกริยา make ไม่ใช่ do จึงต้องเป็น had been made by a technician"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Collocation: condemn something as","th":"collocation: condemn … as"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: news / feature article","th":"เติมคำในบทความข่าว/สารคดี"},"passage":"When Bangkok's city authorities announced plans to clear food vendors from several busy pavements, the reaction was swift. Food writers and ordinary residents alike (1) ______ the move as an attack on one of the city's most valuable traditions. Officials, on the other hand, insisted that the decision had been made (2) ______ public safety rather than appearance: pedestrians, they argued, were being forced into the road because the stalls took up so much space. The debate soon became (3) ______ political. For many, it raised the question of who a city is really for — the tourists who photograph its markets or the workers who (4) ______ a living from them. Eventually a compromise was reached. Vendors were allowed to stay, (5) ______ they kept a marked lane clear for pedestrians and closed their stalls by midnight.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (1)?","explanation":{"en":"Only 'condemn' fits the pattern 'condemn X as Y'. We 'blame someone for', 'accuse someone of' and 'complain about', so none of them can be followed by 'the move as'.","th":"มีเพียง condemn ที่ใช้รูป condemn X as Y ได้ ส่วน blame ใช้กับ for, accuse ใช้กับ of และ complain ใช้กับ about จึงตามด้วย the move as ไม่ได้"},"choices":["condemned","accused","complained","blamed"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verb: dwell on","th":"phrasal verb: dwell on"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: short story","th":"เติมคำในเรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"My grandfather was never one to (6) ______ on the war years, and as children we learned not to ask. So when a parcel arrived from Japan addressed to him, the whole family gathered round, curious and a little (7) ______. Inside was a battered wooden box and a letter written in careful English. The writer explained that her father had kept the box for sixty years, (8) ______ that one day he would find its owner. My grandfather turned it over in his hands for a long time. Then, to our astonishment, he began to talk — about a bridge, a storm, and a young soldier who had shared his last cup of rice with a stranger. (9) ______ had he finished than the room fell completely silent. It was as if he had been waiting all his life for someone to (10) ______ him permission to remember.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (6)?","explanation":{"en":"'Dwell on' means to keep thinking or talking about something unpleasant, which suits a grandfather who avoided the war. 'Depend on', 'count on' and 'rely on' mean to trust or need, which makes no sense here.","th":"dwell on แปลว่าครุ่นคิดหรือพูดถึงเรื่องไม่สบายใจซ้ำ ๆ เหมาะกับคุณตาที่ไม่อยากพูดเรื่องสงคราม ส่วน depend on, count on และ rely on แปลว่าพึ่งพา ซึ่งไม่เข้ากับความหมาย"},"choices":["depend","rely","dwell","count"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verb: bear out","th":"phrasal verb: bear out"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: opinion article","th":"เติมคำในบทความแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"Thai students are often said to be among the most hard-working in the region, and the hours many of them spend in tutoring schools would seem to (11) ______ this reputation. Yet hard work is not the same as effective learning. A student who memorises a hundred model answers may score well on a test (12) ______ the questions are familiar, but struggle the moment a problem is phrased in an unexpected way. The real goal of education, surely, is not to fill students' heads with facts but to develop minds (13) ______ of coping with the unfamiliar. (14) ______ this is achieved, longer hours at a desk will only produce tired students, not capable ones. Schools that have shifted towards discussion and projects have found that exam results, far from (15) ______, often improve.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (11)?","explanation":{"en":"'Bear out' means to confirm or support, which is what long tutoring hours do to a reputation for hard work. 'Carry on' means continue, 'put off' means postpone, and 'take over' means gain control of.","th":"bear out แปลว่ายืนยันหรือสนับสนุน ชั่วโมงเรียนพิเศษที่ยาวจึงยืนยันชื่อเสียงเรื่องความขยัน ส่วน carry on แปลว่าทำต่อ put off แปลว่าเลื่อน และ take over แปลว่าเข้าควบคุม"},"choices":["put off","bear out","take over","carry on"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Main idea of a news report","th":"ใจความหลักของข่าว"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: news report","th":"การอ่าน: รายงานข่าว"},"passage":"CHIANG RAI — A secondary school in northern Thailand has become the latest to abandon traditional end-of-year examinations, replacing them with projects that students must complete together with members of the local community.\n\nUnder the scheme, introduced at Ban Huai Sai School this term, Mathayom 4 students work in teams to tackle a problem identified by village leaders. Projects so far have included a study of water quality in a nearby stream, a website for a weaving cooperative and a survey of elderly residents' access to health care. Each team presents its findings to a panel that includes teachers, parents and the village headman.\n\nDirector Somchai Kaewmanee said the change had been \"a long time coming\". \"For years we tested whether students could remember what we had told them,\" he said. \"Now we are asking whether they can use it.\"\n\nNot everyone is convinced. Some parents fear that their children will be at a disadvantage when they sit national university entrance tests, which remain heavily based on multiple-choice questions. \"It's a lovely idea,\" said one mother, who asked not to be named, \"but lovely ideas don't get you into medical school.\"\n\nEducation researchers are also cautious. Dr Anong Prasert, who studies school assessment, noted that similar schemes elsewhere had produced mixed results. \"Projects can be transformative when teachers are trained to assess them fairly,\" she said. \"Without that training, the marks tend to reward whoever speaks most confidently on presentation day.\"\n\nThe school says it has addressed this concern by giving every teacher a week of training and by publishing its marking criteria in advance. Early signs are encouraging: attendance has risen by eight per cent since the scheme began, and the weaving cooperative reports that its online orders have doubled.\n\nWhether the approach will survive the pressure of university admissions, however, remains to be seen.","stem":"What is the report mainly about?","explanation":{"en":"The whole report describes the school replacing end-of-year exams with community projects, and the reactions to it. The other choices are either details twisted out of context or not stated.","th":"ข่าวทั้งเรื่องเล่าถึงโรงเรียนที่เปลี่ยนจากการสอบปลายปีมาเป็นโครงงานกับชุมชน และปฏิกิริยาต่อการเปลี่ยนแปลงนี้ ตัวเลือกอื่นบิดเบือนรายละเอียดหรือไม่มีในข่าว"},"choices":["A school's decision to assess students through community projects instead of exams","A parents' campaign to bring back multiple-choice examinations","A weaving cooperative that has started employing secondary school students","A university study proving that projects improve entrance-test results"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Interpreting a simile","th":"การตีความการเปรียบเทียบ (simile)"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: short story","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"Mali had practised the piece so many times that her fingers seemed to know it better than she did. For six months she had woken at five, played for an hour before school, and played again until her mother called her to dinner. Her teacher, Khun Wipa, rarely praised anyone, so when she said, \"You might actually win this,\" Mali carried the words around like a coin in her pocket.\n\nOn the morning of the competition, the hall smelled of floor polish and nerves. The boy who played before her was perhaps nine years old. He walked to the piano as if he were crossing his own living room, sat down, and played a simple folk song — badly, in places, but with such obvious delight that several people in the audience began to smile.\n\nThen it was Mali's turn. She played every note exactly as she had rehearsed it. Not one finger slipped. When she finished, the applause was polite and brief, and she noticed one of the judges glancing at his watch.\n\nShe came second. The nine-year-old came first.\n\n\"It isn't fair,\" she told Khun Wipa afterwards, her voice shaking. \"He made at least five mistakes.\"\n\nHer teacher was quiet for a moment. \"Six,\" she said. \"I counted too.\" She looked out of the window at the boy, who was now chasing his little sister round the car park, his medal bouncing on its ribbon. \"Tell me, Mali — when did you last enjoy playing that piece?\"\n\nMali opened her mouth to answer, and found that she could not remember.\n\nThat evening she did not practise. Instead, she sat at the piano and played the first song she had ever learned, a silly tune about a duck, and she played it far too fast, and laughed when she got it wrong.","stem":"The phrase \"carried the words around like a coin in her pocket\" suggests that Mali ______.","explanation":{"en":"A coin in your pocket is something small and valuable you keep with you and touch from time to time. Because Khun Wipa 'rarely praised anyone', the words were precious to Mali.","th":"เหรียญในกระเป๋าเป็นของเล็ก ๆ มีค่าที่พกติดตัวและหยิบจับอยู่เรื่อย ๆ เพราะครูวิภา 'แทบไม่เคยชมใคร' คำชมจึงมีค่ามากสำหรับมะลิ"},"choices":["soon forgot what her teacher had said","felt she had paid for the praise with her hard work","treasured the rare praise and kept returning to it","was afraid someone would take her prize away"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Recognising sarcasm","th":"การรู้ทันการเสียดสี"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: critical review","th":"การอ่าน: บทวิจารณ์"},"passage":"There is a moment about forty minutes into The Last Monsoon when the camera rises slowly over a flooded rice field at dawn, and for a few seconds the film becomes genuinely beautiful. Enjoy it. It does not happen again.\n\nDirector Krit Anan has clearly been told that he is a visual artist, and he has believed every word. Almost every scene is framed like a postcard — rain on lotus leaves, a lantern reflected in a puddle, an old woman's hands sorting grain — and the effect, after two hours and twenty minutes, is less like watching a story than like being trapped inside a very expensive calendar.\n\nThe story, such as it is, follows a hydrologist who returns to her home village to warn its residents that a new dam upstream will flood their land. It is an important subject, and the film knows it. Characters do not so much talk to each other as deliver short lectures on water management, and every villager who doubts the heroine is revealed, within a scene or two, to be either foolish or corrupt.\n\nThis is a pity, because the lead actress, Rasa Thongdee, deserves better. In the few moments when the script allows her to be uncertain — a phone call to her estranged father, a late-night argument with her younger brother — she suggests a person far more interesting than the saint the film wants her to be. One wishes the director had trusted her, and his audience, with a little more doubt.\n\nThe Last Monsoon will no doubt win prizes; it has the look of a film that wins prizes. Whether anyone will remember it a year from now is another matter. Those who care about the issues it raises would be better served by a good documentary — or, failing that, by watching the first forty minutes and leaving at dawn.","stem":"\"Director Krit Anan has clearly been told that he is a visual artist, and he has believed every word.\" The tone of this sentence is best described as ______.","explanation":{"en":"'Believed every word' mocks the director's vanity: the reviewer implies his love of pretty images has taken over the film. This is sarcasm, not sympathy or neutral reporting.","th":"believed every word เป็นการเย้ยหยันความหลงตัวเองของผู้กำกับ ผู้วิจารณ์บอกเป็นนัยว่าความหลงใหลในภาพสวยครอบงำหนังทั้งเรื่อง จึงเป็นการเสียดสี ไม่ใช่ความเห็นใจหรือการรายงานกลาง ๆ"},"choices":["admiring of the director's confidence in himself","sympathetic, suggesting that others misled the director","sarcastic, implying that the director is too pleased with his own visual style","neutral, reporting a fact from an interview"],"answer":2}]}},{"exam":"tu4","set":2,"title":{"en":"Triam Udom Suksa M.4 English — Practice paper 2","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 เตรียมอุดมศึกษา — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes in the Triam Udom style: cloze and reading at the core, error identification (collocations), and conversation. Tutor summaries report about 40 questions in recent years. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที แนวเตรียมอุดมฯ: เติมคำในบทความและการอ่านเป็นแกนหลัก หาจุดผิด (เน้น collocation) และบทสนทนา (ข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชาระบุราว 40 ข้อในปีหลัง ๆ) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"tu4-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Agreeing to a favour","th":"การรับปากช่วยเหลือ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: Could you keep an eye on my bag while I grab a coffee?\nB: ______ Take your time.","explanation":{"en":"'Take your time' shows B agrees, so 'Sure, no problem' fits. 'I'd rather not say' refuses to give information, 'Keep the change' is said when paying, and 'It's not my cup of tea' means B does not like something.","th":"Take your time แสดงว่า B ยินดีช่วย จึงตอบ Sure, no problem ส่วน I'd rather not say คือไม่อยากบอก Keep the change ใช้ตอนจ่ายเงิน และ It's not my cup of tea แปลว่าไม่ชอบสิ่งนั้น"},"choices":["I'd rather not say.","It's not my cup of tea.","Keep the change.","Sure, no problem."],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Accepting an apology","th":"การรับคำขอโทษ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: I'm sorry I snapped at you earlier. I'd had a terrible morning.\nB: ______ We all have days like that.","explanation":{"en":"'We all have days like that' is forgiving, so B accepts the apology: 'Don't worry about it.' 'You should be' is cold, 'Same to you' returns a wish, and 'That's very kind of you' thanks someone for help.","th":"We all have days like that เป็นการให้อภัย B จึงตอบ Don't worry about it ส่วน You should be ฟังดูเย็นชา Same to you ใช้ตอบคำอวยพร และ That's very kind of you ใช้ขอบคุณเมื่อได้รับความช่วยเหลือ"},"choices":["That's very kind of you.","Same to you.","You should be.","Don't worry about it."],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Offering to deal with a problem","th":"การอาสาจัดการปัญหา"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: The new manager wants everyone to stay late on Friday.\nB: Friday? But that's the night of the concert!\nA: ______ I'll see if I can swap shifts with someone.","explanation":{"en":"A then promises to try to swap shifts, so A is offering to handle the problem: 'Leave it with me.' 'Suit yourself' sounds indifferent, 'Over my dead body' is a strong refusal, and 'That's beside the point' dismisses B's worry.","th":"A บอกต่อว่าจะลองขอแลกกะ แปลว่า A อาสาจัดการให้ จึงพูดว่า Leave it with me ส่วน Suit yourself ฟังดูไม่ใส่ใจ Over my dead body เป็นการปฏิเสธรุนแรง และ That's beside the point เป็นการปัดความกังวลของ B"},"choices":["Over my dead body.","Leave it with me.","Suit yourself.","That's beside the point."],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"for / since with a period of time","th":"for / since กับช่วงเวลา"},"topic":{"en":"Tenses","th":"กาล (tense)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My sister [[has been]] [[working]] at the hospital [[since]] five years and [[still]] enjoys it.","choices":["has been","working","since","still"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Five years' is a period of time, so it takes 'for'. 'Since' is used with a starting point, such as 'since 2021'.","th":"five years เป็นช่วงระยะเวลา ต้องใช้ for ส่วน since ใช้กับจุดเริ่มต้น เช่น since 2021"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Dependent preposition: married to","th":"บุพบทที่ตามมา: married to"},"topic":{"en":"Prepositions","th":"คำบุพบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My aunt, [[who]] [[is married with]] a surgeon, [[rarely]] sees her husband [[before]] midnight.","choices":["who","is married with","rarely","before"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"In English we are 'married to' someone, not 'married with' them.","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษใช้ married to someone ไม่ใช่ married with"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Not only … but also with inversion","th":"Not only … but also กับการสลับที่"},"topic":{"en":"Inversion","th":"การสลับที่ประธานกับกริยา (inversion)"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Not only [[did]] the storm [[damage]] the harvest, [[but also it destroyed]] several homes [[along]] the coast.","choices":["did","damage","but also it destroyed","along"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"After an inverted 'Not only' clause, the second clause keeps normal order with 'also' after the subject: 'but it also destroyed' (or 'but also destroyed').","th":"เมื่อประโยคแรกขึ้นต้นด้วย Not only แบบสลับที่แล้ว ประโยคหลังเรียงตามปกติ โดย also อยู่หลังประธาน เป็น but it also destroyed (หรือ but also destroyed)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Word form: adverb before a participle","th":"รูปคำ: adverb ขยาย participle"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: news / feature article","th":"เติมคำในบทความข่าว/สารคดี"},"passage":"Maya Bay, the beach made famous by a Hollywood film, was closed to visitors in 2018 after years of mass tourism had left its coral reefs (1) ______ damaged. At its peak, the bay received several thousand visitors a day, and boats regularly dropped anchor directly on the reef. Scientists warned that, (2) ______ urgent action was taken, the damage could become permanent. The closure, initially intended to last a few months, was (3) ______ extended several times. During that period, marine biologists planted thousands of young corals, and blacktip reef sharks, which had all but (4) ______ from the area, began to return. When the bay finally reopened, strict rules had been (5) ______ place: swimming was banned, visitor numbers were capped and boats had to dock on the far side of the island.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (1)?","explanation":{"en":"The past participle 'damaged' is modified by an adverb: 'severely damaged'. 'Severe' is an adjective, 'severity' a noun, and 'sever' a verb meaning 'cut off'.","th":"past participle damaged ต้องขยายด้วย adverb จึงเป็น severely damaged ส่วน severe เป็น adjective, severity เป็นคำนาม และ sever เป็นกริยาแปลว่าตัดขาด"},"choices":["severity","sever","severe","severely"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Idiom: pluck up the courage","th":"สำนวน: pluck up the courage"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: short story","th":"เติมคำในเรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"The letter had been sitting on the kitchen table for three days before Arthit (6) ______ up the courage to open it. He already knew what it would say — or thought he did. His older brother had been accepted by the same university without difficulty, and his parents (7) ______ that he would follow. As he unfolded the page, his hands were trembling so much that he had to read the first line twice. \"We regret to inform you…\" He sat down slowly. Strangely, the feeling that (8) ______ over him was not disappointment but something closer to relief. For years he had been preparing for a future that (9) ______ never really been his own. Now, for the first time, he would have to decide what he actually wanted — a prospect that was as (10) ______ as it was frightening.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (6)?","explanation":{"en":"The idiom is 'pluck up the courage' (find enough bravery to do something). 'Pull up', 'pick up' and 'bring up' do not combine with 'the courage' in this way.","th":"สำนวนคือ pluck up the courage (รวบรวมความกล้า) ส่วน pull up, pick up และ bring up ไม่ใช้กับ the courage ในความหมายนี้"},"choices":["plucked","picked","brought","pulled"],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Comparative with uncountable nouns: less","th":"การเปรียบเทียบกับนามนับไม่ได้: less"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: opinion article","th":"เติมคำในบทความแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"Every year the average consumer buys more clothes and keeps them for (11) ______ time than ever before. Fast-fashion brands, which can move a design from the catwalk to the shop floor in a matter of weeks, are often blamed for this, and (12) ______. But it is too easy to point the finger solely at companies. Shoppers, after all, are not forced to buy a new outfit for every party; they choose to. If we are serious about reducing waste, we need to (13) ______ our own habits as well as demanding change from manufacturers. That does not mean giving up fashion altogether. Buying second-hand, repairing rather than replacing, and simply (14) ______ fewer but better items could make a considerable difference. The problem, in other words, is not that we enjoy clothes — it is that we have stopped (15) ______ them.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (11)?","explanation":{"en":"'Time' is uncountable and 'than' signals a comparative, so 'less time than'. 'Fewer' is for countable nouns, 'lesser' means 'smaller in importance', and 'least' is superlative.","th":"time เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ และมี than แสดงการเปรียบเทียบ จึงใช้ less time than ส่วน fewer ใช้กับนามนับได้ lesser แปลว่าสำคัญน้อยกว่า และ least เป็นขั้นสูงสุด"},"choices":["fewer","least","less","lesser"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Locating key information","th":"การหาข้อมูลสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: news report","th":"การอ่าน: รายงานข่าว"},"passage":"BANGKOK — Motor vehicles will be banned from a two-kilometre stretch of the city's historic Rattanakosin district every Sunday from next month, under a six-month trial announced by city officials on Tuesday.\n\nThe scheme will close several roads near the Grand Palace to private cars, taxis and motorcycles between 7 a.m. and 7 p.m., turning them over to pedestrians, cyclists and street performers. Residents will be issued permits allowing them to drive to and from their homes at walking pace, and a free electric shuttle bus will link the zone to the nearest metro stations.\n\nOfficials say the trial is intended to reduce air pollution and make the area more pleasant for the millions of tourists who visit each year. \"Our old town was built for people, not engines,\" a deputy governor told reporters. \"For one day a week, we want to give it back to them.\"\n\nThe announcement drew a mixed response. Shop owners on one of the affected roads, where many businesses rely on deliveries, said they had not been consulted. \"Of course we want clean air,\" said one restaurant owner. \"But if suppliers cannot reach us on our busiest day, the clean air will be very quiet.\"\n\nTaxi drivers' associations have also raised objections, arguing that the closures will simply push traffic onto surrounding roads. Transport researchers say that risk is real but often overstated. Studies of similar schemes in other Asian cities have found that congestion in neighbouring streets typically rises for the first few weeks before settling below its original level, as drivers change their routes or leave their cars at home.\n\nCity officials have promised to review the trial after three months and say they are open to adjusting delivery hours. Whether the scheme becomes permanent, they added, will depend largely on what residents and businesses make of it.","stem":"According to the report, what will happen on Sundays during the trial?","explanation":{"en":"The first two paragraphs say cars, taxis and motorcycles will be banned from a two-kilometre stretch between 7 a.m. and 7 p.m., with exceptions for residents. It is not the whole city, and it is the shuttle bus, not the metro, that is free.","th":"สองย่อหน้าแรกบอกว่ารถยนต์ แท็กซี่ และมอเตอร์ไซค์ถูกห้ามเข้าช่วงถนนสองกิโลเมตร ตั้งแต่ 7 โมงเช้าถึง 1 ทุ่ม ยกเว้นผู้อยู่อาศัย ไม่ใช่ทั้งเมือง และที่ฟรีคือรถรับส่งไฟฟ้า ไม่ใช่รถไฟฟ้าใต้ดิน"},"choices":["Metro trains will run free of charge to the Grand Palace.","All roads in Bangkok will be closed to private cars.","Tourists will need permits to enter the Rattanakosin district.","Most motor vehicles will be kept out of part of the old town during the day."],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Interpreting a descriptive phrase","th":"การตีความวลีบรรยาย"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: short story","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"At twelve, Nok was the best English speaker in her family, which meant that whenever her father had to deal with the outside world, Nok went with him. She translated letters from the bank, phone calls from the landlord and, once, a long argument with a man who had reversed into their car.\n\nShe was proud of the job, mostly. It made her feel grown-up, and adults sometimes looked at her with a kind of surprised respect. But there were moments when she wished she could simply be a child who did not understand.\n\nThe worst of these came on a grey Tuesday in the doctor's office. The doctor spoke quickly, glancing at her screen rather than at Nok's father. Nok caught words she knew — \"results\", \"further tests\" — and others she did not. Then the doctor said, \"I'm afraid it's not good news,\" and looked, finally, at Nok, waiting.\n\nHer father was watching her face. Nok understood, in that instant, that whatever she said next would become true for him in a way it was not yet true for her.\n\n\"She says they need to do more tests,\" Nok said in Thai. \"She says not to worry yet.\"\n\nIt was not exactly a lie. The doctor had said something about tests. Her father nodded, and some of the tightness left his shoulders.\n\nOn the bus home he bought her a mango smoothie, the expensive kind, and talked about football. Nok held the cup with both hands and did not drink it. She was thinking about the word \"yet\", and about how much weight one small word could carry, and about the second appointment, next Thursday, when she would have to choose again.","stem":"What does \"a kind of surprised respect\" suggest about the adults' reaction to Nok?","explanation":{"en":"'Surprised' shows they did not expect a twelve-year-old to handle adult business; 'respect' shows they admired her for doing it.","th":"คำว่า surprised แสดงว่าผู้ใหญ่ไม่คาดว่าเด็กอายุสิบสองจะจัดการเรื่องของผู้ใหญ่ได้ ส่วน respect แสดงว่าพวกเขาชื่นชมเธอ"},"choices":["They were annoyed that a child was interrupting them.","They felt sorry for her because she had to miss school.","They were impressed that someone so young could take on such responsibility.","They did not believe she could really speak English."],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Understanding a criticism of evidence","th":"การเข้าใจคำวิจารณ์เรื่องการใช้หลักฐาน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: critical review","th":"การอ่าน: บทวิจารณ์"},"passage":"Daniel Reyes wakes at four-thirty every morning, and he would very much like you to know it. In Ten Thousand Mornings, his third book on what he calls \"deliberate living\", Reyes argues that the hours before dawn are the key to a successful life, and that almost anyone who claims to be too busy to achieve their goals is simply sleeping through the solution.\n\nThere is something here worth taking seriously. Reyes is good on the value of routine, and his chapter on protecting time for difficult work — switching off notifications, saying no to unnecessary meetings — contains advice that many readers will find useful. His prose is clear and occasionally funny, particularly when he describes his own early failures, such as the morning he fell asleep in the middle of a meditation session and woke with his face in a bowl of oatmeal.\n\nThe problems begin when Reyes moves from describing what works for him to prescribing what should work for everyone. His readers, it seems, are assumed to have flexible jobs, no young children and no need to work a night shift. A single mother who writes to say that she cannot possibly rise at four-thirty is gently told that she has \"not yet found her why\". One suspects she has found it; it is asleep in the next room.\n\nReyes cites a great deal of research, but rather selectively. Studies that support early rising are described in detail, while the considerable evidence that people's natural sleep patterns differ — and that forcing an early schedule on a \"night owl\" can harm health — appears only in a brief footnote.\n\nReaders who already enjoy early mornings will find much here to confirm what they believe. Those who do not may wish to read it in the afternoon, which is, after all, when most of us are properly awake.","stem":"What is the reviewer's criticism of Reyes's use of research?","explanation":{"en":"Supporting studies 'are described in detail', while contrary evidence 'appears only in a brief footnote' — that is selective use of evidence.","th":"งานวิจัยที่สนับสนุน 'ถูกอธิบายอย่างละเอียด' ส่วนหลักฐานที่ขัดแย้ง 'อยู่แค่ในเชิงอรรถสั้น ๆ' จึงเป็นการเลือกใช้หลักฐานแบบลำเอียง"},"choices":["He misunderstands studies that support early rising.","He gives detail to evidence that supports him and minimises evidence against him.","He relies only on studies of night-shift workers.","He does not cite any research at all."],"answer":1}]}},{"exam":"tu4","set":3,"title":{"en":"Triam Udom Suksa M.4 English — Practice paper 3","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษ สอบเข้า ม.4 เตรียมอุดมศึกษา — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"50 questions in 60 minutes in the Triam Udom style: cloze and reading at the core, error identification (collocations), and conversation. Tutor summaries report about 40 questions in recent years. DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"50 ข้อ 60 นาที แนวเตรียมอุดมฯ: เติมคำในบทความและการอ่านเป็นแกนหลัก หาจุดผิด (เน้น collocation) และบทสนทนา (ข้อมูลจากสถาบันกวดวิชาระบุราว 40 ข้อในปีหลัง ๆ) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":60,"kinds":{"m1":50},"subjects":{"eng":50},"free":false,"file":"tu4-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Accepting an offer politely","th":"การรับข้อเสนออย่างสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: Would you like me to give you a lift to the station?\nB: ______ I'd hate to take you out of your way.\nA: It's no trouble — I'm heading that way anyway.","explanation":{"en":"B is grateful but worried about inconveniencing A, and A reassures B. 'That's kind of you, but are you sure?' fits both. 'No, I wouldn't like that at all' is rude, 'I'll give you one too' misunderstands the offer, and 'I'm heading that way too' does not lead to A's reassurance.","th":"B รู้สึกขอบคุณแต่เกรงใจว่าจะทำให้ A ต้องอ้อม และ A ก็ยืนยันว่าไม่ลำบาก That's kind of you, but are you sure? จึงเหมาะที่สุด ส่วน No, I wouldn't like that at all ไม่สุภาพ I'll give you one too เข้าใจข้อเสนอผิด และ I'm heading that way too ไม่สอดคล้องกับคำตอบของ A"},"choices":["No, I wouldn't like that at all.","Don't bother — I'm heading that way too.","That's kind of you, but are you sure?","Yes, I'll give you one too."],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Making a suggestion that fits the reply","th":"การเสนอแนะให้สอดคล้องกับคำตอบ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: everyday response","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบโต้ในชีวิตประจำวัน"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: Have you finished the group report?\nB: Not quite. Ton was supposed to write the conclusion, but he's been ill.\nA: ______\nB: I'd rather not. It's his section, and he said he'd send it tonight.","explanation":{"en":"B's answer 'I'd rather not' must reply to a suggestion, and the reason ('It's his section') shows the suggestion was to write Ton's part: 'Should we write it ourselves, then?' The other questions cannot be answered with 'I'd rather not'.","th":"คำตอบ I'd rather not ต้องตอบข้อเสนอ และเหตุผล It's his section แสดงว่าข้อเสนอคือการเขียนส่วนของต้นแทน จึงเป็น Should we write it ourselves, then? ส่วนคำถามอื่นตอบด้วย I'd rather not ไม่ได้"},"choices":["Is he feeling any better?","Should we write it ourselves, then?","Why didn't you tell me sooner?","Has he sent it to you already?"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Interpreting an indirect answer","th":"การตีความคำตอบอ้อม ๆ"},"topic":{"en":"Conversation: implied meaning","th":"บทสนทนา: ความหมายแฝง"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the most appropriate response to complete the conversation.\nA: How was the job interview?\nB: Put it this way: I don't think I'll be buying a new suit for my first day.\nA: ______","explanation":{"en":"B indirectly says there will be no first day, meaning the interview went badly. A sympathises: 'I'm sorry it didn't go well.' The other replies take B's words literally and miss the implication.","th":"B บอกอ้อม ๆ ว่าคงไม่มีวันแรกของการทำงาน แปลว่าสัมภาษณ์ไม่ดี A จึงแสดงความเห็นใจว่า I'm sorry it didn't go well ส่วนคำตอบอื่นตีความตามตัวอักษรและพลาดความหมายแฝง"},"choices":["Why don't you buy one anyway?","Oh no — I'm sorry it didn't go well.","Great! When do you start?","So they gave you a uniform instead?"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Agreement with 'every one of'","th":"ความสอดคล้องกับ every one of"},"topic":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement","th":"ความสอดคล้องของประธานและกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Every one of the [[students]] [[have]] [[handed in]] the form [[on]] time.","choices":["students","have","handed in","on"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The subject is 'Every one', which is singular, so the verb must be 'has handed in'.","th":"ประธานคือ Every one ซึ่งเป็นเอกพจน์ กริยาจึงต้องเป็น has handed in"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Uncountable noun: evidence","th":"คำนามนับไม่ได้: evidence"},"topic":{"en":"Countable and uncountable nouns","th":"คำนามนับได้และนับไม่ได้"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Researchers [[have]] [[found]] [[a clear]] evidence that the drug [[reduces]] symptoms in most patients.","choices":["have","found","a clear","reduces"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Evidence' is uncountable and cannot take 'a': 'clear evidence' or 'a clear piece of evidence'.","th":"evidence เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ ใช้ a ไม่ได้ ต้องเป็น clear evidence หรือ a clear piece of evidence"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"The more …, the more …","th":"โครงสร้าง The more …, the more …"},"topic":{"en":"Sentence structure","th":"โครงสร้างประโยค"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: [[More]] the city [[expands]], the [[harder]] it becomes to [[preserve]] its green spaces.","choices":["More","expands","harder","preserve"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The double comparative needs 'the' in both halves: 'The more the city expands, the harder it becomes…'.","th":"โครงสร้างเปรียบเทียบคู่ต้องมี the ทั้งสองส่วน: The more the city expands, the harder it becomes…"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Verb + as: hail as","th":"กริยา + as: hail as"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: news / feature article","th":"เติมคำในบทความข่าว/สารคดี"},"passage":"Cultivated meat — animal cells grown in steel tanks rather than on farms — has been hailed (1) ______ a solution to some of the most pressing problems of our time, from greenhouse gas emissions to animal welfare. Its supporters argue that it could (2) ______ the need to raise and slaughter billions of animals a year. Sceptics, however, point out that the technology remains extremely expensive, and that producing it on an industrial scale would require (3) ______ amounts of energy. There are cultural obstacles, too. Food is bound up with identity, and many consumers are (4) ______ of anything they regard as \"unnatural\", however safe it may be shown to be. Whether cultivated meat becomes a staple or remains a curiosity may therefore depend less on scientific breakthroughs (5) ______ on public perception.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (1)?","explanation":{"en":"The pattern is 'hail something as something' (praise it publicly as): 'hailed as a solution'.","th":"รูปแบบคือ hail something as something (ยกย่องว่าเป็น) จึงเป็น hailed as a solution"},"choices":["like","to","as","for"],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"stop + -ing","th":"stop + V-ing"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: short story","th":"เติมคำในเรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"The house had stood empty for so long that the neighbours had stopped (6) ______ it. So when lights appeared in its windows one October evening, the whole street took notice. The new owner turned out to be an elderly woman who kept (7) ______ herself, rarely leaving the house except to buy bread and, oddly, enormous quantities of string. Rumours multiplied. Some said she was a retired spy; (8) ______ insisted she was a witch. It was not until the spring that the truth came out, when the children of the street, who had been dared to look over her wall, discovered a garden (9) ______ every branch and post was hung with tiny, intricately knotted birds made of string — hundreds of them, swaying in the breeze. Far from (10) ______ the children away, the woman invited them in and showed them how to make their own.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (6)?","explanation":{"en":"The neighbours no longer noticed the house, so 'stopped noticing'. 'Stopped to notice' would mean they paused in order to notice it — the opposite.","th":"เพื่อนบ้านเลิกสังเกตบ้านหลังนั้นแล้ว จึงใช้ stopped noticing ส่วน stopped to notice แปลว่าหยุดเพื่อจะสังเกต ซึ่งความหมายตรงข้าม"},"choices":["to notice","noticing","notice","noticed"],"answer":1},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Verb + preposition: blame … for","th":"กริยา + บุพบท: blame … for"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze: opinion article","th":"เติมคำในบทความแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"It has become fashionable to (11) ______ social media for almost every ill of modern adolescence, from poor sleep to rising anxiety. There is some truth in this. (12) ______, the evidence is far less conclusive than the headlines suggest. Many of the studies most often cited show correlation rather than causation: teenagers who are unhappy may use their phones more, rather than the other way round. Moreover, the effects, (13) ______ they exist, appear to vary enormously from one young person to another. To treat \"screen time\" as a single, uniformly harmful substance is to (14) ______ the point. What matters is not simply how long teenagers spend online but what they do there, and with whom. Policies built on panic rather than evidence risk (15) ______ young people of the genuine benefits — connection, creativity, information — that the online world can offer.","stem":"Which option best fills gap (11)?","explanation":{"en":"'Blame X for Y' fits 'social media for almost every ill'. We 'accuse someone of', 'attribute something to', and 'charge someone for' means asking them to pay.","th":"blame X for Y เหมาะกับ social media for almost every ill ส่วน accuse ใช้กับ of, attribute ใช้กับ to และ charge someone for แปลว่าเรียกเก็บเงิน"},"choices":["attribute","charge","accuse","blame"],"answer":3},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Main finding of a report","th":"ผลการศึกษาหลักในข่าว"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: news report","th":"การอ่าน: รายงานข่าว"},"passage":"BANGKOK — Tiny fragments of plastic were found in the digestive tracts of more than two-thirds of fish bought from markets along the Gulf of Thailand, according to a study published this week.\n\nResearchers bought 400 fish of twelve common species from markets in four coastal provinces over a period of eighteen months. Particles smaller than five millimetres — mostly fibres from synthetic clothing and fragments of packaging — were detected in 71 per cent of the samples. Species that feed near the sea floor were the most heavily affected.\n\nThe lead author, a marine scientist who has studied plastic pollution for more than a decade, was careful to put the findings in context. \"We are not telling people to stop eating fish,\" she said. \"Most of the particles were in the gut, which is usually removed before cooking. What concerns us is what these numbers tell us about the state of the sea itself.\"\n\nHer caution was not shared by all. Within hours of the study's release, several social media accounts had circulated posts warning that seafood was \"poisoned\", and at least one large coastal market reported a sharp fall in sales the following morning.\n\nFishing associations reacted angrily, accusing the media of sensationalism. A spokesman pointed out that the study did not measure whether eating such fish posed any risk to human health — a point the researchers themselves acknowledge. \"That is the next question,\" the lead author said, \"and it is a much harder one to answer.\"\n\nEnvironmental groups, meanwhile, said the study confirmed what they had long argued: that the problem cannot be solved by beach clean-ups alone. They are calling for limits on single-use packaging and for washing machines to be fitted with filters that capture synthetic fibres before they reach the drains.","stem":"What was the study's main finding?","explanation":{"en":"Particles were found in 71 per cent of the fish. The study did not measure health risk, and the other claims are not made.","th":"พบเศษพลาสติกในปลาร้อยละ 71 การศึกษาไม่ได้วัดความเสี่ยงต่อสุขภาพ และข้ออ้างอื่นไม่มีในข่าว"},"choices":["Most plastic in the sea comes from fishing boats.","Fish sold in markets contain more plastic than fish caught at sea.","Plastic particles were present in most of the fish sampled.","Eating fish from the Gulf of Thailand is dangerous to health."],"answer":2},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Interpreting tone of voice","th":"การตีความน้ำเสียง"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: short story","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องสั้น"},"passage":"For forty years my uncle Prasit painted the same view: the river below his house, the temple roof on the far bank, and the hills beyond. He painted it at dawn and at dusk, in the rainy season and in the dry, and he sold almost none of the pictures. They stood stacked against the walls of every room, so that visiting him was like walking through a very slow film of the same afternoon.\n\nThe family regarded this as a harmless eccentricity, like his refusal to own a telephone. My mother, his younger sister, described him to strangers as \"artistic\", in the tone other people use for \"unemployed\".\n\nWhen he died, we expected to spend a weekend clearing the house. Instead, a man in a linen suit arrived from a gallery in the capital and asked, very politely, whether he might look at the paintings. He spent four hours with them. Then he asked whether we had noticed the temple.\n\nWe had not. Looking again, in the order he arranged them, we saw what forty years of glances had missed. In the earliest paintings the temple roof was bright and whole. Over the years it faded, cracked and sagged; in some of the later canvases it had all but disappeared behind the scaffolding of a new hotel. And then — in the final picture, finished a week before my uncle died — the hotel was gone, and the temple stood as it had in the first painting, golden and entire.\n\n\"The hotel is still there,\" my mother said quietly. \"I drove past it this morning.\"\n\nThe man in the linen suit nodded. \"Yes,\" he said. \"I don't think he was painting what he saw.\"\n\nThe exhibition opens next month. My mother has been to the gallery twice to help with the labels, and she has stopped saying \"artistic\" in that particular tone.","stem":"What does \"in the tone other people use for 'unemployed'\" suggest about the mother?","explanation":{"en":"Saying 'artistic' as others say 'unemployed' means the word was a polite cover for disapproval — she saw his painting as not a real occupation.","th":"การพูดคำว่า artistic ด้วยน้ำเสียงแบบที่คนพูดว่า unemployed แสดงว่าเป็นคำสุภาพที่แฝงความไม่เห็นด้วย เธอมองว่าการวาดภาพไม่ใช่งานจริงจัง"},"choices":["Her use of the word 'artistic' was mildly disapproving.","She was proud of her brother's achievements.","She believed her brother had lost his job.","She did not know what her brother did all day."],"answer":0},{"kind":"m1","program":"tu4","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Recognising understatement","th":"การรู้ทันการพูดน้อยกว่าความจริง (understatement)"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: critical review","th":"การอ่าน: บทวิจารณ์"},"passage":"The new Central Library has been open for six weeks, and it has already appeared in three international design magazines. It is not hard to see why. The building is a gleaming curve of glass and white concrete that seems to float above the plaza, and at night, lit from within, it looks less like a library than a lantern someone has set down gently on the city. Photographers adore it.\n\nReaders may be less enthusiastic. The main reading room, the building's centrepiece, is a vast, echoing space with a ceiling four storeys high. It is magnificent to stand in and almost impossible to concentrate in: every footstep, every whispered conversation, every chair scraped across the stone floor carries to the far corners. On both of my visits, the most crowded area of the building was not the reading room at all but a narrow, carpeted corridor on the second floor, where students sat on the floor with their backs against the wall because it was the only quiet place they could find.\n\nThe books themselves seem almost an afterthought. Large parts of the collection have been moved to an off-site store and must be ordered two days in advance; the shelves that remain have been arranged, apparently, for visual effect, with the result that the children's section is on the top floor, reached by a staircase that no parent with a pushchair will enjoy.\n\nNone of this is to say the building is a failure. As a civic symbol it is undeniably powerful, and the plaza outside has quickly become a popular place to meet. But a library is not only a symbol. It is a place where people need to sit, think and find things, and on those modest measures the Central Library has some way to go. Perhaps its next prize could be for acoustics.","stem":"\"…a staircase that no parent with a pushchair will enjoy.\" The writer's tone here is best described as ______.","explanation":{"en":"Saying parents will not 'enjoy' the stairs is a mild way of saying the children's section is almost inaccessible to them — irony through understatement.","th":"การบอกว่าพ่อแม่ที่เข็นรถเข็นเด็กจะไม่ 'สนุก' กับบันได เป็นการพูดเบา ๆ ว่าแผนกหนังสือเด็กแทบเข้าถึงไม่ได้ จึงเป็นการประชดด้วยการพูดน้อยกว่าความจริง"},"choices":["light-hearted praise of the design","genuine sympathy for the architects","understated irony that points to a real practical problem","angry and openly insulting"],"answer":2}]}},{"exam":"alevel","set":1,"title":{"en":"A-Level English (TCAS) — Practice paper 1","th":"A-Level ภาษาอังกฤษ (TCAS) — ชุดที่ 1"},"note":{"en":"80 questions in 90 minutes following the official A-Level English blueprint: Listening & Speaking 20 (conversations), Reading 40 (ads, review, news, visuals, articles) and Writing 20 (text completion, paragraph organization). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"80 ข้อ 90 นาที ตามโครงสร้างข้อสอบ A-Level ภาษาอังกฤษ: Listening & Speaking 20 ข้อ (บทสนทนา) Reading 40 ข้อ (โฆษณา บทวิจารณ์ ข่าว ภาพประกอบ บทความ) Writing 20 ข้อ (เติมข้อความ เรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้า) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":90,"kinds":{"m1":80},"subjects":{"eng":80},"free":true,"file":"alevel-1","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-short","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing an appropriate response in a conversation","th":"การเลือกถ้อยคำตอบโต้ที่เหมาะสมในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Campus conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในมหาวิทยาลัย"},"passage":"Read the conversation at a university library loan desk.\n\nStudent: Hi, I'd like to renew these two books, please. They're both due tomorrow.\nLibrarian: Let me check... (1) ______ Someone has placed a hold on the history book, so it has to come back by the due date.\nStudent: Oh no. I still need it for my term paper. Is there any way around that?\nLibrarian: (2) ______ You could scan the chapters you need before you return it. The copy room is on the second floor, and you're allowed up to ten per cent of a book.\nStudent: That should work. And the other one?\nLibrarian: That's renewed until the 28th. Just remember that the late fines went up this semester — it's twenty baht a day now.\nStudent: (3) ______ I'd better set a reminder on my phone, then.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (1)?","choices":["I'm afraid the library is closed tomorrow.","You've already paid the fines on both of them.","The economics book is fine, but I can't renew the other one.","Both books have been renewed for another two weeks."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The librarian goes on to explain that the history book has a hold on it, so only one book can be renewed: 'The economics book is fine, but I can't renew the other one.' The other replies contradict what follows.","th":"บรรณารักษ์อธิบายต่อว่าหนังสือประวัติศาสตร์มีคนจองไว้ จึงต่ออายุได้เพียงเล่มเดียว คำตอบที่ถูกคือ 'The economics book is fine, but I can't renew the other one.' ส่วนคำตอบอื่นขัดกับข้อความที่ตามมา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-short","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing language suited to register and politeness","th":"การเลือกภาษาให้เหมาะกับระดับภาษาและความสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Workplace conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในที่ทำงาน"},"passage":"Read the conversation between a manager and an intern at a marketing company.\n\nMs Lee: Nattapong, have you got a minute? It's about the market report.\nNattapong: Sure. Is something wrong with it?\nMs Lee: Not wrong exactly. The data's solid, but the summary runs to three pages. The directors won't read past the first paragraph.\nNattapong: (5) ______\nMs Lee: Exactly. Lead with the two or three findings that really matter, and put the detail in an appendix.\nNattapong: Got it. When do you need the revised version?\nMs Lee: Thursday would be ideal, but (6) ______ if you need to check the figures again.\nNattapong: Thursday should be fine. Would you mind looking over the new summary before I send it out?\nMs Lee: (7) ______ Just drop it in my inbox by Wednesday afternoon.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (7)?","choices":["Never mind about that.","Yes, I do mind, actually.","You're welcome to.","Not at all — happy to."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Would you mind...?' is answered with 'Not at all' when you agree, and Ms Lee then tells him when to send it, so 'Not at all — happy to.' fits. 'Yes, I do mind' is a refusal, 'You're welcome to' gives permission rather than agreeing to help, and 'Never mind' dismisses the request.","th":"คำถาม 'Would you mind...?' ถ้ายินดีช่วยต้องตอบ 'Not at all' และคุณลีก็บอกต่อว่าให้ส่งมาเมื่อไร จึงตอบ 'Not at all — happy to.' ส่วน 'Yes, I do mind' คือปฏิเสธ 'You're welcome to' เป็นการอนุญาตไม่ใช่รับปากช่วย และ 'Never mind' เป็นการปัดคำขอ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-long","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing an appropriate response in a conversation","th":"การเลือกถ้อยคำตอบโต้ที่เหมาะสมในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Campus conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในมหาวิทยาลัย"},"passage":"Read the conversation between Mai, a second-year student, and her academic advisor, Dr Carter.\n\nDr Carter: Come in, Mai. Your email said you're thinking about changing programmes?\nMai: Yes. I'm in my second year of civil engineering, but honestly, the course I enjoyed most last term was the environmental impact one — and that was only an elective.\nDr Carter: (13) ______ Lots of students discover their real interests through electives. What is it about the environmental side that appeals to you?\nMai: I like that it connects the technical work to real consequences. In my structures classes we calculate loads all day, but nobody asks whether the bridge should be built at all.\nDr Carter: That's a fair point, although I'd say good engineers do ask that question. Have you looked at the Environmental Science requirements?\nMai: A little. I'd need chemistry, which I haven't studied since high school.\nDr Carter: Right. And here's the thing — if you transfer, only about half of your engineering credits would count. (14) ______\nMai: An extra year? My parents won't be thrilled about that.\nDr Carter: There may be a middle way. We introduced a minor in Environmental Systems last year. You'd stay in civil engineering but take five related courses on top.\nMai: (15) ______ Would that even fit in my timetable?\nDr Carter: It's a heavy load, I won't pretend otherwise. But several of the minor's courses double as engineering electives, so in practice it's only two or three extra.\nMai: That sounds much more manageable. Who should I talk to about signing up?\nDr Carter: Professor Wongsa coordinates it. I'd also suggest you sit in on her water-quality class next week before you commit.\nMai: (16) ______ Thank you so much. I came in thinking I had to choose one or the other.\nDr Carter: Not at all. Just don't let the decision drift — applications for the minor close at the end of the month.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (13)?","choices":["You should have told me about this last year.","Electives don't really count for much.","That's a strange reason to change programmes.","That's not unusual at all."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Dr Carter continues 'Lots of students discover their real interests through electives', which supports 'That's not unusual at all.' The other replies criticise Mai, which does not match the encouraging follow-up.","th":"ดร.คาร์เตอร์พูดต่อว่า 'Lots of students discover their real interests through electives' ซึ่งสนับสนุน 'That's not unusual at all.' (เป็นเรื่องปกติ) คำตอบอื่นเป็นการตำหนิซึ่งไม่เข้ากับน้ำเสียงให้กำลังใจที่ตามมา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-long","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Finding specific information","th":"การหาข้อมูลเฉพาะ"},"topic":{"en":"Campus conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในมหาวิทยาลัย"},"passage":"Read the conversation between Mai, a second-year student, and her academic advisor, Dr Carter.\n\nDr Carter: Come in, Mai. Your email said you're thinking about changing programmes?\nMai: Yes. I'm in my second year of civil engineering, but honestly, the course I enjoyed most last term was the environmental impact one — and that was only an elective.\nDr Carter: (13) ______ Lots of students discover their real interests through electives. What is it about the environmental side that appeals to you?\nMai: I like that it connects the technical work to real consequences. In my structures classes we calculate loads all day, but nobody asks whether the bridge should be built at all.\nDr Carter: That's a fair point, although I'd say good engineers do ask that question. Have you looked at the Environmental Science requirements?\nMai: A little. I'd need chemistry, which I haven't studied since high school.\nDr Carter: Right. And here's the thing — if you transfer, only about half of your engineering credits would count. (14) ______\nMai: An extra year? My parents won't be thrilled about that.\nDr Carter: There may be a middle way. We introduced a minor in Environmental Systems last year. You'd stay in civil engineering but take five related courses on top.\nMai: (15) ______ Would that even fit in my timetable?\nDr Carter: It's a heavy load, I won't pretend otherwise. But several of the minor's courses double as engineering electives, so in practice it's only two or three extra.\nMai: That sounds much more manageable. Who should I talk to about signing up?\nDr Carter: Professor Wongsa coordinates it. I'd also suggest you sit in on her water-quality class next week before you commit.\nMai: (16) ______ Thank you so much. I came in thinking I had to choose one or the other.\nDr Carter: Not at all. Just don't let the decision drift — applications for the minor close at the end of the month.","stem":"Why is Mai interested in environmental studies?","choices":["Her parents have encouraged her to change.","She has always enjoyed chemistry.","She is not doing well in her structures classes.","She wants her studies to consider the wider effects of engineering projects."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Mai says environmental studies 'connects the technical work to real consequences' and that nobody asks 'whether the bridge should be built at all', so she wants her studies to consider the wider effects of engineering projects. She never mentions poor grades, and she has not studied chemistry since school.","th":"ใหม่บอกว่าวิชาสิ่งแวดล้อม 'connects the technical work to real consequences' และไม่มีใครถามว่าควรสร้างสะพานหรือไม่ เธอจึงอยากเรียนสิ่งที่คำนึงถึงผลกระทบในวงกว้างของงานวิศวกรรม ไม่ได้พูดถึงผลการเรียนไม่ดี และไม่ได้เรียนเคมีมาตั้งแต่มัธยม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-ads","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Advertisement","th":"โฆษณา"},"passage":"STUDYHIVE — Quiet Study & Co-working Space\nJust 3 minutes' walk from the university's North Gate\n\nOpen: 7 a.m. – midnight (Mon–Sat) | 9 a.m. – 9 p.m. (Sun)\n• Silent Zone: phones on mute, no calls\n• Group rooms for 4–8 people (book online, max. 3 hours) — monthly members only\n• High-speed Wi-Fi • Printing 2 baht/page • Free drinking water, coffee bar in the lobby\n\nPRICES\nDay pass ............................ 120 baht\n10-visit card (valid 60 days) ........ 990 baht\nMonthly unlimited ................. 1,800 baht\nStudents with a valid ID: 20% off monthly memberships only\n\nEXAM SEASON SPECIAL (1–31 May): Buy a monthly membership and get one group-room session FREE!","stem":"A student with a valid ID buys a monthly membership on 10 May. What will the student pay, and what extra will the student receive?","choices":["1,620 baht and unlimited group-room use","1,440 baht and one free group-room session","1,440 baht and free printing for the month","1,800 baht and one free group-room session"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Students get 20% off monthly memberships: 1,800 − 360 = 1,440 baht, and the May special adds one free group-room session. So the answer is 1,440 baht and one free group-room session.","th":"นักศึกษาได้ส่วนลด 20% สำหรับสมาชิกรายเดือน: 1,800 − 360 = 1,440 บาท และโปรโมชันเดือนพฤษภาคมแถมห้องกลุ่มฟรีหนึ่งครั้ง คำตอบจึงเป็น 1,440 บาทพร้อมห้องกลุ่มฟรีหนึ่งครั้ง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-ads","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Advertisement","th":"โฆษณา"},"passage":"PART-TIME RESEARCH ASSISTANT WANTED\nFaculty of Public Health — Urban Air Quality Project\n\nWe are looking for a reliable, detail-oriented student to help with field data collection and data entry.\n\nDuties: setting up and checking air sensors at 12 sites across Bangkok (some early mornings); entering and cleaning data in Excel; helping to prepare monthly summaries.\n\nRequirements: current undergraduate (Year 2 or above), any faculty; basic Excel skills; able to travel independently around the city. Knowledge of R or Python is an advantage but not essential.\n\nHours: 10–12 hours per week, flexible, for six months (June–November)\nPay: 150 baht/hour plus travel expenses\n\nTo apply: send a CV and a short statement (max. 300 words) explaining your interest to the project office by 15 May. Shortlisted applicants will be asked to complete a 20-minute Excel task.","stem":"Which applicant meets ALL the stated requirements?","choices":["A third-year engineering student who knows Python but has never used Excel","A recent graduate with excellent R and Excel skills","A second-year economics student who uses Excel and can get around Bangkok alone","A first-year public health student who is fluent in Python"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The job needs a current undergraduate in Year 2 or above from any faculty, with basic Excel and able to travel independently: the second-year economics student who uses Excel and can get around Bangkok alone. A first-year student, a graduate and someone without Excel each fail one requirement.","th":"งานนี้ต้องการนักศึกษาปริญญาตรีปี 2 ขึ้นไปจากคณะใดก็ได้ ใช้ Excel ได้ และเดินทางเองได้ จึงเป็นนักศึกษาเศรษฐศาสตร์ปี 2 ที่ใช้ Excel และเดินทางในกรุงเทพฯ เองได้ ส่วนนักศึกษาปี 1 ผู้ที่จบแล้ว และคนที่ไม่เคยใช้ Excel ขาดคุณสมบัติคนละอย่าง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-review","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Finding specific information","th":"การหาข้อมูลเฉพาะ"},"topic":{"en":"Product / service review","th":"บทวิจารณ์สินค้าและบริการ"},"passage":"Lumo Page 7 e-reader — reviewed by Kanya R. ★★★★☆\n\nI've owned the Lumo Page 7 for three months, mostly reading on my commute and in bed, so here's an honest take.\n\nThe good: The 7-inch screen is the best I've used at this price. Text is sharp, and the warm-light setting means I can read late at night without the glare that used to keep me awake when I read on my tablet. Battery life is genuinely impressive: I charge it about once every three weeks. It's also waterproof, which I tested — unintentionally — when I dropped it in the bath. It survived without a scratch.\n\nThe not-so-good: The Lumo store is still small. About a third of the titles on my wish list weren't available, so I've ended up buying some e-books elsewhere and transferring them by cable, which is fiddly. Page turns are slightly slower than on my old e-reader — not a deal-breaker, but noticeable if you read fast. And the case, sold separately at 890 baht, feels overpriced for what is basically folded plastic.\n\nCustomer service deserves a mention. When the screen froze after a software update, I contacted support on a Sunday and had a fix within two hours.\n\nVerdict: At 4,290 baht, the Page 7 is excellent value if you mostly read novels and don't mind shopping around for books. If you rely on a large store and instant downloads, you may be happier paying more for a better-known brand.","stem":"What does the reviewer say about the battery?","choices":["It stopped working after a software update.","It runs out quickly when the warm light is on.","It needs charging every night.","It lasts about three weeks between charges."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"She writes 'I charge it about once every three weeks', so it lasts about three weeks between charges. The software problem was a frozen screen, not the battery.","th":"ผู้วิจารณ์เขียนว่า 'I charge it about once every three weeks' แบตเตอรี่จึงใช้ได้ราวสามสัปดาห์ต่อการชาร์จหนึ่งครั้ง ปัญหาหลังอัปเดตซอฟต์แวร์คือหน้าจอค้าง ไม่ใช่แบตเตอรี่"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-review","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying information that is not stated","th":"การระบุข้อมูลที่ไม่ได้กล่าวถึง"},"topic":{"en":"Product / service review","th":"บทวิจารณ์สินค้าและบริการ"},"passage":"Lumo Page 7 e-reader — reviewed by Kanya R. ★★★★☆\n\nI've owned the Lumo Page 7 for three months, mostly reading on my commute and in bed, so here's an honest take.\n\nThe good: The 7-inch screen is the best I've used at this price. Text is sharp, and the warm-light setting means I can read late at night without the glare that used to keep me awake when I read on my tablet. Battery life is genuinely impressive: I charge it about once every three weeks. It's also waterproof, which I tested — unintentionally — when I dropped it in the bath. It survived without a scratch.\n\nThe not-so-good: The Lumo store is still small. About a third of the titles on my wish list weren't available, so I've ended up buying some e-books elsewhere and transferring them by cable, which is fiddly. Page turns are slightly slower than on my old e-reader — not a deal-breaker, but noticeable if you read fast. And the case, sold separately at 890 baht, feels overpriced for what is basically folded plastic.\n\nCustomer service deserves a mention. When the screen froze after a software update, I contacted support on a Sunday and had a fix within two hours.\n\nVerdict: At 4,290 baht, the Page 7 is excellent value if you mostly read novels and don't mind shopping around for books. If you rely on a large store and instant downloads, you may be happier paying more for a better-known brand.","stem":"Which problem is NOT mentioned in the review?","choices":["Turning pages is a little slow.","The case is expensive for its quality.","Many books are not available in the Lumo store.","The screen is hard to read in bright sunlight."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The review mentions the small store, slow page turns and the overpriced case, but says nothing about reading in sunlight; it actually praises the screen. So 'The screen is hard to read in bright sunlight' is not mentioned.","th":"บทวิจารณ์พูดถึงร้านหนังสือที่มีหนังสือน้อย การเปลี่ยนหน้าช้า และเคสที่แพงเกินคุณภาพ แต่ไม่ได้พูดถึงการอ่านกลางแดด ซึ่งกลับชมหน้าจอด้วย คำตอบจึงเป็น 'The screen is hard to read in bright sunlight.'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-news","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"News report","th":"ข่าว"},"passage":"Chiang Mai trials car-free days as haze season begins\n\nCHIANG MAI — City authorities will close the old town to private cars on two weekdays a week from next month in an attempt to reduce air pollution during the annual haze season, officials announced on Monday.\n\nUnder the scheme, which runs for eight weeks from 1 March, only buses, emergency vehicles, residents' vehicles and delivery vans before 10 a.m. will be allowed inside the moat on Tuesdays and Thursdays. Free shuttle buses will run every ten minutes from three park-and-ride sites on the edge of the city.\n\nDeputy Mayor Siriporn Kaewmanee said the trial was \"a first step rather than a solution\". Most of the fine particulate matter known as PM2.5 that blankets the north each spring comes from agricultural burning and forest fires, not traffic, she acknowledged. \"But we cannot ask farmers to change while doing nothing ourselves. Traffic is the part of the problem the city controls.\"\n\nThe announcement received a mixed reaction. Business owners in the old town fear that fewer visitors will mean lower sales. \"Tourists already stay away in March because of the smoke,\" said Anan Chaiyo, who runs a guesthouse near Tha Phae Gate. \"Now they'll have another reason not to come.\"\n\nEnvironmental groups welcomed the move but said it did not go far enough. A spokesperson for the Northern Breath Network said the city should publish hourly air-quality readings from every district so that residents could judge for themselves whether the trial was working.\n\nResearchers at a local university will place sensors at 20 points inside and outside the old town to compare readings on car-free and normal days. Dr Pimchanok Saelee, who leads the team, warned that the results might be hard to interpret. \"Wind direction and the number of fires on a given day have a far bigger effect than traffic,\" she said. \"We'll need several seasons of data before we can say anything with confidence.\"\n\nIf the trial is judged successful, the city council will consider extending it to the whole haze season next year.","stem":"What is the news report mainly about?","choices":["A plan to ban agricultural burning around Chiang Mai","Falling tourist numbers in Chiang Mai's old town","A university study proving that traffic causes haze","A short trial of traffic restrictions aimed at reducing air pollution"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The report describes an eight-week scheme closing the old town to private cars to reduce pollution, with reactions to it — a short trial of traffic restrictions aimed at reducing air pollution. Burning and tourism are mentioned only as part of the discussion, and the study has not proved anything yet.","th":"ข่าวรายงานโครงการแปดสัปดาห์ที่ห้ามรถส่วนตัวเข้าเมืองเก่าเพื่อลดมลพิษ พร้อมความเห็นต่าง ๆ จึงเป็นการทดลองจำกัดการจราจรเพื่อลดมลพิษทางอากาศ ส่วนการเผาและนักท่องเที่ยวเป็นเพียงประเด็นประกอบ และงานวิจัยยังไม่ได้พิสูจน์อะไร"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-news","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมานจากข้อมูลในเรื่อง"},"topic":{"en":"News report","th":"ข่าว"},"passage":"Chiang Mai trials car-free days as haze season begins\n\nCHIANG MAI — City authorities will close the old town to private cars on two weekdays a week from next month in an attempt to reduce air pollution during the annual haze season, officials announced on Monday.\n\nUnder the scheme, which runs for eight weeks from 1 March, only buses, emergency vehicles, residents' vehicles and delivery vans before 10 a.m. will be allowed inside the moat on Tuesdays and Thursdays. Free shuttle buses will run every ten minutes from three park-and-ride sites on the edge of the city.\n\nDeputy Mayor Siriporn Kaewmanee said the trial was \"a first step rather than a solution\". Most of the fine particulate matter known as PM2.5 that blankets the north each spring comes from agricultural burning and forest fires, not traffic, she acknowledged. \"But we cannot ask farmers to change while doing nothing ourselves. Traffic is the part of the problem the city controls.\"\n\nThe announcement received a mixed reaction. Business owners in the old town fear that fewer visitors will mean lower sales. \"Tourists already stay away in March because of the smoke,\" said Anan Chaiyo, who runs a guesthouse near Tha Phae Gate. \"Now they'll have another reason not to come.\"\n\nEnvironmental groups welcomed the move but said it did not go far enough. A spokesperson for the Northern Breath Network said the city should publish hourly air-quality readings from every district so that residents could judge for themselves whether the trial was working.\n\nResearchers at a local university will place sensors at 20 points inside and outside the old town to compare readings on car-free and normal days. Dr Pimchanok Saelee, who leads the team, warned that the results might be hard to interpret. \"Wind direction and the number of fires on a given day have a far bigger effect than traffic,\" she said. \"We'll need several seasons of data before we can say anything with confidence.\"\n\nIf the trial is judged successful, the city council will consider extending it to the whole haze season next year.","stem":"What is Anan Chaiyo's main worry?","choices":["The scheme may further reduce visitors in a month that is already quiet.","Smoke from fires will damage his guesthouse.","Tourists will park at the park-and-ride sites.","Delivery vans will not be able to reach him."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"He says tourists 'already stay away in March because of the smoke' and 'now they'll have another reason not to come', so he fears the scheme will further reduce visitors in a month that is already quiet.","th":"เขาบอกว่านักท่องเที่ยว 'already stay away in March because of the smoke' และ 'now they'll have another reason not to come' จึงกังวลว่าจำนวนนักท่องเที่ยวที่น้อยอยู่แล้วจะลดลงอีก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-visual","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Interpreting data in a table","th":"การตีความข้อมูลจากตาราง"},"topic":{"en":"Table with caption","th":"ตารางพร้อมคำอธิบาย"},"passage":"The table shows the results of two surveys of students at a Thai university, carried out ten years apart. In each survey, every student named one main way of travelling to campus. 1,200 students took part in the 2025 survey. Journey times are 2025 averages.","stem":"Which mode showed the largest increase between 2015 and 2025?","choices":["Motorcycle","The university shuttle","Bicycle / walking","Public bus"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The university shuttle rose from 5% to 21% (+16 points), more than bicycle / walking (+13). Motorcycle and bus use fell.","th":"รถรับส่งของมหาวิทยาลัย (university shuttle) เพิ่มจาก 5% เป็น 21% (+16) มากกว่าจักรยาน/เดิน (+13) ส่วนมอเตอร์ไซค์และรถเมล์ลดลง"},"table":{"title":"Main way of travelling to campus (% of students)","rows":[["Mode","2015","2025","Average journey 2025 (min)"],["Private car","28","14","42"],["Motorcycle","35","22","25"],["Public bus","20","18","50"],["University shuttle","5","21","30"],["Bicycle / walking","12","25","15"]]}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-visual","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Interpreting data in a table","th":"การตีความข้อมูลจากตาราง"},"topic":{"en":"Table with caption","th":"ตารางพร้อมคำอธิบาย"},"passage":"The table shows results from a national survey of smartphone, computer and TV use in 2025. Respondents recorded the time they spent on each activity over one week; the figures are daily averages.","stem":"Which activity takes up the most time for people aged 19–24?","choices":["Video streaming","Social media","Work / study","Gaming"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"For 19–24 year-olds, work / study is 2.9 hours, just above social media at 2.8 hours.","th":"กลุ่มอายุ 19–24 ปีใช้เวลากับ work / study 2.9 ชั่วโมง มากกว่าสื่อสังคมออนไลน์ (2.8 ชั่วโมง) เล็กน้อย"},"table":{"title":"Average daily screen time (hours) by activity and age group","rows":[["Activity","15–18","19–24","25–40","41–60"],["Social media","3.1","2.8","1.9","1.2"],["Video streaming","2.2","2.5","1.8","1.6"],["Gaming","1.5","1.1","0.6","0.2"],["Work / study","1.4","2.9","3.6","2.2"],["Total","8.2","9.3","7.9","5.2"]]}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-article","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Article: sleep and memory","th":"บทความ: การนอนหลับกับความจำ"},"passage":"Why sleep is part of studying\n\nFor generations, students have treated sleep as the first thing to sacrifice before an exam. An all-night study session feels productive: the hours are there, the notes are open, and every page read seems like progress. Yet a growing body of research suggests that this trade-off is, in most cases, a poor one. Sleep is not simply a pause between periods of learning; it is part of the learning itself.\n\nTo understand why, it helps to know what happens to a new memory. When we first encounter information — a formula, a list of dates, the steps of a chemical reaction — it is stored in a fragile form, largely dependent on a small, seahorse-shaped region of the brain called the hippocampus. In this state, the memory is easily disrupted. Over the following hours and days, it is gradually reorganised and linked to existing knowledge in the cortex, the brain's outer layer. Scientists call this process consolidation, and a large share of it appears to happen while we sleep.\n\nDifferent stages of sleep seem to play different roles. During deep, slow-wave sleep, which dominates the first half of the night, recordings show the hippocampus \"replaying\" patterns of activity from the day, as if rehearsing what was learned. Rapid eye movement (REM) sleep, which becomes more frequent towards morning, has been linked to finding connections between ideas and to solving problems creatively. Cutting the night short therefore does not just reduce sleep by a fixed amount; it removes a disproportionate share of REM.\n\nThe evidence does not come only from brain recordings. In one common type of experiment, volunteers learn pairs of words in the evening and are tested the next morning. Those who sleep normally typically remember considerably more than those kept awake, even when both groups are given time to recover before the test. Similar results have been found for physical skills, such as typing a sequence on a keyboard: performance often improves after a night's sleep without any further practice.\n\nNone of this means that sleep can replace study. Consolidation can only strengthen what was learned in the first place, and a student who has never opened the textbook will not wake up knowing its contents. Nor are the findings entirely uniform; some studies have found smaller effects than others, and researchers still debate exactly how much each stage of sleep contributes. What the evidence does challenge is the assumption that time spent asleep is time taken away from learning.\n\nThe practical implications are fairly simple. Spreading study over several days, with sleep in between, tends to produce more durable memories than cramming the same number of hours into a single session. Reviewing difficult material in the evening, rather than first thing in the morning, may give it a better chance of being consolidated. And on the night before an exam, an extra hour of sleep is likely to be worth more than an extra hour of reading — however unconvincing that may feel at 2 a.m.","stem":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the article?","choices":["The hippocampus is the most important part of the brain for learning.","Students should stop studying late at night and study only in the morning.","REM sleep is more useful for learning than slow-wave sleep.","Sleep plays an active role in turning new information into lasting memories."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The article argues that sleep 'is part of the learning itself' and explains consolidation during sleep, so the main idea is that sleep plays an active role in turning new information into lasting memories. The other statements are details or overstatements.","th":"บทความเสนอว่าการนอน 'is part of the learning itself' และอธิบายกระบวนการ consolidation ระหว่างนอน ใจความสำคัญคือการนอนมีบทบาทในการเปลี่ยนข้อมูลใหม่ให้เป็นความจำที่คงทน ส่วนข้อความอื่นเป็นรายละเอียดหรือกล่าวเกินจริง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-article","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the writer's purpose","th":"การระบุจุดประสงค์ของผู้เขียน"},"topic":{"en":"Article: urban heat islands","th":"บทความ: เกาะความร้อนในเมือง"},"passage":"Cooling the city\n\nOn a hot afternoon in April, the difference can be felt simply by crossing a road. On one side, a car park of dark asphalt radiates heat back at passers-by; on the other, under a row of rain trees, the air is noticeably cooler. Multiply that small contrast across an entire city and you have what scientists call the urban heat island effect: the tendency of built-up areas to be several degrees warmer than the countryside around them.\n\nThe causes are well understood. Concrete, asphalt and brick absorb sunlight during the day and release it slowly at night, so cities cool down far less after sunset than rural areas do. Buildings block the wind that would otherwise carry heat away. Air conditioners, vehicles and factories add heat of their own. And because cities have replaced soil and vegetation with hard surfaces, they lose much of the cooling that comes from water evaporating from leaves and ground — the same process that cools our skin when we sweat.\n\nFor tropical cities such as Bangkok, the consequences are serious. Night-time temperatures matter particularly for health, since the body needs cooler hours to recover from daytime heat. Older people, outdoor workers and those without air conditioning are most at risk. There is also a cruel feedback loop: as the city warms, more people switch on air conditioners, which pump heat outside and increase demand for electricity, much of it still generated by burning fossil fuels.\n\nOf the many proposed remedies, trees are among the most popular, and with good reason. A mature tree shades the ground beneath it and, through its leaves, releases large quantities of water vapour. Studies in several cities have measured surface temperatures under tree canopies that are more than ten degrees lower than on nearby exposed pavements. Parks can cool not only their own area but also streets some distance downwind.\n\nYet planting trees is not as straightforward as it sounds. In dense neighbourhoods there is often simply no room: pavements are narrow, and underground space is occupied by pipes and cables that roots can damage. Young trees need years of watering and care before they provide meaningful shade, and many planted in publicity campaigns die within a few years because no one is responsible for maintaining them. Choosing the wrong species can create new problems, from falling branches in storms to roots that lift pavements.\n\nFor these reasons, urban planners increasingly argue that trees should be one part of a broader strategy. Light-coloured \"cool roofs\" reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it. Permeable paving lets rainwater soak into the ground, where it can later evaporate. Building regulations can require shaded walkways or limit the share of a plot that may be covered with hard surfaces. None of these measures is dramatic on its own. Combined, and sustained over decades, they could make the difference between a city that is merely hot and one that is dangerously so.","stem":"What is the writer's main purpose?","choices":["To explain why cities are hotter and to evaluate ways of cooling them","To criticise urban planners for ignoring the heat problem","To compare the climate of Bangkok with that of other cities","To persuade readers to plant trees in their own gardens"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The article first explains the causes and effects of urban heat islands and then weighs remedies (trees, cool roofs, paving, regulations), so it explains why cities are hotter and evaluates ways of cooling them.","th":"บทความอธิบายสาเหตุและผลกระทบของเกาะความร้อนในเมือง แล้วประเมินวิธีแก้ (ต้นไม้ หลังคาเย็น ทางเท้าซึมน้ำ กฎหมายอาคาร) จุดประสงค์จึงเป็นการอธิบายและประเมินวิธีทำให้เมืองเย็นลง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"text-completion","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Coherence: choosing content that fits the meaning","th":"การเลือกเนื้อความให้สอดคล้องกับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Formal email: requesting an extension","th":"อีเมลแบบทางการ: ขอขยายเวลาส่งงาน"},"passage":"Read the email and choose the best option for each gap.\n\nDear Dr Somchai,\n\nI am writing to (1) ______ for the research essay in ENG 214, which is due this Friday.\n\nLast week my grandfather was admitted to hospital, and I have been travelling to Nakhon Sawan to help my family care for him. (2) ______ I have completed the research and about half of the draft, but I am concerned that the final sections would be rushed if I submitted it on time.\n\n(3) ______ would be extremely helpful. I would of course be happy to provide a letter from the hospital (4) ______.\n\n(5) ______ Thank you for considering my request.\n\nYours sincerely,\nNicha Boonmee (Student ID 6612345)","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (1)?","choices":["ask whether the essay topic could be changed","request a short extension of the deadline","inform you that I will not be submitting the essay","let you know that I have already submitted the essay"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The rest of the email explains why she needs more time and ends with 'my request', so she is writing to request a short extension of the deadline.","th":"เนื้อหาที่เหลืออธิบายว่าทำไมต้องการเวลาเพิ่ม และปิดท้ายด้วย 'my request' จึงเป็นการเขียนเพื่อ request a short extension of the deadline (ขอขยายเวลาส่ง)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"text-completion","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Cohesion: linking ideas logically","th":"การเชื่อมโยงความคิดอย่างมีเหตุผล"},"topic":{"en":"Expository text: plastic recycling","th":"ข้อความเชิงอธิบาย: การรีไซเคิลพลาสติก"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each gap.\n\nMany people assume that putting a plastic bottle in a recycling bin guarantees that it will become a new product. (6) ______ In practice, only a small fraction of all the plastic ever produced has been recycled. One reason is that there are many different types of plastic, (7) ______. Food waste and mixed materials, such as labels glued to containers, add further costs. (8) ______, a large proportion of collected plastic is burned, buried or shipped abroad. Some experts therefore argue that recycling has received too much attention (9) ______. If fewer single-use items were produced in the first place, they say, there would be less waste to manage. (10) ______","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (8)?","choices":["In contrast to these difficulties","For example","Even though these costs have been removed","Because sorting and cleaning are so expensive"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The costs of sorting and cleaning explain why much plastic is burned or buried, so a cause is needed: 'Because sorting and cleaning are so expensive'. 'Even though... removed' contradicts the costs just mentioned, and the other connectors give the wrong relationship.","th":"ค่าใช้จ่ายในการคัดแยกและทำความสะอาดเป็นสาเหตุที่พลาสติกจำนวนมากถูกเผาหรือฝัง จึงต้องใช้ 'Because sorting and cleaning are so expensive' ส่วน 'Even though... removed' ขัดกับค่าใช้จ่ายที่เพิ่งกล่าว และตัวเชื่อมอื่นแสดงความสัมพันธ์ผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"paragraph","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Organising sentences into a logical paragraph","th":"การเรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้าอย่างมีลำดับ"},"topic":{"en":"Paragraph organization: process","th":"เรียงประโยค: ขั้นตอน"},"passage":"[1] When you have finished your journey, return the bike to any docking station.\n[2] Using the city's bike-share system is straightforward once you have registered.\n[3] Your account will be charged automatically according to how long you used the bike.\n[4] First, download the app and link it to a payment card.\n[5] Then scan the QR code on any available bike to unlock it.","stem":"The sentences above are in jumbled order. Which order makes the most logical paragraph?","choices":["[2] – [4] – [5] – [1] – [3]","[4] – [5] – [2] – [1] – [3]","[2] – [5] – [4] – [1] – [3]","[2] – [4] – [5] – [3] – [1]"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"[2] – [4] – [5] – [1] – [3]: the topic sentence ('straightforward once you have registered') comes first, then the steps in sequence marked by 'First', 'Then' and 'When you have finished', and finally the automatic charge after the bike is returned.","th":"[2] – [4] – [5] – [1] – [3]: เริ่มด้วยประโยคใจความหลัก ('straightforward once you have registered') แล้วเรียงขั้นตอนตามคำบอกลำดับ 'First', 'Then', 'When you have finished' และจบด้วยการตัดเงินอัตโนมัติหลังคืนจักรยาน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"paragraph","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Organising sentences into a logical paragraph","th":"การเรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้าอย่างมีลำดับ"},"topic":{"en":"Paragraph organization: argument","th":"เรียงประโยค: การเสนอข้อโต้แย้ง"},"passage":"[1] Early results suggest that both views contain some truth.\n[2] Supporters claim that employees who work fewer days are more focused and less likely to burn out.\n[3] Productivity rose in many of the trial firms, but some had to hire extra staff to maintain their service.\n[4] Several companies have recently experimented with a four-day working week.\n[5] Critics, on the other hand, worry that customers will suffer if offices close for an extra day.","stem":"The sentences above are in jumbled order. Which order makes the most logical paragraph?","choices":["[4] – [2] – [5] – [3] – [1]","[4] – [5] – [2] – [1] – [3]","[1] – [4] – [2] – [5] – [3]","[4] – [2] – [5] – [1] – [3]"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"[4] – [2] – [5] – [1] – [3]: the topic is introduced, then 'Supporters' and 'Critics, on the other hand' give the two sides; 'both views' must follow both sides, and the final sentence gives evidence for each view.","th":"[4] – [2] – [5] – [1] – [3]: เริ่มด้วยการแนะนำหัวข้อ ตามด้วยฝ่าย 'Supporters' และ 'Critics, on the other hand' ประโยค 'both views' ต้องมาหลังจากกล่าวถึงทั้งสองฝ่าย และประโยคสุดท้ายให้หลักฐานสนับสนุนแต่ละฝ่าย"}}]}},{"exam":"alevel","set":2,"title":{"en":"A-Level English (TCAS) — Practice paper 2","th":"A-Level ภาษาอังกฤษ (TCAS) — ชุดที่ 2"},"note":{"en":"80 questions in 90 minutes following the official A-Level English blueprint: Listening & Speaking 20 (conversations), Reading 40 (ads, review, news, visuals, articles) and Writing 20 (text completion, paragraph organization). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"80 ข้อ 90 นาที ตามโครงสร้างข้อสอบ A-Level ภาษาอังกฤษ: Listening & Speaking 20 ข้อ (บทสนทนา) Reading 40 ข้อ (โฆษณา บทวิจารณ์ ข่าว ภาพประกอบ บทความ) Writing 20 ข้อ (เติมข้อความ เรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้า) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":90,"kinds":{"m1":80},"subjects":{"eng":80},"free":false,"file":"alevel-2","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-short","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing an appropriate response in a conversation","th":"การเลือกถ้อยคำตอบโต้ที่เหมาะสมในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Campus conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในมหาวิทยาลัย"},"passage":"Read the conversation between two university classmates.\n\nPloy: Have you heard from Krit? Our slides are due on Monday and he still hasn't sent his section.\nBen: Not a word. I messaged him twice yesterday. (1) ______\nPloy: Maybe something's happened. He's usually pretty reliable.\nBen: True. I'd hate to accuse him of slacking off if he's actually ill or something.\nPloy: (2) ______ Why don't you call him instead of sending another message? A call is harder to ignore.\nBen: Fair enough. But if we still haven't heard anything by tomorrow, (3) ______\nPloy: Agreed. We can split his part between us and explain the situation to Dr Anong. She'd rather know now than find out halfway through the presentation.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (1)?","choices":["He replied straight away, actually.","He's seen them, but he hasn't replied.","I'm going to tell Dr Anong he's lazy.","He told me he finished it last week."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Ben has had 'not a word' from Krit, so 'He's seen them, but he hasn't replied.' fits. Replying or saying he has finished contradicts 'Not a word', and calling him lazy conflicts with Ben's later wish not to accuse him unfairly.","th":"เบนไม่ได้รับคำตอบเลย ('Not a word') จึงต้องเป็น 'He's seen them, but he hasn't replied.' (อ่านแล้วแต่ไม่ตอบ) การตอบกลับทันทีหรือบอกว่าทำเสร็จแล้วขัดกับ 'Not a word' และการกล่าวหาว่าขี้เกียจขัดกับที่เบนไม่อยากกล่าวหาเพื่อนโดยไม่เป็นธรรม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-short","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing an appropriate response in a conversation","th":"การเลือกถ้อยคำตอบโต้ที่เหมาะสมในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Workplace conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในที่ทำงาน"},"passage":"Read part of a job interview at a consulting firm.\n\nInterviewer: Thanks for coming in, Arisa. I see from your CV that you led the debating club at university.\nArisa: Yes, for two years. (5) ______\nInterviewer: That's useful — this role involves a lot of client presentations. What would you say is your main weakness?\nArisa: (6) ______ I used to take on too many projects at once. I've learned to say no to things that aren't a priority, and I plan my week far more carefully now.\nInterviewer: Good. Now, the position is based in Bangkok, but it involves travel to our regional offices about once a month. Would that be a problem?\nArisa: Not at all. (7) ______\nInterviewer: Excellent. Do you have any questions for us?\nArisa: Yes — what would a typical first month look like for a new analyst?","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (7)?","choices":["But I can only travel at weekends.","I've never been to Bangkok before.","I'd actually welcome the chance to see the other offices.","I'd prefer not to leave Bangkok, if possible."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"After 'Not at all', Arisa must say something positive about travel: 'I'd actually welcome the chance to see the other offices.' Preferring not to travel or adding a limitation contradicts 'Not at all', and the job is based in Bangkok, where she is being interviewed.","th":"หลัง 'Not at all' อริสาต้องพูดเชิงบวกเรื่องการเดินทาง: 'I'd actually welcome the chance to see the other offices.' การไม่อยากเดินทางหรือตั้งเงื่อนไขขัดกับ 'Not at all'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-long","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Cohesion: linking ideas logically","th":"การเชื่อมโยงความคิดอย่างมีเหตุผล"},"topic":{"en":"Campus conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในมหาวิทยาลัย"},"passage":"Read the interview between Fah, a student journalist, and Mr Hughes, the university's sustainability officer.\n\nFah: Thanks for making time for this, Mr Hughes. Our readers have been asking about the new food-waste scheme in the canteens. Could you start by explaining what's changed?\nMr Hughes: Certainly. Since January, every canteen vendor has weighed the food that's thrown away at the end of each day. (13) ______ — we simply had no idea how much was being wasted.\nFah: And what did the figures show?\nMr Hughes: Roughly 400 kilograms a day across the campus. About a third of that was unsold cooked food; the rest was what students left on their plates.\nFah: That's a lot. Students have complained that portions got smaller this term. Is that connected?\nMr Hughes: (14) ______ Vendors can now offer half portions at a lower price. Full portions are the same size as before. But I take the point — we didn't communicate the change very well.\nFah: What happens to the waste now?\nMr Hughes: Unsold food that's still safe goes to a local charity kitchen every evening. Plate waste goes to the composting unit behind the Faculty of Agriculture, and the compost is used in the university gardens.\nFah: Some people say composting is just a way of making waste look green. (15) ______\nMr Hughes: There's some truth in that. Composting is better than landfill, but the best food waste is the waste we never create. That's why the half-portion option matters more, in my view, than the composting.\nFah: Has the scheme actually reduced waste?\nMr Hughes: It's early days. Plate waste is down about fifteen per cent, but unsold food has barely changed. (16) ______\nFah: So what's next?\nMr Hughes: We're trialling an app that lets students order before noon, so vendors know how much to cook. If it works in two canteens, we'll roll it out everywhere next year.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (13)?","choices":["Before that, nobody measured it at all","Weighing was the vendors' own idea","Students now do the weighing themselves","We have always weighed it very carefully"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The words after the gap ('we simply had no idea how much was being wasted') explain why weighing was introduced, so the gap is 'Before that, nobody measured it at all'. 'Always weighed it' contradicts having no idea, and vendors, not students, weigh the food.","th":"ข้อความหลังช่องว่าง ('we simply had no idea how much was being wasted') อธิบายว่าทำไมเริ่มชั่งน้ำหนัก ช่องว่างจึงเป็น 'Before that, nobody measured it at all' การบอกว่าชั่งเสมอขัดกับการไม่รู้ปริมาณ และผู้ชั่งคือร้านค้าไม่ใช่นักศึกษา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-long","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Comparing and calculating from data","th":"การเปรียบเทียบและคำนวณจากข้อมูล"},"topic":{"en":"Campus conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในมหาวิทยาลัย"},"passage":"Read the interview between Fah, a student journalist, and Mr Hughes, the university's sustainability officer.\n\nFah: Thanks for making time for this, Mr Hughes. Our readers have been asking about the new food-waste scheme in the canteens. Could you start by explaining what's changed?\nMr Hughes: Certainly. Since January, every canteen vendor has weighed the food that's thrown away at the end of each day. (13) ______ — we simply had no idea how much was being wasted.\nFah: And what did the figures show?\nMr Hughes: Roughly 400 kilograms a day across the campus. About a third of that was unsold cooked food; the rest was what students left on their plates.\nFah: That's a lot. Students have complained that portions got smaller this term. Is that connected?\nMr Hughes: (14) ______ Vendors can now offer half portions at a lower price. Full portions are the same size as before. But I take the point — we didn't communicate the change very well.\nFah: What happens to the waste now?\nMr Hughes: Unsold food that's still safe goes to a local charity kitchen every evening. Plate waste goes to the composting unit behind the Faculty of Agriculture, and the compost is used in the university gardens.\nFah: Some people say composting is just a way of making waste look green. (15) ______\nMr Hughes: There's some truth in that. Composting is better than landfill, but the best food waste is the waste we never create. That's why the half-portion option matters more, in my view, than the composting.\nFah: Has the scheme actually reduced waste?\nMr Hughes: It's early days. Plate waste is down about fifteen per cent, but unsold food has barely changed. (16) ______\nFah: So what's next?\nMr Hughes: We're trialling an app that lets students order before noon, so vendors know how much to cook. If it works in two canteens, we'll roll it out everywhere next year.","stem":"About how much unsold cooked food was thrown away each day before the scheme had any effect?","choices":["About 270 kilograms","About 400 kilograms","About 130 kilograms","About 60 kilograms"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Total waste was about 400 kg a day and 'about a third' was unsold cooked food: 400 ÷ 3 ≈ 130 kilograms. 270 kg is the plate waste.","th":"ขยะอาหารรวมราว 400 กิโลกรัมต่อวัน และ 'about a third' เป็นอาหารที่ขายไม่หมด: 400 ÷ 3 ≈ 130 กิโลกรัม ส่วน 270 กิโลกรัมคือเศษอาหารบนจาน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-ads","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Advertisement","th":"โฆษณา"},"passage":"DATALEAP | 12-Week Online Data Analytics Bootcamp\nNo coding experience? No problem.\n\n• Live online classes: Tue & Thu, 7–9 p.m., plus Saturday workshops, 10 a.m.–1 p.m.\n• Learn Python, SQL and data visualisation through real business projects\n• One-to-one mentoring with industry analysts every two weeks\n• Certificate awarded to students who attend at least 80% of classes AND complete all 4 projects\n\nFees: 24,000 baht (pay in full) or 3 × 8,900 baht (instalments)\nEarly bird: 15% off full payment for enrolments before 1 July\nMoney-back guarantee: full refund if you withdraw within the first 14 days. No refunds after that.\n\nNext intake starts 15 July. Places limited to 40.","stem":"Somchai enrols on 20 June and pays the whole fee at once. How much does he pay?","choices":["24,000 baht","26,700 baht","21,600 baht","20,400 baht"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Enrolling before 1 July and paying in full gives 15% off: 24,000 × 0.85 = 20,400 baht.","th":"สมัครก่อน 1 กรกฎาคมและจ่ายเต็มจำนวนได้ส่วนลด 15%: 24,000 × 0.85 = 20,400 บาท"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-ads","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Advertisement","th":"โฆษณา"},"passage":"VOLUNTEERS NEEDED: SUMMER ENGLISH CAMP 2026\nNan Province | 6–17 April (12 days)\n\nHelp 60 primary school pupils from mountain villages build confidence in spoken English through games, songs and drama.\n\nWe provide: accommodation in the school guest house, three meals a day, return bus tickets from Bangkok and a training day on 4 April.\nYou provide: enthusiasm, patience and at least B2-level English (shown by an IELTS, TOEIC or other CEFR-linked certificate, or by an interview with our coordinator).\n\nTeaching experience is NOT required — full lesson plans are supplied.\n\nPlease note: there is no mobile signal at the school. Wi-Fi is available at the village office, 2 km away.\nApplications close 1 March. All volunteers must attend the training day.","stem":"Which applicant is NOT eligible to volunteer?","choices":["A Year 3 student with a B2 certificate who has never visited Nan","A university student with an IELTS certificate who is free only from 6 to 17 April","A retired engineer with no teaching experience who passes the interview","A bank employee who can take leave from 3 to 18 April"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"All volunteers 'must attend the training day' on 4 April, so someone free only from 6 to 17 April is not eligible. Teaching experience is not required, and the interview is an accepted way to show English level.","th":"อาสาสมัครทุกคน 'must attend the training day' ในวันที่ 4 เมษายน ผู้ที่ว่างเฉพาะ 6–17 เมษายนจึงไม่มีสิทธิ์ ส่วนประสบการณ์สอนไม่จำเป็น และการสัมภาษณ์ใช้แสดงระดับภาษาได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-review","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Vocabulary in context","th":"การเดาความหมายคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Product / service review","th":"บทวิจารณ์สินค้าและบริการ"},"passage":"Lingora Plus — is the premium plan worth it? ★★★☆☆ reviewed by Thanawat S.\n\nI've been using the free version of Lingora for about a year to keep up my Japanese, and last month I finally gave in and paid for Lingora Plus (1,290 baht a year). Here's what changed — and what didn't.\n\nThe headline feature is the AI conversation partner, and it is genuinely clever. It adapts to your level, corrects mistakes gently and lets you practise situations like ordering food or asking for directions. My only complaint is that it is a little too patient: in a month of chatting, it has never once failed to understand me, which is more than I can say for real people in Osaka.\n\nRemoving adverts makes the app feel calmer, and offline lessons are handy on the BTS. But the much-advertised \"personalised review\" feature is a disappointment. It simply repeats the words I got wrong most recently, which the free version already did, just less attractively.\n\nThe bigger problem is that Plus doesn't fix Lingora's weaknesses. Grammar explanations are still thin — a single sentence where a short paragraph is needed — and there is still almost no listening practice at natural speed. If you're aiming for an exam such as the JLPT, you'll need other materials anyway.\n\nSo, is it worth it? If you enjoy the app and want to speak more, the conversation partner alone justifies the price. If you were hoping Plus would turn Lingora into a complete course, save your money.","stem":"The phrase 'finally gave in' suggests that the reviewer","choices":["had resisted paying for some time before deciding to","paid immediately when he first saw the offer","regretted paying as soon as he had done it","was forced to pay because the free version stopped working"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Gave in' means stopped resisting, and 'finally' after a year of the free version shows he had resisted paying for some time before deciding to.","th":"'Gave in' แปลว่ายอมหลังจากต้านทานมานาน และคำว่า 'finally' หลังใช้เวอร์ชันฟรีมาหนึ่งปี แสดงว่าเขาลังเลอยู่นานก่อนตัดสินใจจ่าย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-review","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Vocabulary in context","th":"การเดาความหมายคำศัพท์จากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Product / service review","th":"บทวิจารณ์สินค้าและบริการ"},"passage":"Lingora Plus — is the premium plan worth it? ★★★☆☆ reviewed by Thanawat S.\n\nI've been using the free version of Lingora for about a year to keep up my Japanese, and last month I finally gave in and paid for Lingora Plus (1,290 baht a year). Here's what changed — and what didn't.\n\nThe headline feature is the AI conversation partner, and it is genuinely clever. It adapts to your level, corrects mistakes gently and lets you practise situations like ordering food or asking for directions. My only complaint is that it is a little too patient: in a month of chatting, it has never once failed to understand me, which is more than I can say for real people in Osaka.\n\nRemoving adverts makes the app feel calmer, and offline lessons are handy on the BTS. But the much-advertised \"personalised review\" feature is a disappointment. It simply repeats the words I got wrong most recently, which the free version already did, just less attractively.\n\nThe bigger problem is that Plus doesn't fix Lingora's weaknesses. Grammar explanations are still thin — a single sentence where a short paragraph is needed — and there is still almost no listening practice at natural speed. If you're aiming for an exam such as the JLPT, you'll need other materials anyway.\n\nSo, is it worth it? If you enjoy the app and want to speak more, the conversation partner alone justifies the price. If you were hoping Plus would turn Lingora into a complete course, save your money.","stem":"In paragraph 4, 'thin' is closest in meaning to","choices":["easy to understand","printed in small type","too difficult for beginners","lacking in detail"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The writer explains 'a single sentence where a short paragraph is needed', so 'thin' means lacking in detail.","th":"ผู้เขียนอธิบายว่า 'a single sentence where a short paragraph is needed' คำว่า 'thin' จึงหมายถึงมีรายละเอียดไม่เพียงพอ (lacking in detail)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-news","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"News report","th":"ข่าว"},"passage":"University sets rules for AI in coursework\n\nBANGKOK — A leading Thai university will require students to declare any use of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in their coursework from next semester, under a policy approved by its academic senate on Wednesday.\n\nThe policy stops short of a ban. Instead, each course will be placed by its lecturer in one of three categories: courses in which AI tools are not permitted, courses in which they may be used for limited tasks such as brainstorming or checking grammar, and courses in which they may be used freely as long as their use is declared. Students who fail to declare their use of AI will be treated as having committed academic misconduct.\n\n\"Banning these tools outright would be both unenforceable and short-sighted,\" said Associate Professor Rattana Phongsri, the vice-president for academic affairs. \"Our graduates will use AI in their careers. The question is not whether they use it, but whether they use it honestly and well.\"\n\nThe university will also change how some work is assessed. Take-home essays in first-year courses will be partly replaced by in-class writing and short oral examinations, in which students explain and defend the work they have submitted.\n\nReaction among students has been broadly positive, though some have raised concerns about consistency. \"If one lecturer allows AI and another doesn't, for very similar assignments, that's confusing,\" said Pakorn, a third-year law student. \"We need clear examples, not just categories.\"\n\nSome lecturers, meanwhile, question whether declarations will be truthful. Detection software, they note, is unreliable and has wrongly flagged work by students writing in a second language. The university says it will not use such software as the sole evidence of misconduct.\n\nA review of the policy is scheduled for the end of the academic year.","stem":"What is the main topic of the report?","choices":["A university's decision to ban AI tools completely","Students' complaints about unreliable detection software","A university's new rules allowing AI in coursework under certain conditions","A plan to replace all essays with oral examinations"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The report explains a policy that 'stops short of a ban' and sets categories and declaration rules — new rules allowing AI in coursework under certain conditions. Detection software and oral exams are supporting details.","th":"ข่าวอธิบายนโยบายที่ 'stops short of a ban' โดยแบ่งประเภทรายวิชาและกำหนดให้แจ้งการใช้ AI จึงเป็นกฎใหม่ที่อนุญาตให้ใช้ AI ภายใต้เงื่อนไข ส่วนซอฟต์แวร์ตรวจจับและการสอบปากเปล่าเป็นรายละเอียดประกอบ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-news","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมานจากข้อมูลในเรื่อง"},"topic":{"en":"News report","th":"ข่าว"},"passage":"University sets rules for AI in coursework\n\nBANGKOK — A leading Thai university will require students to declare any use of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in their coursework from next semester, under a policy approved by its academic senate on Wednesday.\n\nThe policy stops short of a ban. Instead, each course will be placed by its lecturer in one of three categories: courses in which AI tools are not permitted, courses in which they may be used for limited tasks such as brainstorming or checking grammar, and courses in which they may be used freely as long as their use is declared. Students who fail to declare their use of AI will be treated as having committed academic misconduct.\n\n\"Banning these tools outright would be both unenforceable and short-sighted,\" said Associate Professor Rattana Phongsri, the vice-president for academic affairs. \"Our graduates will use AI in their careers. The question is not whether they use it, but whether they use it honestly and well.\"\n\nThe university will also change how some work is assessed. Take-home essays in first-year courses will be partly replaced by in-class writing and short oral examinations, in which students explain and defend the work they have submitted.\n\nReaction among students has been broadly positive, though some have raised concerns about consistency. \"If one lecturer allows AI and another doesn't, for very similar assignments, that's confusing,\" said Pakorn, a third-year law student. \"We need clear examples, not just categories.\"\n\nSome lecturers, meanwhile, question whether declarations will be truthful. Detection software, they note, is unreliable and has wrongly flagged work by students writing in a second language. The university says it will not use such software as the sole evidence of misconduct.\n\nA review of the policy is scheduled for the end of the academic year.","stem":"Why are short oral examinations most likely being introduced?","choices":["To check that students understand the work they hand in","To replace all written assignments in every year","To test students' spoken English","To reduce the amount of marking lecturers do"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"In the oral exams students 'explain and defend the work they have submitted', which checks that they understand the work they hand in — important when AI could have written it. Only some first-year essays are affected.","th":"ในการสอบปากเปล่า นักศึกษาต้อง 'explain and defend the work they have submitted' จึงเป็นการตรวจว่าเข้าใจงานที่ส่งจริง ซึ่งสำคัญเมื่อ AI อาจเป็นผู้เขียน และมีผลเพียงบางเรียงความของปีหนึ่ง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-visual","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Interpreting data in a table","th":"การตีความข้อมูลจากตาราง"},"topic":{"en":"Table with caption","th":"ตารางพร้อมคำอธิบาย"},"passage":"The table shows the share of electricity produced from each source in Country X. Total electricity generation rose from 180 terawatt-hours (TWh) in 2014 to 230 TWh in 2024.","stem":"Which source's share grew the most between 2019 and 2024?","choices":["Solar","Hydropower","Wind","Natural gas"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Solar rose from 7% to 17% (+10 points), more than wind (+5). Natural gas and hydropower fell.","th":"พลังงานแสงอาทิตย์ (Solar) เพิ่มจาก 7% เป็น 17% (+10) มากกว่าพลังงานลม (+5) ส่วนก๊าซธรรมชาติและพลังน้ำลดลง"},"table":{"title":"Electricity generation by source in Country X (% of total)","rows":[["Source","2014","2019","2024"],["Coal","42","35","24"],["Natural gas","38","40","38"],["Hydropower","9","9","8"],["Solar","2","7","17"],["Wind","1","4","9"],["Other","8","5","4"]]}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-visual","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Table with caption","th":"ตารางพร้อมคำอธิบาย"},"passage":"The table compares four plans from one mobile operator. *On the Unlimited plan, speed is reduced after 80 GB in a month. Cancelling a 12-month contract early costs a fee of 1,500 baht.","stem":"A university student uses about 20 GB of data and 150 minutes of calls a month and does not want to sign a contract. Which plan is the best choice?","choices":["Basic 10","Flex 30","Student 25","Unlimited"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Basic 10 gives too little data, and Student 25 and Unlimited both need a 12-month contract. Only Flex 30 (30 GB, 300 min, no contract) meets all the needs.","th":"Basic 10 ให้ดาต้าไม่พอ ส่วน Student 25 และ Unlimited ต้องทำสัญญา 12 เดือน มีเพียง Flex 30 (30 GB, 300 นาที, ไม่มีสัญญา) ที่ตรงทุกความต้องการ"},"table":{"title":"Mobile phone plans offered by one operator","rows":[["Plan","Price per month (baht)","Data","Calls","Contract"],["Basic 10","299","10 GB","100 min","None"],["Flex 30","449","30 GB","300 min","None"],["Student 25","349","25 GB","200 min","12 months (student ID needed)"],["Unlimited","699","Unlimited*","Unlimited","12 months"]]}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-article","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Article: attention and multitasking","th":"บทความ: สมาธิและการทำหลายอย่างพร้อมกัน"},"passage":"The myth of multitasking\n\nWatch a university student at work and you are likely to see several things happening at once: a lecture recording playing at double speed, a group chat flashing in the corner of the screen, a phone face-up on the desk. Many young people describe themselves as skilled multitaskers, and in a world of constant notifications it can seem like a necessary skill. The research, however, tells a less flattering story.\n\nStrictly speaking, the human brain cannot give full attention to two demanding tasks at the same time. What feels like multitasking is usually rapid switching: attention moves from the essay to the message and back again. Each switch carries a cost. The brain has to drop the \"rules\" of one task and load those of the next, and this takes time — often only a fraction of a second, but enough to add up over an evening. More seriously, after an interruption people frequently take several minutes to return to the depth of concentration they had before.\n\nSwitching also seems to affect the quality of what we produce. In laboratory studies, participants who are interrupted while writing tend to make more errors and produce work that is judged less thoughtful than that of participants who work without interruption, even when the total time spent is the same. Ironically, those who multitask most often are not necessarily better at it. Some studies have found that heavy media multitaskers perform worse on tests of filtering out irrelevant information, although researchers disagree about whether multitasking causes this weakness or whether people who are easily distracted are simply drawn to multitasking.\n\nThere are exceptions. When one of the tasks is automatic — walking while talking, or folding laundry while listening to a podcast — doing both at once causes little difficulty, because the automatic task makes few demands on conscious attention. Problems arise when both tasks require thought, or when the \"easy\" task turns out to need more attention than expected, as drivers who use their phones discover.\n\nIf multitasking is so inefficient, why is it so tempting? Part of the answer is that switching is rewarding in the short term. A new message offers a small, immediate reward, while an essay offers only distant and uncertain ones. Every check of the phone feels like activity, even when little has been accomplished. People also tend to overestimate their own ability: in surveys, those who multitask most are often the most confident that it does them no harm.\n\nThe solution is not to retreat from technology but to manage it deliberately. Many students find it helpful to work in focused blocks of 25 to 50 minutes with notifications switched off, followed by short breaks in which they can check messages freely. Others simply put their phones in another room. Such strategies may feel restrictive at first, but they protect what is, for most students, the scarcest resource of all: not time, but undivided attention.","stem":"Which statement best summarises the article?","choices":["Young people are better at multitasking than older generations.","What we call multitasking is mostly switching, which costs time and lowers the quality of work.","Technology should be banned from places where students study.","Multitasking is harmless as long as one of the tasks is easy."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The article argues that multitasking is really 'rapid switching' with costs in time and quality, and recommends managing technology — not banning it. Exceptions apply only to automatic tasks.","th":"บทความเสนอว่าการทำหลายอย่างพร้อมกันคือ 'rapid switching' ซึ่งเสียเวลาและลดคุณภาพงาน และแนะนำให้จัดการเทคโนโลยี ไม่ใช่ห้ามใช้ ข้อยกเว้นมีเฉพาะงานที่ทำได้อัตโนมัติ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-article","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Understanding text organisation","th":"การเข้าใจโครงสร้างของบทความ"},"topic":{"en":"Article: citizen science","th":"บทความ: วิทยาศาสตร์ภาคพลเมือง"},"passage":"Science without a laboratory\n\nEvery December, thousands of volunteers in dozens of countries spend a day counting birds. They are not professional scientists; many are retired teachers, office workers or schoolchildren with a pair of binoculars and a notebook. Yet the data they collect, gathered over more than a century in some places, has become one of the most valuable records of how bird populations respond to changes in climate and land use. Projects like this are known as citizen science: research in which members of the public help to collect or analyse data.\n\nThe idea is not new — amateur naturalists and astronomers made important contributions long before science became a profession — but the internet and smartphones have transformed its scale. Today, volunteers classify images of distant galaxies, transcribe handwritten weather records from old ships' logbooks, and photograph insects in their gardens for apps that identify species automatically. Some projects attract hundreds of thousands of participants.\n\nFor scientists, the attractions are obvious. Many research questions require more observations than any team could gather on its own, especially when the data must be collected across large areas or over long periods. Volunteers can be almost everywhere at once, at very little cost. In some cases, humans still outperform computers: people are remarkably good at spotting unusual patterns, and several unexpected discoveries have been made by volunteers who noticed something that automated systems had ignored.\n\nCritics have raised two main concerns. The first is data quality. Volunteers may misidentify species, record observations carelessly, or report only what they find interesting, so that common species are under-recorded. Researchers have responded by building checks into their projects: asking several volunteers to classify the same image, for example, or using statistical methods to correct for the fact that observers visit some places far more than others. Studies comparing volunteer and professional data generally find that, with such checks in place, the differences are smaller than feared.\n\nThe second concern is more ethical than technical. If volunteers do much of the work, should they share in the credit? Some projects now list contributors in published papers or name discoveries after the people who made them. Others argue that the relationship should run in both directions, with communities helping to decide which questions are worth asking rather than merely supplying data for questions chosen by professionals. This matters particularly when research concerns local issues such as water pollution, where residents may have knowledge that outsiders lack.\n\nCitizen science will not replace professional research, and it is not suitable for every question; few volunteers can operate a particle accelerator. But it has already changed what is possible in fields from ecology to astronomy. Perhaps just as importantly, it gives thousands of people direct experience of how science actually works — slowly, collectively and with careful attention to error — at a time when public trust in expertise can no longer be taken for granted.","stem":"What is the function of the first paragraph?","choices":["To argue that birds are threatened by climate change","To compare amateur and professional bird counts","To criticise volunteers for lacking scientific training","To illustrate citizen science with a concrete example before defining it"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The paragraph describes the annual bird count and only then gives the term: 'Projects like this are known as citizen science' — an example first, then the definition.","th":"ย่อหน้าแรกเล่าเรื่องการนับนกประจำปีก่อน แล้วจึงให้นิยาม 'Projects like this are known as citizen science' เป็นการยกตัวอย่างที่เป็นรูปธรรมก่อนให้คำนิยาม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"text-completion","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Register in written texts","th":"การใช้ระดับภาษาในงานเขียน"},"topic":{"en":"Cover letter for an internship","th":"จดหมายสมัครฝึกงาน"},"passage":"Read the cover letter and choose the best option for each gap.\n\nDear Ms Wilson,\n\nI am writing to apply for the summer internship in your Communications Department, (1) ______. I am currently in the third year of a degree in Communication Arts, (2) ______.\n\nLast year I worked as a volunteer editor for our faculty's online magazine. (3) ______ This experience taught me how to work to tight deadlines and adapt my writing for different audiences.\n\nI am particularly drawn to your organisation because of its public health campaigns, (4) ______. I would welcome the opportunity to contribute to this work while developing my skills.\n\n(5) ______ Thank you for your time and consideration.\n\nYours sincerely,\nSupitcha Wongsa","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (1)?","choices":["which I am sure I will get","which I saw advertised on your website","although I don't know much about it","because I need some extra money this summer"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A cover letter normally says where the position was advertised: 'which I saw advertised on your website'. The other phrases sound overconfident, too personal or weaken the application.","th":"จดหมายสมัครงานมักบอกว่าเห็นประกาศจากที่ใด: 'which I saw advertised on your website' ส่วนวลีอื่นฟังดูมั่นใจเกินไป เป็นเรื่องส่วนตัว หรือทำให้ใบสมัครดูอ่อน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"text-completion","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Coherence: choosing content that fits the meaning","th":"การเลือกเนื้อความให้สอดคล้องกับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Expository text: the placebo effect","th":"ข้อความเชิงอธิบาย: ปรากฏการณ์ยาหลอก"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each gap.\n\nA placebo is a treatment that contains no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill. (6) ______, patients who receive placebos often report real improvements, particularly in symptoms such as pain and nausea. This \"placebo effect\" is not simply imagination. (7) ______ that expecting relief can trigger the release of the body's own pain-reducing chemicals. The effect also depends on context: (8) ______. Because the placebo effect is so powerful, new medicines are normally tested against a placebo rather than against no treatment at all. (9) ______ Only if patients who receive the real drug improve significantly more than those who receive the placebo can researchers conclude that the drug itself works. (10) ______","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (8)?","choices":["the effect is exactly the same in every situation","placebos work only on animals","larger pills, injections and confident doctors tend to produce stronger effects","patients must always be told they are taking a placebo"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Depends on context' must be followed by examples of context changing the effect: 'larger pills, injections and confident doctors tend to produce stronger effects'. 'Exactly the same in every situation' contradicts 'depends on context'.","th":"'Depends on context' ต้องตามด้วยตัวอย่างที่บริบทเปลี่ยนผลได้: 'larger pills, injections and confident doctors tend to produce stronger effects' ส่วน 'exactly the same in every situation' ขัดกับคำว่าขึ้นกับบริบท"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"paragraph","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Organising sentences into a logical paragraph","th":"การเรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้าอย่างมีลำดับ"},"topic":{"en":"Paragraph organization: process","th":"เรียงประโยค: ขั้นตอน"},"passage":"[1] These reviewers assess the methods and conclusions and recommend whether the paper should be accepted, revised or rejected.\n[2] The authors first submit their manuscript to the journal's editor.\n[3] In most cases, the authors are asked to make changes before the paper can finally appear in print.\n[4] Before a scientific study is published in a journal, it usually goes through a process called peer review.\n[5] If the editor considers it suitable, he or she sends it to two or three independent experts in the field.","stem":"The sentences above are in jumbled order. Which order makes the most logical paragraph?","choices":["[4] – [5] – [2] – [1] – [3]","[2] – [4] – [5] – [1] – [3]","[4] – [2] – [5] – [1] – [3]","[4] – [2] – [1] – [5] – [3]"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"[4] – [2] – [5] – [1] – [3]: the sentence naming 'peer review' introduces the topic; 'first submit' is step one; 'If the editor considers it suitable' follows submission; 'These reviewers' must come after the experts are mentioned; asking for changes is the final stage.","th":"[4] – [2] – [5] – [1] – [3]: ประโยคที่กล่าวถึง 'peer review' เป็นบทนำ 'first submit' คือขั้นแรก 'If the editor considers it suitable' ตามหลังการส่ง 'These reviewers' ต้องมาหลังการกล่าวถึงผู้เชี่ยวชาญ และการแก้ไขเป็นขั้นสุดท้าย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"paragraph","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Organising sentences into a logical paragraph","th":"การเรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้าอย่างมีลำดับ"},"topic":{"en":"Paragraph organization: problem and solution","th":"เรียงประโยค: ปัญหาและทางแก้"},"passage":"[1] Others provide small booths that staff can book for calls or focused work.\n[2] Some studies have even found that people in such offices talk face to face less, not more, and send more emails instead.\n[3] Open-plan offices were originally designed to encourage communication between colleagues.\n[4] To address this, a number of companies have introduced 'quiet zones' where phone calls and conversations are not allowed.\n[5] In practice, many employees find them noisy and distracting.","stem":"The sentences above are in jumbled order. Which order makes the most logical paragraph?","choices":["[3] – [5] – [2] – [1] – [4]","[3] – [5] – [2] – [4] – [1]","[3] – [2] – [5] – [4] – [1]","[5] – [3] – [2] – [4] – [1]"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"[3] – [5] – [2] – [4] – [1]: the original purpose comes first; 'In practice' introduces the problem; 'even' adds a stronger point about the problem; 'To address this' introduces a solution; 'Others' must follow 'a number of companies'.","th":"[3] – [5] – [2] – [4] – [1]: เริ่มด้วยจุดประสงค์เดิม 'In practice' นำปัญหา 'even' เพิ่มประเด็นที่หนักกว่า 'To address this' นำทางแก้ และ 'Others' ต้องตามหลัง 'a number of companies'"}}]}},{"exam":"alevel","set":3,"title":{"en":"A-Level English (TCAS) — Practice paper 3","th":"A-Level ภาษาอังกฤษ (TCAS) — ชุดที่ 3"},"note":{"en":"80 questions in 90 minutes following the official A-Level English blueprint: Listening & Speaking 20 (conversations), Reading 40 (ads, review, news, visuals, articles) and Writing 20 (text completion, paragraph organization). DeepDistrict practice — not an official or past paper.","th":"80 ข้อ 90 นาที ตามโครงสร้างข้อสอบ A-Level ภาษาอังกฤษ: Listening & Speaking 20 ข้อ (บทสนทนา) Reading 40 ข้อ (โฆษณา บทวิจารณ์ ข่าว ภาพประกอบ บทความ) Writing 20 ข้อ (เติมข้อความ เรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้า) — แบบฝึกของ DeepDistrict ไม่ใช่ข้อสอบจริงหรือข้อสอบเก่า"},"minutes":90,"kinds":{"m1":80},"subjects":{"eng":80},"free":false,"file":"alevel-3","sample":{"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-short","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing language suited to register and politeness","th":"การเลือกภาษาให้เหมาะกับระดับภาษาและความสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Campus conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในมหาวิทยาลัย"},"passage":"Read the conversation between a student and a lecturer.\n\nJane: Dr Prasert, sorry to bother you — do you have a moment?\nDr Prasert: Of course, Jane. What can I do for you?\nJane: I'm applying for a summer research programme in Singapore, and I was wondering (1) ______\nDr Prasert: I'd be glad to help, though I should be frank with you. You took my introductory course along with about two hundred other students, so I could really only comment on your exam results.\nJane: Oh, I see. (2) ______\nDr Prasert: Not at all — it's a perfectly reasonable request. But the selection committee will be looking for evidence of how you work on a project. Didn't you do your independent study with Dr Malee?\nJane: Yes, last semester. (3) ______\nDr Prasert: Then she's the obvious choice. If she's willing, I could add a short note supporting your application as well.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (1)?","choices":["whether you remembered who I was.","whether you might be willing to write me a letter of recommendation.","if you could tell me how to get to Singapore.","why you haven't sent my letter of recommendation yet."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Dr Prasert's reply ('I'd be glad to help... I could really only comment on your exam results') shows she has asked for a reference politely: 'whether you might be willing to write me a letter of recommendation.' Asking why it has not been sent assumes he already agreed.","th":"คำตอบของ ดร.ประเสริฐ ('I'd be glad to help... I could really only comment on your exam results') แสดงว่าเจนขอจดหมายรับรองอย่างสุภาพ: 'whether you might be willing to write me a letter of recommendation.' ส่วนการถามว่าทำไมยังไม่ส่งเป็นการถือว่าอาจารย์ตกลงแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-short","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Choosing language suited to register and politeness","th":"การเลือกภาษาให้เหมาะกับระดับภาษาและความสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Workplace conversation","th":"บทสนทนาในที่ทำงาน"},"passage":"Read the conversation from a meeting at a software company.\n\nSomsak: So, are we all agreed that we launch the app on the 1st?\nNina: I'm not sure I'd go that far. (5) ______ The payment feature still failed about one in fifty test transactions last week.\nSomsak: One in fifty isn't bad, surely?\nNina: For a game, maybe. (6) ______ If customers lose money, we won't get a second chance.\nSomsak: Fair point. But marketing have already booked the advertising for the 1st, and moving it would cost us.\nNina: (7) ______ Could we launch on the 1st without the payment feature and add it in an update once it's stable?\nSomsak: That might work. Let me run it past marketing and get back to you by Friday.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (7)?","choices":["Then marketing will have to pay for their own mistake.","In that case, let's cancel the whole project.","Frankly, that's not my concern at all.","I see where you're coming from, so here's a compromise."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Nina acknowledges Somsak's point about the advertising and then proposes launching without the payment feature, so 'I see where you're coming from, so here's a compromise.' fits. The other responses are confrontational or extreme.","th":"นีน่ารับฟังเหตุผลเรื่องโฆษณาของสมศักดิ์ แล้วเสนอเปิดตัวโดยยังไม่มีระบบชำระเงิน จึงต้องเป็น 'I see where you're coming from, so here's a compromise.' ตัวเลือกอื่นเป็นการเผชิญหน้าหรือสุดโต่งเกินไป"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-long","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Cohesion: linking ideas logically","th":"การเชื่อมโยงความคิดอย่างมีเหตุผล"},"topic":{"en":"Seminar discussion","th":"การอภิปรายในชั้นสัมมนา"},"passage":"Read the seminar discussion between a tutor, Dr Lim, and two students, Anan and Sophie.\n\nDr Lim: Right, let's start. The article argued that universities should replace letter grades with written feedback. Anan, you were sceptical?\nAnan: I was. I can see the appeal, but grades serve a purpose outside the university. Employers and scholarship committees need some way of comparing hundreds of applicants. (13) ______\nSophie: But that's exactly the article's point, isn't it? Grades are convenient for employers, not necessarily useful for learning. The study it cites found that students who got only comments improved more on the next assignment than students who got comments plus a grade.\nAnan: (14) ______ That was one study, at one school, with fourteen-year-olds. I'm not sure it tells us much about university students.\nDr Lim: That's a fair methodological point. Sophie, how would you respond?\nSophie: I'd say the mechanism is likely to be similar. When students see a grade, they look at the number and ignore the comments. (15) ______\nAnan: Possibly. But getting rid of grades entirely seems like throwing the baby out with the bathwater. Why not keep grades but release them a week after the feedback, so that students have to read the comments first?\nDr Lim: Interesting — that has actually been tried in a few places. Sophie?\nSophie: (16) ______ It keeps the comparison that employers want while giving the feedback a chance to be read. I'd still want evidence that it works, though.\nDr Lim: Good. Notice how you've both moved from opposing positions to something more nuanced. For next week, I'd like you to find any evidence on delayed grading and assess how strong it is.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (13)?","choices":["Feedback is always more useful than grades.","Employers never look at grades anyway.","That's why I think grades should be abolished.","Without grades, they'd probably just rely more on which university you went to."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Anan is defending grades as a tool for comparison, so he warns of a worse alternative: 'Without grades, they'd probably just rely more on which university you went to.' The other sentences support the article's position, which he is arguing against.","th":"อนันต์กำลังปกป้องเกรดว่าใช้เปรียบเทียบผู้สมัครได้ จึงเตือนถึงทางเลือกที่แย่กว่า: 'Without grades, they'd probably just rely more on which university you went to.' ประโยคอื่นสนับสนุนบทความซึ่งเขากำลังโต้แย้ง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation-long","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Finding specific information","th":"การหาข้อมูลเฉพาะ"},"topic":{"en":"Seminar discussion","th":"การอภิปรายในชั้นสัมมนา"},"passage":"Read the seminar discussion between a tutor, Dr Lim, and two students, Anan and Sophie.\n\nDr Lim: Right, let's start. The article argued that universities should replace letter grades with written feedback. Anan, you were sceptical?\nAnan: I was. I can see the appeal, but grades serve a purpose outside the university. Employers and scholarship committees need some way of comparing hundreds of applicants. (13) ______\nSophie: But that's exactly the article's point, isn't it? Grades are convenient for employers, not necessarily useful for learning. The study it cites found that students who got only comments improved more on the next assignment than students who got comments plus a grade.\nAnan: (14) ______ That was one study, at one school, with fourteen-year-olds. I'm not sure it tells us much about university students.\nDr Lim: That's a fair methodological point. Sophie, how would you respond?\nSophie: I'd say the mechanism is likely to be similar. When students see a grade, they look at the number and ignore the comments. (15) ______\nAnan: Possibly. But getting rid of grades entirely seems like throwing the baby out with the bathwater. Why not keep grades but release them a week after the feedback, so that students have to read the comments first?\nDr Lim: Interesting — that has actually been tried in a few places. Sophie?\nSophie: (16) ______ It keeps the comparison that employers want while giving the feedback a chance to be read. I'd still want evidence that it works, though.\nDr Lim: Good. Notice how you've both moved from opposing positions to something more nuanced. For next week, I'd like you to find any evidence on delayed grading and assess how strong it is.","stem":"What is Anan's first objection to the article?","choices":["Grades help people outside the university to compare applicants.","The article did not cite any research.","Students prefer grades to written comments.","Written feedback takes lecturers too long to write."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"He says 'grades serve a purpose outside the university' because employers and committees 'need some way of comparing hundreds of applicants'.","th":"อนันต์บอกว่า 'grades serve a purpose outside the university' เพราะนายจ้างและกรรมการทุน 'need some way of comparing hundreds of applicants'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-ads","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Comparing and calculating from data","th":"การเปรียบเทียบและคำนวณจากข้อมูล"},"topic":{"en":"Advertisement","th":"โฆษณา"},"passage":"BANGKOK SUSTAINABLE CITIES FORUM 2026\nCity Convention Centre | 14–15 November\n\nREGISTRATION (two days, lunch included)\nEarly rate (until 30 September) ......... 2,800 baht\nStandard rate (from 1 October) .......... 3,500 baht\nStudents* (any time) .................... 1,200 baht\nOne-day pass: 60% of the relevant two-day rate (lunch not included)\n*Full-time students with a valid ID; limited to 300 places\n\nSTUDENT VOLUNTEERS: Work for one day (8 a.m.–5 p.m.) and attend the other day free, with lunch provided on both days. Apply by 15 September. All volunteers must attend a two-hour briefing on 10 November.\n\nGROUP DISCOUNT: 10% off for five or more two-day registrations from the same organisation (cannot be combined with student rates).","stem":"A company registers five employees for both days on 20 September. What is the total cost?","choices":["11,200 baht","12,600 baht","15,750 baht","14,000 baht"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"On 20 September the early rate applies: 5 × 2,800 = 14,000 baht, minus the 10% group discount = 12,600 baht. 15,750 baht wrongly uses the standard rate.","th":"วันที่ 20 กันยายนยังใช้ราคา early: 5 × 2,800 = 14,000 บาท หักส่วนลดกลุ่ม 10% เหลือ 12,600 บาท ส่วน 15,750 บาทคิดจากราคา standard ซึ่งผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-ads","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Applying information to a situation","th":"การนำข้อมูลไปใช้กับสถานการณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Advertisement","th":"โฆษณา"},"passage":"THE MEKONG FUTURES SCHOLARSHIP 2027\n\nFor Thai, Lao and Cambodian nationals beginning a master's degree in environmental science, water management or public policy at a partner university in Australia.\n\nCovers: full tuition fees, return airfare, a monthly living allowance (AUD 2,300) and health insurance.\nDoes not cover: costs for family members, visa application fees.\n\nEligibility:\n• Bachelor's degree with a GPA of at least 3.00 (or equivalent)\n• IELTS 6.5 overall with no band below 6.0, or an equivalent TOEFL score\n• At least two years' relevant work experience after graduation\n• Not currently holding another full scholarship\n\nScholars must commit to returning to their home region for at least two years after completing their degree.\n\nDeadline: 31 January 2027, 5 p.m. Bangkok time. Late or incomplete applications will not be considered.","stem":"Which applicant meets ALL the eligibility requirements?","choices":["A Cambodian policy officer with a GPA of 3.10 and IELTS 6.5 overall but 5.5 in writing","A Vietnamese researcher with a GPA of 3.80, IELTS 8.0 and five years' experience","A Lao engineer with a GPA of 3.20, IELTS 7.0 (lowest band 6.0) and three years at a water utility","A Thai graduate with a GPA of 3.50 and IELTS 7.5 who finished her bachelor's degree last month"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The Lao engineer meets every condition. The Thai graduate lacks two years' work experience, the Cambodian officer has a band below 6.0, and Vietnamese nationals are not eligible.","th":"วิศวกรชาวลาวผ่านทุกเงื่อนไข ส่วนบัณฑิตไทยยังไม่มีประสบการณ์ทำงานสองปี เจ้าหน้าที่ชาวกัมพูชามีคะแนนทักษะหนึ่งต่ำกว่า 6.0 และชาวเวียดนามไม่อยู่ในกลุ่มสัญชาติที่รับสมัคร"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-review","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมานจากข้อมูลในเรื่อง"},"topic":{"en":"Product / service review","th":"บทวิจารณ์สินค้าและบริการ"},"passage":"Saffron & Smoke, Sukhumvit Soi 31 — ★★★☆☆ reviewed by Daniel K.\n\nSaffron & Smoke arrives with the kind of publicity most restaurants can only dream of: a celebrity chef, a six-week waiting list and an interior apparently inspired by a 1920s railway carriage. Expectations, in other words, could hardly be higher — which may be part of the problem.\n\nLet's start with what works. The grilled river prawns, finished over charcoal and brushed with a tamarind glaze, are superb: smoky, sweet and sour in perfect proportion. A green curry made with smoked duck sounds like a gimmick but turns out to be the most thoughtful dish on the menu, the smoke deepening rather than overwhelming the curry. Service is warm and well informed, without the rehearsed speeches that plague many upmarket places.\n\nElsewhere, ambition outruns execution. A \"deconstructed\" som tam arrives as five separate elements on a slate, which looks striking but misses the point of the dish: the pounding together of flavours. The rice, astonishingly for a restaurant charging 480 baht for a bowl of it, was slightly undercooked on both of my visits. And desserts feel like an afterthought; a coconut panna cotta was perfectly pleasant and entirely forgettable.\n\nThen there is the price. Dinner for two with a modest bottle of wine came to just over 7,000 baht. That is not unreasonable for this part of the city, but at this level a meal should be faultless rather than merely interesting.\n\nSaffron & Smoke is not a bad restaurant — at its best it is a very good one. But it is not yet the restaurant its reputation suggests. Go for the prawns and the duck curry; skip the theatre.","stem":"What does the reviewer suggest with the words 'which may be part of the problem'?","choices":["The waiting list prevents most people from visiting.","The railway-carriage interior is uncomfortable.","The celebrity chef rarely cooks in the restaurant.","The restaurant's fame makes its weaknesses more disappointing."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Because 'expectations... could hardly be higher', any faults feel worse — the restaurant's fame makes its weaknesses more disappointing. Nothing is said about the chef's absence or discomfort.","th":"เพราะ 'expectations... could hardly be higher' ข้อบกพร่องใด ๆ จึงดูแย่ลง ชื่อเสียงของร้านทำให้จุดอ่อนน่าผิดหวังยิ่งขึ้น บทวิจารณ์ไม่ได้พูดถึงเชฟที่ไม่อยู่หรือความไม่สะดวกสบาย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-review","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Identifying attitude and tone","th":"การระบุทัศนคติและน้ำเสียง"},"topic":{"en":"Product / service review","th":"บทวิจารณ์สินค้าและบริการ"},"passage":"Saffron & Smoke, Sukhumvit Soi 31 — ★★★☆☆ reviewed by Daniel K.\n\nSaffron & Smoke arrives with the kind of publicity most restaurants can only dream of: a celebrity chef, a six-week waiting list and an interior apparently inspired by a 1920s railway carriage. Expectations, in other words, could hardly be higher — which may be part of the problem.\n\nLet's start with what works. The grilled river prawns, finished over charcoal and brushed with a tamarind glaze, are superb: smoky, sweet and sour in perfect proportion. A green curry made with smoked duck sounds like a gimmick but turns out to be the most thoughtful dish on the menu, the smoke deepening rather than overwhelming the curry. Service is warm and well informed, without the rehearsed speeches that plague many upmarket places.\n\nElsewhere, ambition outruns execution. A \"deconstructed\" som tam arrives as five separate elements on a slate, which looks striking but misses the point of the dish: the pounding together of flavours. The rice, astonishingly for a restaurant charging 480 baht for a bowl of it, was slightly undercooked on both of my visits. And desserts feel like an afterthought; a coconut panna cotta was perfectly pleasant and entirely forgettable.\n\nThen there is the price. Dinner for two with a modest bottle of wine came to just over 7,000 baht. That is not unreasonable for this part of the city, but at this level a meal should be faultless rather than merely interesting.\n\nSaffron & Smoke is not a bad restaurant — at its best it is a very good one. But it is not yet the restaurant its reputation suggests. Go for the prawns and the duck curry; skip the theatre.","stem":"The word 'astonishingly' in paragraph 3 expresses the reviewer's","choices":["approval of the restaurant's prices","surprise that such a basic error occurs at such a high price","admiration for the restaurant's unusual rice","amusement at the size of the bowl"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"It introduces undercooked rice at '480 baht for a bowl', expressing surprise that such a basic error occurs at such a high price.","th":"คำนี้นำเรื่องข้าวที่สุกไม่ทั่วในราคา '480 baht for a bowl' แสดงความประหลาดใจว่าร้านราคาสูงขนาดนี้ยังพลาดเรื่องพื้นฐาน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-news","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"News report","th":"ข่าว"},"passage":"Thai coffee farmers head for the hills as the climate shifts\n\nCHIANG RAI — Coffee farmers in northern Thailand are planting at ever higher altitudes as rising temperatures and erratic rainfall make traditional growing areas less suitable for arabica, the high-quality bean that commands premium prices, according to a report published yesterday.\n\nThe report, by a regional agricultural research institute, found that the average altitude of new arabica plantings in three northern provinces had risen by around 150 metres over the past decade. Farmers interviewed for the study described flowering at unexpected times, more frequent pest outbreaks and beans ripening unevenly — all problems associated with warmer conditions.\n\n\"Arabica is a fussy plant,\" said the report's lead author, Dr Sunee Kittisak. \"It does best in a fairly narrow range of temperatures. When nights stop being cool, the quality of the bean suffers, and so does the price the farmer can get.\"\n\nMoving uphill is not a simple solution. Much of the higher land is forested, and some lies within protected areas. Conservation groups have warned that unchecked expansion could destroy forest that helps to regulate water supplies for communities downstream. \"We understand the pressure farmers are under,\" said a spokesperson for one group. \"But clearing forest to adapt to climate change would only make the underlying problem worse.\"\n\nThe report suggests alternatives. Growing coffee under the shade of taller trees can lower temperatures around the plants by several degrees, while also protecting the soil and providing habitat for birds. Some farmers are experimenting with more heat-tolerant varieties, though these tend to produce beans with a less distinctive flavour.\n\nFor many, the choice is economic as much as environmental. Shade-grown coffee yields less per hectare, at least in the first years, and specialty buyers do not always pay enough extra to make up the difference. Dr Sunee called for government support during the transition. \"If we want farmers to adapt in a way that protects the forest, we have to make sure they can afford to,\" she said.\n\nThe Ministry of Agriculture said it would study the report's recommendations.","stem":"What is the report mainly about?","choices":["A government plan to protect forests in northern Thailand","How a warming climate is pushing coffee farming uphill, and the problems this creates","The success of shade-grown coffee in Chiang Rai","Why arabica coffee is more expensive than other types"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The article reports farmers 'planting at ever higher altitudes' because of climate change and then discusses the risks to forests and possible alternatives.","th":"ข่าวรายงานว่าเกษตรกร 'planting at ever higher altitudes' เพราะสภาพอากาศเปลี่ยน แล้วอภิปรายผลกระทบต่อป่าและทางเลือกอื่น ๆ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-news","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Making inferences","th":"การอนุมานจากข้อมูลในเรื่อง"},"topic":{"en":"News report","th":"ข่าว"},"passage":"Thai coffee farmers head for the hills as the climate shifts\n\nCHIANG RAI — Coffee farmers in northern Thailand are planting at ever higher altitudes as rising temperatures and erratic rainfall make traditional growing areas less suitable for arabica, the high-quality bean that commands premium prices, according to a report published yesterday.\n\nThe report, by a regional agricultural research institute, found that the average altitude of new arabica plantings in three northern provinces had risen by around 150 metres over the past decade. Farmers interviewed for the study described flowering at unexpected times, more frequent pest outbreaks and beans ripening unevenly — all problems associated with warmer conditions.\n\n\"Arabica is a fussy plant,\" said the report's lead author, Dr Sunee Kittisak. \"It does best in a fairly narrow range of temperatures. When nights stop being cool, the quality of the bean suffers, and so does the price the farmer can get.\"\n\nMoving uphill is not a simple solution. Much of the higher land is forested, and some lies within protected areas. Conservation groups have warned that unchecked expansion could destroy forest that helps to regulate water supplies for communities downstream. \"We understand the pressure farmers are under,\" said a spokesperson for one group. \"But clearing forest to adapt to climate change would only make the underlying problem worse.\"\n\nThe report suggests alternatives. Growing coffee under the shade of taller trees can lower temperatures around the plants by several degrees, while also protecting the soil and providing habitat for birds. Some farmers are experimenting with more heat-tolerant varieties, though these tend to produce beans with a less distinctive flavour.\n\nFor many, the choice is economic as much as environmental. Shade-grown coffee yields less per hectare, at least in the first years, and specialty buyers do not always pay enough extra to make up the difference. Dr Sunee called for government support during the transition. \"If we want farmers to adapt in a way that protects the forest, we have to make sure they can afford to,\" she said.\n\nThe Ministry of Agriculture said it would study the report's recommendations.","stem":"Why would clearing forest 'make the underlying problem worse'?","choices":["Losing forest would add to the climate and water problems that are causing the difficulty.","Protected areas charge farmers high fees to use the land.","Coffee grown in former forests has a poor flavour.","Forest land is too steep for coffee to grow well."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The underlying problem is climate change; forests help 'regulate water supplies' and their loss would worsen the climate and water problems causing farmers' difficulties.","th":"ปัญหาพื้นฐานคือการเปลี่ยนแปลงสภาพภูมิอากาศ ป่าช่วย 'regulate water supplies' การสูญเสียป่าจึงซ้ำเติมปัญหาสภาพอากาศและน้ำที่เป็นต้นเหตุ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-visual","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Interpreting data in a table","th":"การตีความข้อมูลจากตาราง"},"topic":{"en":"Table with caption","th":"ตารางพร้อมคำอธิบาย"},"passage":"The table shows a survey of 4,000 graduates of one university (800 from each faculty) six months after graduation. Median salaries are for employed graduates only.","stem":"Which faculty had the highest proportion of graduates who were NOT employed?","choices":["Arts & Humanities","Engineering","Science","Business"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Not employed = further study + seeking work: Arts & Humanities 22 + 20 = 42%, Science 39%, Business 26%, Engineering 22%.","th":"ไม่ได้ทำงาน = เรียนต่อ + กำลังหางาน: Arts & Humanities 22 + 20 = 42% Science 39% Business 26% Engineering 22%"},"table":{"title":"Graduate outcomes six months after graduation (%)","rows":[["Faculty","Employed","Further study","Seeking work","Median monthly salary (baht)"],["Engineering","78","12","10","28,000"],["Health sciences","85","9","6","32,000"],["Business","74","8","18","24,500"],["Arts & Humanities","58","22","20","18,000"],["Science","61","27","12","22,000"]]}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-visual","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Interpreting data in a table","th":"การตีความข้อมูลจากตาราง"},"topic":{"en":"Table with caption","th":"ตารางพร้อมคำอธิบาย"},"passage":"In an experiment, 120 students were randomly divided into four equal groups. Each group spent three hours studying the same biology chapter using one method (the spaced group's three hours were spread over three days) and then took equivalent tests at three points in time.","stem":"Which method produced the highest score on the immediate test?","choices":["Practice testing","Spaced practice testing","Re-reading notes","Highlighting"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"On the immediate test, re-reading notes scored 74, the highest.","th":"ในการสอบทันทีหลังเรียน การอ่านทบทวนโน้ต (Re-reading notes) ได้ 74 คะแนน สูงที่สุด"},"table":{"title":"Average test scores (out of 100) by study method","rows":[["Study method","Immediate test","After 1 week","After 1 month"],["Re-reading notes","74","58","41"],["Highlighting","71","55","39"],["Practice testing","68","66","60"],["Spaced practice testing","66","69","64"]]}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-article","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Article: the illusory truth effect","th":"บทความ: ผลของความจริงลวงจากการทำซ้ำ"},"passage":"Why repetition makes falsehoods believable\n\nIn the 1970s, psychologists asked volunteers to rate how true a series of statements seemed. Some of the statements were accurate, some were false, and some were repeated across several sessions. The finding was striking in its simplicity: statements people had seen before were rated as more likely to be true, regardless of whether they actually were. Researchers named this the illusory truth effect, and in the decades since, it has been reproduced in dozens of experiments.\n\nThe explanation appears to lie in how the brain uses ease as a signal. A statement we have encountered before is processed more fluently — it feels familiar, and familiarity is easily mistaken for accuracy. In everyday life this shortcut usually serves us well, since most of what we hear repeatedly is, in fact, true. The trouble is that the shortcut cannot tell the difference between a claim repeated because it is correct and one repeated because someone wants us to believe it.\n\nPerhaps the most unsettling finding is that knowledge offers less protection than we might hope. In one series of studies, participants who could correctly answer a factual question when asked directly were still more likely to rate a repeated false version of the fact as true. Knowing the right answer, it seems, does not guarantee that we will use that knowledge when a familiar-sounding claim slips past our attention. The effect is weaker for obviously absurd statements, but even for those it does not disappear entirely.\n\nThis has obvious implications in an age of social media, where the same claim may appear in a person's feed dozens of times, shared by different accounts and phrased in slightly different ways. It also creates a dilemma for those trying to correct misinformation. Repeating a myth in order to debunk it risks increasing its familiarity, and some early research suggested that corrections could \"backfire\", leaving people more convinced of the myth than before.\n\nMore recent work has softened that conclusion. Large studies have generally failed to find a consistent backfire effect; corrections usually reduce belief in false claims, at least in the short term. But the way a correction is framed matters. Researchers recommend leading with the accurate information rather than the myth, mentioning the false claim only once and clearly labelling it as false, and explaining why it is wrong rather than simply asserting that it is. An alternative explanation that fills the gap left by the myth tends to be more effective than a bare denial.\n\nThere is also evidence that people can be encouraged to pause. Simply asking participants to consider whether a statement is accurate when they first read it — rather than later — appears to reduce the effect of later repetition. Small changes in platform design, such as prompts that ask users whether they have read an article before sharing it, may work on the same principle.\n\nNone of this makes us helpless. But it does suggest that the feeling of \"I've heard that somewhere\" deserves more suspicion than we usually give it. Familiarity is evidence that a claim has been repeated — not that it is true.","stem":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the article?","choices":["People with good general knowledge cannot be misled by repetition.","Social media companies deliberately repeat false claims to mislead users.","Repeated exposure makes claims feel true, which affects how misinformation spreads and how it should be corrected.","Corrections of false claims usually make people believe them more strongly."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The article explains the illusory truth effect and then discusses its implications for social media and for correcting myths. The backfire idea is said to have been 'softened', and knowledge offers 'less protection than we might hope'.","th":"บทความอธิบายปรากฏการณ์ที่การทำซ้ำทำให้ข้อความดูจริง แล้วอภิปรายผลต่อสื่อสังคมและวิธีแก้ข่าวเท็จ แนวคิด backfire ถูกระบุว่า 'softened' และความรู้ให้การป้องกัน 'less... than we might hope'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading-article","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Finding specific information","th":"การหาข้อมูลเฉพาะ"},"topic":{"en":"Article: endangered languages","th":"บทความ: ภาษาที่ใกล้สูญหาย"},"passage":"When a language falls silent\n\nOf the roughly 7,000 languages spoken in the world today, linguists estimate that around 40 per cent are endangered, many with only a few hundred or a few dozen speakers. Some estimates suggest that, at current rates, half of the world's languages could cease to be spoken by the end of this century. Unlike the extinction of a species, the loss of a language rarely makes headlines. Yet each one represents a distinctive way of describing the world, built up over many generations.\n\nLanguages rarely die because their speakers disappear. Far more often, they are abandoned gradually, as a community shifts to a more widely spoken language that offers access to education, jobs and national life. The process typically takes about three generations. Grandparents speak the heritage language fluently; their children understand it but use the dominant language in most situations; the grandchildren may know only a handful of words. By the time anyone notices, the chain of transmission from parent to child has already been broken — and that chain, rather than the number of speakers, is the most reliable indicator of a language's health.\n\nWhy should this matter to anyone outside the communities concerned? One answer is scientific. Languages vary in ways that challenge assumptions about what human language must be like. Some have no words for left and right, describing positions instead by the points of the compass; others mark in their grammar whether the speaker witnessed an event personally or heard about it from someone else. Every undocumented language that disappears removes evidence that linguists, psychologists and anthropologists might have used to understand the human mind.\n\nLanguages also carry knowledge of a more practical kind. Communities that have lived in one place for centuries often have detailed vocabularies for local plants, animals and weather patterns, and some of this knowledge has proved valuable to scientists. But it would be a mistake to justify languages only by their usefulness to outsiders. For many speakers, a heritage language is bound up with identity, memory and a sense of belonging — values that cannot be measured in terms of information.\n\nReversing language shift is difficult but not impossible. Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in religious and written contexts, was revived as an everyday spoken language in the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries. More modest successes include \"language nests\", in which young children are cared for entirely in the heritage language by older fluent speakers, and programmes that pair learners with elders. Technology helps too, from recorded dictionaries to messaging apps with keyboards for minority scripts.\n\nWhat these efforts have in common is that they depend on the communities themselves. Outsiders can record and describe a language, but they cannot make anyone speak it. Revitalisation succeeds when speakers — especially parents — come to see the language not as a relic of the past but as something worth passing on. Whether that happens depends less on linguists than on the economic and social conditions that led people to abandon the language in the first place.","stem":"According to the article, how do most languages die?","choices":["Their speakers are killed by war or disease.","Their speakers gradually switch to a more dominant language.","Linguists fail to record them in time.","Governments ban them from being taught in schools."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'Languages rarely die because their speakers disappear. Far more often, they are abandoned gradually, as a community shifts to a more widely spoken language.'","th":"บทความบอกว่า 'Languages rarely die because their speakers disappear. Far more often, they are abandoned gradually, as a community shifts to a more widely spoken language' คือผู้พูดค่อย ๆ เปลี่ยนไปใช้ภาษาที่แพร่หลายกว่า"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"text-completion","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Coherence: choosing content that fits the meaning","th":"การเลือกเนื้อความให้สอดคล้องกับบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Formal proposal email","th":"อีเมลเสนอโครงการแบบทางการ"},"passage":"Read the formal email and choose the best option for each gap.\n\nDear Professor Chanida,\n\nOn behalf of the Student Union, I am writing to propose (1) ______ during the final examination period.\n\nAt present, the library closes at 10 p.m. (2) ______ A survey we conducted last month, which received over 1,800 responses, found that 64 per cent of students had been unable to find a quiet place to study in the evenings.\n\nWe recognise that (3) ______. With this in mind, we would suggest a six-week trial in which only the ground floor remains open until 2 a.m., staffed by trained student assistants.\n\n(4) ______, we would be happy to share the full survey results and to help evaluate the trial at its conclusion.\n\n(5) ______\n\nYours sincerely,\nKittipat Rojanaporn\nPresident, Student Union","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (1)?","choices":["that the library close earlier in the evening","that a new library be built immediately","a reduction in the number of examinations","that the library extend its opening hours"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The rest of the email discusses evening study and a trial of later opening, so the proposal is 'that the library extend its opening hours'.","th":"เนื้อหาที่เหลือพูดถึงการอ่านหนังสือตอนค่ำและการทดลองเปิดดึก ข้อเสนอจึงเป็น 'that the library extend its opening hours'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"text-completion","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Cohesion: linking ideas logically","th":"การเชื่อมโยงความคิดอย่างมีเหตุผล"},"topic":{"en":"Academic text: the tragedy of the commons","th":"ข้อความวิชาการ: โศกนาฏกรรมของทรัพยากรส่วนรวม"},"passage":"Read the passage and choose the best option for each gap.\n\nThe \"tragedy of the commons\" describes a situation in which individuals, each acting in their own interest, overuse a shared resource until it is ruined. (6) ______ Each herder benefits fully from adding one more animal, while the cost of overgrazing is shared by everyone. (7) ______, every herder keeps adding animals, and the pasture is eventually destroyed. For decades, the concept was used to argue that shared resources must either be privatised or controlled by the state. (8) ______ The political economist Elinor Ostrom documented numerous communities, from alpine villages in Switzerland to irrigation systems in the Philippines, that had managed common resources successfully for centuries. (9) ______: clearly defined boundaries, rules adapted to local conditions, and ways for members to monitor one another and resolve conflicts cheaply. Ostrom's work suggests that the \"tragedy\" is not inevitable; (10) ______.","stem":"Which choice best completes gap (8)?","choices":["This conclusion, however, was later challenged.","Privatisation, as a result, was abolished.","This conclusion has never been seriously questioned.","Consequently, herders stopped keeping animals altogether."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The following sentences describe Ostrom's evidence against the privatise-or-state argument, so the gap must signal a challenge: 'This conclusion, however, was later challenged.'","th":"ประโยคต่อมาอธิบายหลักฐานของ Ostrom ที่แย้งข้อสรุปว่าต้องแปรรูปหรือให้รัฐควบคุม ช่องว่างจึงต้องบอกว่ามีการโต้แย้ง: 'This conclusion, however, was later challenged.'"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"paragraph","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Organising sentences into a logical paragraph","th":"การเรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้าอย่างมีลำดับ"},"topic":{"en":"Paragraph organization: argument and counter-argument","th":"เรียงประโยค: ข้อโต้แย้งและข้อแย้งกลับ"},"passage":"[1] Yet the benefits are frequently smaller than they appear.\n[2] Whether tourism helps a community therefore depends less on the number of visitors than on who controls the industry.\n[3] Visitors bring money that may otherwise be hard to find, and they create jobs in transport, food and accommodation.\n[4] Meanwhile, the costs, from rising land prices to pressure on water supplies, are borne mainly by residents.\n[5] Tourism is often presented as an ideal way for remote communities to earn an income.\n[6] Much of the money spent by tourists goes to foreign-owned hotels and tour operators rather than to local people.","stem":"The sentences above are in jumbled order. Which order makes the most logical paragraph?","choices":["[5] – [1] – [3] – [6] – [4] – [2]","[5] – [3] – [6] – [1] – [4] – [2]","[5] – [3] – [1] – [4] – [6] – [2]","[5] – [3] – [1] – [6] – [4] – [2]"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"[5] – [3] – [1] – [6] – [4] – [2]: the common view comes first and is supported by the benefits; 'Yet' turns to the counter-argument; the leakage of money explains why benefits are smaller; 'Meanwhile' adds the costs; 'therefore' concludes.","th":"[5] – [3] – [1] – [6] – [4] – [2]: เริ่มด้วยความเชื่อทั่วไปและประโยชน์ที่สนับสนุน 'Yet' เปลี่ยนเป็นข้อโต้แย้ง เงินที่รั่วไหลอธิบายว่าทำไมประโยชน์น้อยกว่าที่คิด 'Meanwhile' เพิ่มเรื่องต้นทุน และ 'therefore' สรุป"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"alevel","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"paragraph","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Organising sentences into a logical paragraph","th":"การเรียงประโยคเป็นย่อหน้าอย่างมีลำดับ"},"topic":{"en":"Paragraph organization: historical development","th":"เรียงประโยค: พัฒนาการทางประวัติศาสตร์"},"passage":"[1] Some historians argue that this, in turn, helped to make possible the rapid spread of new scientific and religious ideas.\n[2] Within a few decades, printing workshops had spread to cities across the continent.\n[3] Before the mid-fifteenth century, books in Europe were copied by hand, a process that could take months for a single volume.\n[4] The resulting fall in the price of books made them available to a far wider readership.\n[5] Johannes Gutenberg's printing press, which used movable metal type, changed this dramatically.","stem":"The sentences above are in jumbled order. Which order makes the most logical paragraph?","choices":["[3] – [2] – [5] – [4] – [1]","[3] – [5] – [4] – [2] – [1]","[3] – [5] – [2] – [4] – [1]","[5] – [3] – [2] – [4] – [1]"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"[3] – [5] – [2] – [4] – [1]: the situation before printing comes first; 'changed this' refers back to hand-copying; 'Within a few decades' shows printing spreading; 'The resulting fall in price' follows from that spread; 'this, in turn' gives the wider consequence.","th":"[3] – [5] – [2] – [4] – [1]: เริ่มจากสภาพก่อนมีการพิมพ์ 'changed this' อ้างถึงการคัดลอกด้วยมือ 'Within a few decades' แสดงการแพร่หลาย 'The resulting fall in price' เป็นผลที่ตามมา และ 'this, in turn' ให้ผลกระทบในวงกว้าง"}}]}}];
